      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>POULTRY FOR PROFIT.</head>
        <p>POULTRY FOR PROFIT.</p>
        <p>THE WEEK'S WORK IN BRIEF.</p>
        <p>Keep grading the chicks. This is a matter which cannot be done too often. .</p>
        <p>Keep the brooder lids and doors open during the warm days. Less heat will now be required by the grow- ing stock. Do not let the young chicks out</p>
        <p>on-til the grass is fairly dry. Examine chicks from each roster-mother for lice. They will usually be found on the head. Beep the 'feeding troughs clean. Grit and clean water must always be in front of.the birds. Do not forget the broody hen. She should be fed once a day. Preferably when the chicks have a full crop. Green food must be supplied in abundance to the backyarders' chicks. It may be chopped up fine and fed raw, or given in the mash. For the latter it is best boiled.</p>
        <p>A large clod of turf will provide the chicks with exercise. Worms, &amp;c., can be attracted to the surface by laying boards on a portion of the run.</p>
        <p>Pay great attention to the cleanliness of the chicken coops. Dry soil is useful to keep the floors clean. Peat moss dust, or chaff, may be used as litter.</p>
        <p>Turn a piece of ground over occasionally, the chicks will greatly enjoy scratching for worms. Keep the broody hen well fed and contented._</p>
        <p>A small mincing machine is invaluable. No vegetable matter of any description should be wasted. Let it be well boiled and pass through the mincer. It may then be added to the mash. ,The water it has bten boiled in may be used tor scalding the mash.</p>
        <p>Odds and ends from the butchers and fish- mongers may be bought cheaply, but see that it is fairly fresh. Do not buy more than can be used up in two or three days.</p>
        <p>- If kibbled corn is used it should always be sieved before being fed. The dust or meal may be given as mash. Don't throw green food about the runs. It will be fouled and so become unfit for food.— " The Poultry World."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>HOUSING PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>THE HOUSING PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>As the hatching season advances, and the chicks begin to develop into big birds, needing a more spacious home than the brooder pro- vides, our thoughts turn seriously to the housing problem. To the breeder, large and small, this is al*ays an important question, and certainly if the best results are to be obtained the birds must be well housed. It is false economy to be stingy in the matter of a little timber and wire netting, and so cramp them for room. This is a most important item, and twp or three inore boards and another half-yard of netting will make a wonderful difference to the size of the house while you are at it, and as one generally looks forward to increasing the number of birds, and not decreasing, if the house is built amply large enough to commence with, a few more birds can be added without overcrowding' the already existing inhabitants.—" The Feathered World." .</p>
        <p>SUGGESTIONS FOR THE PRESERVATION OF EGGS. Eggs for preservation should be treated as soon as possible after they are laid, but not until they have been cooled. It is recognised that an egg twenty-four hours old is superior to and has greater food value than one a week old. Conse- quently, if when placed in the preserving medium the egg has depreciated to this extent, the final result cannot be as satisfactory as under the former conditions. It is, therefore, desirable that preservation should be as near to the point of production as possible. Eggs should pot be treated in a warm place, and where limewater or waterglass is used the preparation should be quite cold before the eggs are placed in the solution. Eggs from hens fed chiefly upon grain, and with full liberty, are likely to keep better than those laid by fowls in confined runs. The general experience has been that infertile eggs keep in good condition longer than those which contain a living germ. Probably this is less apparent when eggs are preserved at a low temperature. When eggs are preserved in waterglass or lime- water the containing vessels shtvitta be stored in a cool place, at a temperature not Tess than 3; degs. Fahr., nor more than 45 degs. A cool, sweet cellar is excellent for this purpose. Ex- posure to a highec, temperature, even for a few hours, will cause deterioration in spite of the preservative. Preserved eggs should be carefully tested by light before they are sold. For this purpose a I well-constructed candling lamp is to be pre- ferred; but a piece of black cardboard, 8 inches square, with, an over hole in the centre rather smaller than an ordinary; egg, can be used. Each egg is placed against the hole, and held between a strong light and the eye, so that the condition of the contents can be observed. All dark eggs or those showing spots or black shadows should be rejected. The best months for preserving are March, April, May and June. It has been found in many cases that summer eggs do not keep ttearly so well as those laid before the hot days. Preserved eggs should be sold under that name, and not as "new laids," "Breakfast," or " fresh " eggs. —"Board of Agriculture Leaflet."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>MORE CHICKENS WANTED.</head>
        <p>MORE CHICKENS WANTED.</p>
        <p>This season has probably established a record as a breeding season, and has been, I think, in regard to providing suitable weather, the worst I ever remember. Chickens are still very few in the country, but, though spring seems still shy in asserting her influences, there is some encouragement and promise of better condi- tions. There is still plenty of time to hatch profitable birds, particularly of the lighter breeds, such as Leghorns, Anconas, &amp;c., Ste., and let, me urge breeders once again to produce as many as their accommodation will permit. So long as one is engaged in the cultivation of pedi- gree bred-f-lay stock there is not the least danger of the market becoming glutted even in war time. This class of fowl is deservedly be- coming popular, and the genuine goods will ex- perience a ready demand for many years to come.—" The Feathered World."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>USE FOR STERILE EGGS.</head>
        <p>USE FOR STERILE EGGS.</p>
        <p>Infertile eggs which have been within the in- cubator or under the hen for six or seven days are quite good enough for cooking purposes. As a matter of fact, they are probably of much better quality than the great majority of so- called cooking eggs retailed in the grocers' shops. Such eggs cannot, of course, be sold as</p>
        <p>new laid. Seven day incubated eggs would be quite unsuitable for boiling or poaching, but for cooking they are all right. I have sold several hundreds of such eggs to bakers for use in the making of cakes, and excellent they have always proved to be. Another use to which the rejected infertile eggs may be put is for.feeding to chickens during the first few days of their lives. Hard boiled eggs, chopped up finely, and mixed with bread crumbs or biscuit meal, form a capital diet for young birds,—Expert, in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Eggs, Poultry, &amp;c.</head>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHICKEN REARING.—Mr. Win. Coombee, of the " Pleck Gate Poultry Farm," winner of the let Prize in the Blackburn and District Lay- ing Competition for four months ending the '4th Febru_ary. 1916, writes:—</p>
        <p>" I have always made a practice of using the best food. which I am convinced is the cheapest in the end. My pen of four Buff Orpington Pullets, which laid 316 EGQS.in the above competition were reared from the shell on ENTWISTLE BROS.' DRY CHICK FOOD AND CHICKEN MEAL, which I consider to be the best and most reliable on the market. The Pen of Birds from which the winners were bred were also fed on this firm's various lines, and I have no hesitation in recommending them to others." You can get it from your Grocer or Corn Dealer in 3flb. and 71b. bags. Ask for it to-day. Sole manu- facturers: ENTWISTLE BROS., River-st., Bolton. Agents: Carter &amp; Son, Ltd., Barlow &amp; Sons, Warrington; T. Gregory, Earlestown.</p>
        <p>E"S' 3/6 dozen; CHICItENS, from 7d. Wyandottes, Leghorn*, Minoicas, Rhode Island Reds. Finest laying ,strains. Invection in- vited. Reduction quantaies.---Gill, Heath-road, Penketh, Warrington.</p>
        <p>EGGS.—White Leghorn, White Wyandotte, Norman Hunt's competition laying strain, 4/- sitting. Buff Orpington, Golding's direct, sit- tiig. Best Pen all winners. 1.01- sitting. Guaran- teed fertile or replaced.—Bennett, Pelham-road, Retford.</p>
        <p>PPURE-BRED White Leghorns, Barron's 282- egg strain, 48. 6d. dozen.—Miss Bromley, Hem. ineton Hall, Derby.</p>
        <p>ROSE Comb Black Leghorn Eggs, 4s. dozen carriage paid; unlimited grass runs.-4. W. Moorhouse, Cononley, Keighley.</p>
        <p>WHITE Wyandot tee, first prize laying V strains; eggs, 6s. per doz.; chicks double ; table crosses, 7s. 6d..—Ellis, Holly-hill, Blackwell, Bromsgrove.</p>
        <p>Ma PULLETS, just started laying, 5/ each, WY 6 for 30/-, or 12 and unrelated male bird .£3. delivered. Our best birds are marked with rings, bearing our name and numbered, so customers can Drove for themselves the quality of our laying strains. Approval with pleasure. EGGS for sit- ting, 3/6 dozen: CHICKS. 8/- dozen. Approval with pleasure. Lisa sent—Brown's Poultry Farm, Woolton, Lancashire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>FEATHER EATING.</head>
        <p>FEATHER EATING.</p>
        <p>This is a common vice in many poultry yards, but if it is treated in its early stages it is not difficult to cure. There are three frequent causes of feather-plucking, namely, idleness, lack of shell-forming material, and the presence of an insect at the root of the feathers. Any bare parts should be rubbed with a mixture of ten parts vaseline and one part catholic acid, re- peating the dressing every day until the fowls are well. Give a little bonemeal or green cut bone in the.morning ration, about a tablespoon- ful for every dozen birds. Plenty of green food and grit should also be supplied. If the culprit can be caught in the act of plucking out its own or another's feathers, it should, if not a valuable bird, be killed, as if it is allowed to continue the whole flock will soon become affected. If a valuable bird, it should be isolated for a few weeks.—Middlesex; in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>FROM HEN TO HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>FROM HEN TO HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Sixteen Millions 01 Eggs For Base Hospitals.</p>
        <p>It may be of interest to the public to learn that well over sixteen millions of new-laid eggs have been given to "The National Egg Collection" to date, and. distributed by the committee to the Base Hospitals in France and elsewhere, and that up to the end of the first financial year every egg supplied to a wounded soldier cost one-fifth of a penny, not taking into account the freight charges, which, up to the end of the same period, amounted to £11,690. Thousands of egg boxes have been purchased at a total value of nearly £6,000. Of the sum of £10,369, donated by the public up to the 31st March, 1916, .28,261 has been expended on the purchase of eggs; salaries came to £896, and there was a balance at the bank of £1,003. The committee have established all over the country nearly, 2,000 depots, with innumerable sub-dvots and collecting centres. Approxi- mately there are 100,000 collectors engaged in the work. The committee comprises representatives of the two oldest and most influential poultry organisations in the country, the proprietor of the largest poultry farm in the world, and representatives of three independent Poultry Journals. The distribution of these millions of eggs has brought thousands of letters from wounded men, testifying to their grateful appreciation. The Army Council has sent several letters, specially commending the committee's work. They have had testimony as to its value from the Quartermaster-General Sir John Cowans, from the late and present Directors of Army Supplies, and from the Inspector-General of Communications, Headquarters, France, to say nothing of the countless letters of gratitude from the hospitals themselves. As the demand upon them from the wounded now exceeds one million new-laid eggs per week, the committee trust that their appeal to the public to make the contributions of eggs and cash as generous as possible will be promptly responded. to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>HAY AND STRAW FOR THE ARMY.</head>
        <p>HAY AND STRAW FOR THE ARMY.</p>
        <p>The Position Of Farmers And Dealers. Many erroneous and conflicting statements have appeared in the Press in reference to the position of farmers and dealers under the cir- cumstances which have arisen through the acquisition of hay and straw (wheat and oat) by the Army under the Defence of the Realm Regulations. To rectify some of these-impres- sions, we are requested to make the following statement, which may be taken as accurately representing the position:— The maximum prices to be received by pro- ducers of hay and straw in each county are those which have been fixed by the County Com- mittee, and approved by the Forage Committee. This price applies to all hay and straw sold to dealers, and not merely to that taken for Government use, with one exception, viz., a pro- ducer who has also a retail business, can make what price he can from the actual consumer, but before getting an authority for removal, he has to prove to the satisfaction of the military authorities that the amount for which he applies under such authority, for removal, is a monthly one, based on his average sales for the previous three months. It must be understood that a producer, for the purpose of evading the Defence of the Realm Order, cannot suddenly become a retailer. An incorrect statement has been made, that at present no permits will be granted for the removal of wheat or oat straw. Such permits will be given at the discretion of thelDistrict Purchasing Officer to whom application is made, but it must tie certified that the straw is to be used for forage in lieu of hay, and the issue of these licences is only made where justified by all the circumstances of particular cases. All applications for • removal of hay in Cheshire should be addressed to the District Purchasing Officer, 16, Old Bank Buildings, Chester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>WEEKLIES v. DAILIES.</head>
        <p>WEEKLIES v. DAILIES.</p>
        <p>How Weekly Newspaper Has Advantage Over Dailies.</p>
        <p>A remarkable article on the relative value of daily and weekly newspapers as advertising agencies appears in a recent issue of "The Advertisers' Weekly," in which a northern con- tributor contends that the local weekly is the ideal medium for advertising household neces- sities, and asserts that such advertisements in daily papers are not read. It has always been a contention of mine, he says, that the big dailies are not the best media for advertising purely domestic commo- dities; in fact, I believe that the majority of the household advertisements in the halfpenny dailies are never even seen. I don't speak as a "mere man" on the question. I have tested the truth of these statements by questioning the women folk about the advertisements in. the paper taken at the particular home. The "Daily" paper is purely a newspaper. In- stinctively the reader turns to the news columns, and having read these, the whole paper is "ancient history"; consigned to the kitchen for fire lighting; or in these days of economy put aside for the boy scout to cart' away to the rag shop. With the "fair sex" this is especially the case. The good man fre- quently carries the daily paper about with him until he returns from business; and then the wife has to read the news "in a hurry" after the day's duties are ended. The Local Weekly.</p>
        <p>What then do these people read, and, more important stilt, which media contains the domestic advertising that is READ? My experi- ence among Northerners leads me immediately to say, "The local weekly paper." The glorious week-end is awaited by tired housewives with no small degree of pleasurable anticipation, and one of the greatest of the week-end joys is the devouring of the local paper—front page, back, inside and outside. Starting with the "Births, Marriages and Deaths," through the weekly sales and the "Domestics Wanted," on and on, without a pause, unfired, the whole of the letterpress is eagerly read, and the entire paper studied with a devotion which is bked of parochial patriotism.</p>
        <p>The Lingo. Tommy (to Jock, on leave): "What about the lingo? Suppose you want an egg over there. What do you say P" Jock: "Ye juist say 'Oof.'" Tommy: "But suppose you want two?" Jock: "Ye say Twa oofs ' and the silly auld fule wife gies ye thre and ye juist gie he back one. Man it's an awfu' easy language."— "Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 29, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 29, 1916.</p>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>At Edinburgh, a dairykeeper admitted that he was in the habit of adding a small quantity of hot water to the milk in order" to remove the flavour of the turnips." He was fined .£7, the analyst having found that there was 7 per cent. of water in the sainple of milk taken.</p>
        <p>A SUGGESTED CHANGE IN PROCEDURE. There is an old fable that tells us that the wind and the sun entered into a competition to see which of them could first get a traveller out of his coat. The wind blew furiously, but the only result was that the man wrapped his coat more tightly around him. Then the sun came out, and his warmth soon made the traveller glad to cast aside his covering. We have tried prosecutions against adulteration of• milk, and there is plenty of it practised notwithstanding. Perhaps a system of combined close inspection and rewards might effect a change for the better. No, says "Farm and Home," we are not sug- gesting that prosecutions should be done away with. Most people are honest—that is a view from which we have not been weaned during a long lifetime—but there are rogues in the world, and they have to be discovered and punished whenever possible. But the honest men are in the majority, after all, and Government measures might recognise the fact with advan- ' tage. How P Well, that is for the Government to , devise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>IRISH LABOURER.</head>
        <p>THE IRISH LABOURER.</p>
        <p>In ordinary circumstances Irish labourers migrate in considerable though decreasing num- bers to England and Scotland from this time of year onwards. They begin to arrive in time to take part in the operations in late spring that require an increased amount of manual labour, and the influx grows until it reaches high-water mark at the advent of the harvest, after which the aggregate on British farms gradually ,de- clines until the last take their departure in the late, autumn. The farmer in his plight is hoping impatiently for the arrival of Irishmen in un- wonted numbers. His wish is natural and reasonable, and yet it is doubtful if it can be fully gratified, not from an insufficiency of pos- sible migrants, but from the doubtful wisdom of encouraging the migration of unmarried men of military age. The subject has been discussed by various bodies, and it is to their credit that the members have shown a decided „aversion_to engaging men who, if they had been English, Welsh, or Scottish, would have been with the colours. While there may be a good defence for utilising the services of the first man who comes along, whatever his nationality, age, or physical capability, it is impossible to withhold admira- tion of the man who declines to replace kis sons, or labourers, with men that he considers should be fighting alongside them in the trenches. There is a certain delicacy about the point, and it would be a mistake to enlarge upon it unduly, but reference having bEen made to it by various bodies, and the indiscriminate intro- duction of Irishmen having been suggested in Parliament itself, it may be permissible to ex- press sympathy with the farmer who has a con- scientious objection to employing able-bodied young Irishmen, and to make the suggestion that as far as procticable the migration of Irish labourers should be restricted to men ineligible for the Army or Navy.—" Field."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>ALIEN LABOUR ON FARMS.</head>
        <p>ALIEN LABOUR ON FARMS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Cheshire War Agricultural Committee at Crewe, Colonel Dixon presiding, the Hon. J. E. Cross read a letter from Lord Newton on the subject of employing interned aliens on the land, and expressed the hope that Cheshire would give a lead to the country in this matter. Mr. Cross said there were many aliens who had no connection with Germany, such as Ottoman subjects and Galicians. They could ncst speak English perhaps, but that was scarcely necessary, and he did not think they would try to escape. He urged the committee to make arrangements with the police to find some farmers who were sufficiently enterprising to make the experiment. Dr. Hodgson said he hoped spies were not going to be turned loose into Cheshire. The idea was perfectly imprac- ticable. The Chief Constable said if alien enemies in small -numbers were employed on farms and under supervision he saw no reason- able objection, but whether the alien would be welcomed, or made comfortable, he had con- siderable doubt. In certain places he would not allow them to enter. The matter was referred to the district committees for consideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>DESTROYING WASPS BY A SIMPLE METHOD.</head>
        <p>DESTROYING WASPS BY A SIMPLE METHOD.</p>
        <p>Dr. A. L. ittelander, Entomologist of the Wash- ington Experiment Station, gives the following simple method of destroying wasps' nests on the ground or in old trees. If the nest can be dis- covered, which usually means watching the in- sects when they fly home, the entire colony can be quickly exterminated. A gallon jug, quarter- filled with water and placed next to the nest, is all that is needed. A wasp will emerge from the nest opening, perceive the jug, and fly angrily at it. The hollow sound of its buzzing, echoing from the jug, will make it enter, when it</p>
        <p>falls into the water. Wasps are able to corn. municate with each other and the imprisoned wasp possibly calls to its sisters. At any rate, the colony, one by one, in the course of the day, will find its way into the jug.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>RICKETS IN PIGS.</head>
        <p>RICKETS IN PIGS.</p>
        <p>In this disease, which is commonly found among, young.,pigs, there is enlargeznent, bend- ing, and distortion of the bones of the joints and limbs, and fractures of leg bones are not un-</p>
        <p>common. The bones do not contain their nor- mal proportion of mineral matter, and hence lack strength. It is claimed that the disease is most common among closely in-bred pigs. The excessive feeding of maize throughout gener- ations of swine is believed in America, to be an exciting cause, or any conditions which inter- fere with proper nutrition, such as disease or unsanitary surroundings, may predispose to- wards the disease in question. Dr. Alexander, of Wisconsin, says, "Pigs affected with rickets can seldom be profitably treated. Prevention is to be sought by avoiding the causes mentioned, maintaining sanitary conditions about the animals, providing adequate supplies of various feeds, rich in all the requisites of a perfectly nourished animal, and obviating degeneracy by careful selection of robust sows and timely in- fusion of new blood." Plenty of mineral mat- ter should be provided at all times during the life of the pig. Methods and forms of supplying mineral matter have already been given in this bulletin.—" Bulletin," 225,. Ontario Agrie. Coll.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>GARDEN EDGINGS.</p>
        <p>Instead of the hard, formal tiles, so often seen as edgings, it would be far better to use rough stones, planting in the vacant spaces between the stones, which have been filled with good soil. such things as stone-crops, rockfoils, &amp;c., with here and there a few plants of the common single arabis, or the double form, aubretias, hair bells, and other dwarf things. The common thrift, when in bloom a mass of rich pink, makes a fine edging, while the London pride, if given a good root run, flowers freely and remains for several weeks in bloom. What more beautiful as an edging than the common pink, so dear to .the cottager, and which in country districts is so often found forming wide lines of bloom and wafting its fragrance all over the garden. ,Such edgings as these, when they get too wide and occupy too much space, can be easily lifted, divided and replanted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>VEGETABLE MARROWS.</head>
        <p>VEGETABLE MARROWS.</p>
        <p>When marrows are wanted early seeds may now be sown singly in small pots, which should be placed on a hotbed or in a greenhouse. When the seedlings have formed the first rough leaf they may be put into 6-inch pots and returned to the frame or house until they are growing freely. When such is the case they may be gradually moved to the open air, and when well hardened off and all danger of frost is over they may be planted out where to fruit towards the end of May. Those who have no glass and are content to wait a little longer for their marrows may sow the seeds in stations prepared for them in the open air, placing a hand-light or a bell glass or even a flower-pot over them, and when they appear protecting with a basket or such like should the weather be cold. Such plants will fruit freely all through the summer if the precaution is taken to cut the fruits while quite young. A friend of mine always used to sow three seeds in each station, and when the plants appeared destroy the two weakest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>FARMERS' FUND'S PROGRESS.</head>
        <p>FARMERS' FUND'S PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>£70,000 To Restore Lands Devastated By Huns.</p>
        <p>In a little over twelve months the Agricul- tural Relief of Allies Fund (16, ITedford- square, London) has received about £70,000 from British farmers to be applied in restoring the farmers of Allied countries to their lands when the Germans are driven off. By the end of this year the Royal Agricultural Society, who initiated the fund, hope that the amount will at least be doubled.</p>
        <p>Peasant farmers in Belgfahm, France, Serbia and Poland have suffered the horrors of inva- sion, which but for our Navy and the hindrance which these smallholders placed in the way of the German advance, we might have had to suffer, too. British farmers, therefore, have pledged themselves to back up their admiration of the bravery of Allied farmers by practical help in rehabilitating them on their lands—the land on which the . battle for our existence as an Empire is being fought. Several thousands of pounds have already been spent in restoring farmers in the Marne and the Meuse, and the committee hope, with the continued help of British agriculturists at jumble sales and in other ways, to give relief on a much greater scale in Belgium and Serbia when the oppor- tunity occurs.</p>
        <p>The Serbian Government is already provid- ing the committee with information as to tb, best form of help to be given to the farmers in that unfortunate country, in anticipation of the invader being driven out before long.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>BLINDED FOR YOU.</head>
        <p>BLINDED FOR YOU.</p>
        <p>Soldiers And Sailors Who Have Lost Their Sight.</p>
        <p>PREPARED FOR USEFUL FUTURE.</p>
        <p>The report of the first year's doings at St. Dunstan's, the hostel which has been estab- lished at Regent's Park, London, N.W., for blinded soldiers and sailors, under the joint auspices of the British Red Cross Society, the Order of St. John of Jerusalem in England, and the National Institute for the Blind, is a mixture of sad and pleasurable reading, sad because of the need for the institution, pleasur- able because of the way that need is being met. It was on March 26th, 1915, that a start was made at the hostel with 14 blinded soldiers, who were brought from a temporary establishment kindly lent by Mrs. Lewis Hall until the hostel was ready. The number has steadily grown. There are now at St. Dunstan's, or in the an- nexes at Brighton and Torquay, a total of 146 non-eoinmissioned officers and men, among whom are included five Australians, two New Zealanders, and seven Canadians. At 21, Port- land-place, a spacious residence belonging to -Sir John and Lady Stirling-Maxwell, and most geiirously placed at the disposal of the com- mittee by them. there live 10 officers who take their instruction at St. Dunstan's. They learn to read and, write in Braille, to typewrite, and to acquire some ocCupation useful in their future lives for pleasure!'Or profit. • Fifty-one men have passed out from St. Dunstan's, having learned one or more of the following occupations: Boot repairing, mat- making, net-making, basket-making, joinery, poultry-farming and market-gardening, while one officer has qualified as a masseur. So far as is known there are in hospital at this date 45 blinded non-commissioned officers and men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Occupations Taught.</head>
        <p>Occupations Taught.</p>
        <p>The remarkable speed with which the occupa- tions taught in the workshops are acquired— for the men at St. Dunstan's learn trades in far less time than is usually considered necessary— is attributable principally to the influence of the blind teacher. _ _</p>
        <p>The best men. who are nearing the end of their course of training are made pupil teachers. A great feeling of confidence is given* to a new- comer when .he finds that his instructor was himself blinded only a few months ago. Several men who occupied secretarial positions before they joined the Army are learning Braille shorthand, which, combined with a knowledge of typewriting, will enable them to resume their former work. The Remington Typewriter Co., in consultation with expert teachers of the blind, have built special type- writers for the use of these men and men who have lost one hand.</p>
        <p>Massage is likely to prove the most lucrative of the occupations taught at St. Dunstan's. Primary instruction in anatomy and physiology is given there, and when the preliminary stage has been passed tuition is continued in the newly-equipped massage department at The National Institute for the Blind, where there is every appliance and convenience. When these men have been fully trained each has open to him a position in a military hos- pital, where he will receive free lodgings and attendance, and be paid £2 13s. a week, a highly satisfactory start for a man who has been recently handicapped by blindness. • The After Care Of Men.</p>
        <p>The important work of devising a satisfactory scheme of after-care for the men who are settled at their various occupations is proceed- ing satisfactorily. This department will be managed by Mr. T. H. Martin, a well-known worker in the blind world, who was for some years superintendent of the Swiss Cottage School for the Blind. Arrangements have to be made for supervising the men's work, which would otherwise tend to deteriorate, for pro- viding their raw material, for marketing their goods, and for securing continuity of well-paid employment for those who have been trained in such occupations as massage and boot. repairing.</p>
        <p>A large settled fund will be needed if this work, which has been entrusted to a special department of the National Institute for the Blind, is to prove permanently useful. May I ask all who feel touched by this brief record of splendid determination and resolu- tion to overcome a terrible disability to show their appreciation by helping us? I have written a plain, straightforward statement of the work at St. Dunstan's. I do not think i needs to be bolstered up by high-flown senti- mentality. The simple expression of the endeavours which are being made to lighten the burden laid upon the gallant men of St.. Dunstan's, the plain recital of their achieve- ments should be enough. These men have given very much indeed for their country. The least we can do is to take every means in our power to ensure that they are placed in a position successfully to fight their stern battle against so terrible a handicap as the loss of sight. C. ARTHUR PEARSON, Chairman, Blind Soldiers' and Sailors' Care Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <p>iSiOll of the Proprietors of "Punch</p>
        <p>THE CHAMPION OF TIIE SMALLER NATIONS.</p>
        <p>IM rEllj AL PACHYDERM: "Our heart goes out to these poor little unprotected eggs. Theywant mothering. We will sit on them." (Does se.)</p>
        <p>to a noble animal.] [With Mr. Punch's apologies</p>
      </div>
