      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>R7II.EIGH</head>
        <p>R7II.EIGH</p>
        <p>.-STEEL BICYCLE With the liquid-brazed joints. GUARANTEED FOR EVER Fitted with Dunlop Tyres and Sturm ey-Arch er Tr-Coaster. • Prices freo £7 10. to £l6 100. Seed a Post Card for " The Book of the Raleigh."</p>
        <p>Widnes: C. Bingham, 7a, Vic- • toria-road. Ransom : H. Smith and Son, 22, Greenway-road. St. Helens: W. Cook, to 7,</p>
        <p>Church-street. Earlestows : J. A. Churchill &amp; Co., Cross-lane. Ashtoa-in-Makertiald: S. Reeves,</p>
        <p>Warrington-road. Lowton St. Mary: Birkenhead Bros.. 128/146, Newton-road.</p>
        <p>RALEIGH OYOLII CO., LTD., NOTTINGHAM. 'Cycling for Health and Points for Cyclists; by Sir Frank Bowden. Bart., F.R.G.S. 1/- 100 pp. From Agents and Bookstalls.</p>
        <p>d</p>
        <p>wood. Keep the inside of the rim free from rust and the rim tape in good repair. Clean all tyre cuts as soon as they are discovered with petrol, and fill with the special tyre stopping sold for the purpose, giving the stuff plenty of time to set hard. It will never cling to the rubber unless the latter is clean and free from grit. Run your hand round each tyre after every run to feel for half-penetrating nails, flints, and the like. Don't run over a bad road patch when you can possibly avoid it.</p>
        <p>Germany Supplied With Motor Fuel. Soon after the war broke out Germans suffered very acutely from a petrol famine, so much so that private motoring was forbidden. But as time has gone on she has managed somehow or other to secure supplies, and if the evidence of neutrals who have lately been in Germany is to be believed, taxi-cabs, 'buses, and even private cars are again in use. Ac- cording to some particulars which were lately given in a contemporary it appears that when the fuel position became so acute the whole chemical skill of Germany was organised to discover a substitute for petrol—one that could be prepared in plentiful quantities from mate- rial in the country. It is stated that this substitute has been found, and it is in general use. It gives within a fraction the same power and consumption as petrol, viz., five-sixths, and does not require any alteration of the engine or carburetter. All that has been done is to fix an exhaust heating jacket to each carburetter. The petrol substitute Germany is using contains nearly 80 per cent. alcohol, 20 per cent. benzole, and an addition of about 200 grammes per gallon of naphthalene, a solid hydrocarbon, of which they have great quan- tities in their chemical works. Benzole is an easily soluble hydrocarbon, and presumably it adds greatly to the calorific value of the mixture. The alcohol alone has a low calorific value, and therefore the benzole and naphtha- lene are added partly as enrichers. The form of alcohol that Germany is using, it may be presumed, is potato spirit. The benzole gets over the starting difficulty resulting from the low vapour pressure of alcohol.</p>
        <p>SPOKES.</p>
        <p>A Particular Boy. Shopkeeper: "Yes, I want a good useful lad to be partly, indoors and partly outdoors." Applicant: "And what becomes of me when the door slams?"—" Punch."</p>
