      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>HOLIDAY IN NORTH WALES.</head>
        <p>A HOLIDAY IN NORTH WALES.</p>
        <p>One of the most charming holiday grounds is that of North Wales, with its variety of scenery, from bleak rugged moors to well- wooded lanes, while the sea views are particu- larly fine. The roads on the whole are good, although, of course, hilly. Chester is the gate of North Wales, and this will give a good round :—Chester to Rhyl, 311; Llandudno, .161; Bettws-y-Coed, 211; Carnarvon, 231; Barmouth, 34; Druid, 37; Llangollen, 12k; Chebter, 221; total, 200 i miles. Chester itself is one of the most fascinating cities in England, and a day can be very pro- fitably spent in visiting its many interesting associations With the past. The Wall, built by the Romans, is still plainly visible; there is a fine cathedral, and many 17th century timber houses. Six miles out lies Hawarden, Gladstone's historic house, ,and many will visit the little church to view the monument which has just been raised to his grandson, that "very per- fect knight." The ride from Rhyl to Llan- dudno is a most enjoyable one along the coast, passing on the way Abergele and Colwyn Bay; thence up the lovely valley to Bettws-y-Coed, via Conway, Taly-Cafn and Llanrwst, one of the most beautiful vales in Britain. The ride from *Bettws-y-Coed to Barmouth is wild, pre- senting every kind of scenery on the way. Be- yond Capel Curig, down the rough pass of Llanberis, the shoulder of Snowdon looms to the left to Carnarvon, where the castle should be visited; then by the other side of Snowdon and Beddgelert, through the wooded pass of Aberglaslyn to Penrhyndeudraeth, Harlech and Barmouth. From Barmouth the road pro- ceeds up the river side to Dolgelly. A wild moorland ride then ensues by Bala to the Holyhead road, at where turn to the right for Corwen and far-famed Llangollen. The road back to Chester lies through Ruabon,,Mrex- ham, Rossett and Pulford. Between Pulford and Chester is the stately home of the Puke of Westminster, Eaton Hall, in the park of which cyclists but not motorists) are per- mitted to ride, and this makes a very welcome change from the main road. Enter the park at Belgrave Lodge, ride along the avenue until the Golden Gates of Eaton Hall are reached, and continue along the drive for three miles, when the main road is joined at Overleigh Lodge. This adds i miles to the distance, but it is well worth it.</p>
        <p>How To Remove Handle Grips. There are few more uncomfortable things for the cyclist—and more unsightly things, for the matter of that—than a chipped, battered or broken handle grip. As grips are cheap enough in all conscience, there is no excuse for retain- ing those that are broken. The only trouble is to remove the old grips from the bars. Knowing that a loose grip may lead to a nasty accident, makers invariably secure them very firmly with solid-tyre cement. Now, this cement is somewhat similar to glue. When gold it is in solid chunks, but when heated it liquifies, rapidly cooling again, however, and clinging like grim death to anything it has come into contact with—metal handle bars, grips, and 60 on. Obviously, then, in order to remove a grip stuck on with its aid, the thing is to heat the handle bar sufficiently to reduce the glue again to its liquid state. This is most easily done by holding the handle bar in a gas flame just far enough away from the grip to prevent scorching. But remeinber that grips are mostly made of celluloid and are therefore highly inflammable; they must on no account be brought into actual contact with the flame. If you go to work on, the slow and .sure prin- ciple, allowing the heat to work into the grip gradually, you will very soon be able to re- move the latter with a slight twist. Alterna- tively the grip end of the handle bar can be stood in hot water for some time, but the dis- advantage of this method is that it softens a celluloid grip so much that when you take Ii old of. it fo twist it your fingers press it out of shape. Do not forget to sc. ape off all the old cement before fitting the new grips.</p>
        <p>Some Tips To Prevent Punctures. Of course, when you are "meant" to have a puncture, you WILL have a puncture, willy- nilly. But there are certain precautions every wise motor cyclist will take to keep the punc- ture fiend at bay at all times, save when he is conducting an irresistible offensive. Among the means of preventing punctures are the fol- lowing :—Never use anything else, but tyre levers made for the purpose of attaching or replacing covers. To use a screwdriver, for instance, is fatal. After mending a puncture and restoring the tube to the cover never in- flate the tube more than enough to prevent nipping. On no account have it so hard as to prevent you from rolling the cover out of one side of the rim sufficiently to assure yourself that there is no pinching. See there are no dents in the rim. Dents are easily hammered out by means of a hammer and a block of</p>
      </div>
