      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>/dins wily Odor</head>
        <p>/dins wily Odor</p>
        <p>A TRENCH OPINION OF "CONSCIENCES." .</p>
        <p>sir,—As a reader of "The Examiner" for a 411Inber of years I should like to ,be allowed 8,-PaCe to refer to the " conscientious objectors." "0 these objectors know the meaning of this ,war, and what it means to England if we lase? AI they do, why do they hang behind their wives' apron strings and their sweethearts' ekirts? If they don't know, well I must tell Ulem that they must be pro-Germans or cowards, !rid not worthy of the name of Britisher. Some ,notice have appealed on behalf of home and How many men have left Warrington, a IS'iod situation, and a loving wife and children, IV Probably better homes than some of the °:ieetors, and have willingly given their life on ‘41,) battlefield? ~1 see in this last week's "Examiner" a pub- 'lean not wanting his son to go. Is he any better than any other publican's son or working zaan's son? No. I myself left a good job and came out to fight. I have also given a son to the colours, and would willingly _give them all if I thought their help would Win this war, Which I am sure would bo. credit to the old town. This publican says he cannot trust a man over military age. He must remember, and I know publicans' sons who are not as they should be. and it is blamed on the barman. I say to any men of British ranck in the good Old town "Come with a good heart, be of good Courage. Be a man and play the game."</p>
        <p>PTE. T. J. TAYLOR, B.E.F.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>DRUNKENNESS AM) RESTRIC-</head>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS AM) RESTRIC-</p>
        <p>TIONS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Simpson is rather funny in his way of dealing with the above question. When the Control Board uses a period of four weeks for eereParison he says "The police have assisted 7,011"; when twenty weeks' comparison is given ee says "It is not of much value." Why is it not of much value? Because it tells us that he ,k 3 wrong and cannot prove his charge against rtke total abstainers. The depletion of the male Population commenced in 191 i, and I think was ,more rapid during those five months than in &amp;die latter part of 1915. The Government's reply res_ Sir John Rees' request for the removal of P. Snowden from the Control Board was ta ignore the request, and both Dr. Macnamara (Admiralty Department) and Dr. Addison (Munitions Department) testified, in the House Of Commons, to the good result of the restric- tions. Probably the trouble with Mr. Simpson is that the Control Board is out of reach as far 412 "Trade influence" is concerned, a step in right direction; and if we can retain either this or any other Control Board under similar circumstances it will be better for the country. But the best we could do would be to rid for ever from our liind that commodity which is our greatest curse. I submit again that Mr. Simpson has utterly failed to substantiate the charge he made. The evidence from all quarters, even from meetings of members of "The Trade" itself, is entirely against him.</p>
        <p>BENJ. T. PARKES. 8, Willis-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>:flr</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>AN INDICTMENT OF GERMANY.</head>
        <p>AN INDICTMENT OF GERMANY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Perhaps the time has arrived whenl Silence may be broken, and politicians, both amateur and professional, may air their I opinions on what should or should not be after the war and the problems we have to face. The 4rticle by Sir A. H. Crosfield, in your issue of the Bth, seOms to me to be entirely devoted to Would policy, showing what cannot be done. would it not be better to try to formulate something possible instead of wasting time on I the impossible? Though war was declared at the beginning of August, 1914, yet we may consider that war 0,1 a sort had`been in progress for many years. we may take it that, if not from the triumph cd Prussia over her rival, Austria, in 1866, est certainly from her victory over France In 1870-71, the Prussian war spirit has been abroad, and more or less active in a progres- 81Ve sense. There are other ways of waging war than by armies in the field, and no one Understands this mode of warfare better than the Germans of to-day and their immediate Predecessors. Ever since the Franco-German War they have practised, week in, week out. Month by month, year by year, this insidious end underhand ,policy towards all who had dealings with her. Take only the English-speaking countries of the world, and investigate how they have been treated by this German horde, who have in- vaded their shores. They have abused ' the liberty and freedom of Britain and her Colonies; they have imposed upon the hospi- tality of the United States; they have annexed every little patch of unhypothecated land throughout the world. Not only have they done these things, but they have twisted and turned the fundamental laws of each section Of the world to their advantage, and where they eiould congregate in sufficient numbers they nave not hesitated to make full use of their numerical strength, to the benefit of their own Fatherland. Taking advantage of English laws theY have become naturalised Englishmen with- out abandoning one iota of German nationality; ,tileY have adopted all our Joint Stock Company 'awe, trading under English names, and not</p>
        <p>W,7</p>
        <p>only so, but have also, where possible, captured genuine English firms. They have used all these advantages to give preference to their own Fatherland, to the disadvantage of the country of their adoption. Our proud policy of Freedom of Trade has been taken advantage of to dump cheap goods on our markets, till ' they have undersold the Britisher and• driven, him from his own mar- kets, and then they have gained the monopoly. This is instanCed in the dye-making trade. They have secured monopolies in our raw material as instanced in the case of gas mantles and tungsten-powder. The deposits of monazite for gas mantles came from Traveneore, in the extreme south of India, while the wolfram ore, from which tugsten-powder for hardening steel was made, was obtained from the southern extremity of Burma. Both these raw materials were exported in their raw state to Germany, and re-exported in their finished state to Bri- tain at an increased price. Mr. Hughes tells us that while the Germans obtained the finished tungsten at .00 a ton, they had the effrontery to charge the Englishman from .290 to £lOO for the same quantity. Those who have sufficitntly long memories may • easily recollect the Anglophobia of Ger- many during the Boer War; and those whose memory is not so long- will remerhber in. what state of preparedness the Allies found the Ger- man nationality throughout the world at the opening of the present war. In Germany itself was the finest army of the world, waii a navy that at least came second in power. We found colonies of Germans all over the world, irre- spective of whether it was neutral or belligerent territory, German or non-German, prepared to carry out the desires and behests of their own country, without any regard to the status of the ' country in which war found them. The world was encircled with wireless stations, from which were conveyed information to Berlin. This was tile state of affairs when Germany let loose her armed hosts to ravage and to slay. We know the manner in which they have carried out their military operations, and it is not necessary for roe to discuss in detail all of their methods of procedure. Let me just point out that on land and sea and in the air the same ruthlessness has guided German action. No mercy has been shown in any particular case. The same harshness and frightfulness have been dealt out to all, whether combatants or non-combatants, whether civil or military, whether old or young, rich or poor. The same thoroughness has been dealt out and no pity towards even the most helpless has been shown. They have absolutely placed themselves outside the pale of civilisation, and become, in their present attitude, a menace and a danger to the human race.</p>
        <p>The present state of Germany demonstrates to us that in spite of all the wealth of ,culture and learning a nation may be lost to all the finer feelings of humanity and religion, and degenerate into absolute heathenism, as dark and benighted as any period of the Dark Ages of the past. My indictment is severe, but no less just, and in another letter I may try to have something to say on the remedy to be applied.</p>
        <p>PHIL SQUOD</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <p>_</p>
        <p>"AN OUTLINE OF INDUSTRIAL</p>
        <p>HISTORY."</p>
        <p>By Edward Cressy. London: Macmillan and Co., Ltd. 3s. 6d. net.</p>
        <p>This is certainly the best introductory book dealing with Industrial History that we have yet seen. The special feature which distin- guishes it from other" Outlines"' or "Intro- ductions" is that although it covers pretty much the same period, Mr. Cressy's work de- votes particular attention to the modern period and to the problems arising out of modern industrial and political developments. Part I. is a very brief account of the rise of industry, and Part 11. takes us through the Middle Ages up to the end of the 17th century. Mr. Cressy's justification for this summary treatment of the earlier periods is, of course, that the greater portion of his book deals with the modern period. A brief historical account is given of Agriculture, Textile. Industries, .Fuel and its Applications. An extremely interesting chap- ter is devoted to the Union of Science and Practice with the object of illustrating how scientific discovery and mechanical invention have increased the productive capacity of the individual workman, and have altered and are altering the technical character of industrial control. Two chapters dealing with facilities for commercial development treat of transport and communication and banking and the credit system. Mr. Cressy then turns to the evolution of industrial management, and in a series of interesting chapters gives an account of Com- binations of Capital, Conflicts of Capital and Labour, Co-operation and Municipal Owner- ship. The section on Industry and Politics dealing with the Extension of the Franchise, the change in the function of Government, the State and the Factory, the growth of Economic Thought, and National Education, brings the book to a close. A short bibliography provides suggestions for further reading. We think Mr. Cressy has been eminently suc- cessful in his effort to deal with large masses of facts in a brief compass, to give the eesen- tial features of modern industrial history. For the general reader who desires an introduction to wider reading on the subject we think the hook will be an excellent guide, and for that increasing body of earnest students belonging to the Workers' Educational Association, Adult Schools, and other similar associations, it is likely to take its place at once as one of the best and cheapest text-books available.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
