      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>4, v.; ..., 41:,, . . :,:e. . ''''''''!': ~. ' t'.;; .. • W . . 3 ~.. • , • WO .b., LD .</head>
        <p>4, v.; ..., 41:,, . . :,:e. . ''''''''!': ~. ' t'.;; .. • W . . 3 ~.. • , • WO .b., LD .</p>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Sumptuary Censorship. German " thoroughness " is exemplified in thi Teutonic campaign against feminine extrava- gance in dress. Threats of military decrees, an.l prohibitive taxation having failed to suppress the extravagantly dressed woman, the police are now empowered to arrest such offenders., The German newspapers coming to hand, contain' many references to the agitation against ex- pensive attire, and lament the change to the wide skirt and high boots, which make increased demands for woollen materials and leather, when these commodities are wanted for the soldiers. What most aggravates the economy campaignists is, that the German women are adopting a' mode that originated in enemy fashion centres. In this country. the Govern- ment poster " Bad form in Dress," is as far as we have got at present, in official sumptuary censorship. Not every women accustomed to move in fashionable circles, is paying heed to it, but the poster has been followed up by a personal appeal from the Chancellor of the Exchequer, addressed to every woman, to invest her spare money in Exchequer Bonds, and none can doubt that this course would be a more patriotic use of money than spending it on superfluous finery.</p>
        <p>Official Recognition For Policewomen. The collaboration of women in police work is being seriously considered by the various muni- cipal authorities of London. When war broke out, the need for women patrols was recognised both by the officers in command of the newly formed camps, and by the chief constables in the counties. That need is not so pressing now. but with the numbers of women working in various industries, difficulties are constantly arising, that can be dealt with far better by police women than by men. There are no fewer than two thousand women patrols in London and other parts of the kingdom, whose duties are not so much the detection of crime, as the rendering of assistance to women and girls in</p>
      </div>
