      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Useful Information In Popular Language.</head>
        <p>Useful Information In Popular Language.</p>
        <p>The Nutritive Part of Potatoes.</p>
        <p>In peeling potatoes, care should be taken to take off just as little of the skin as possible, and new potatoes should be scraped. The outer layer contains a larger portion of protein than all of the rest of the potato.</p>
        <p>Leek Soup. Cut up two onions and fry them till they are brown; you need not use butter—clarified fat will do very well. Clean the leeks, washing them well, cut them in pieces and fry them also; add any other vegetables that you have, two medium-sized potatoes, pepper, salt, and a little water. Let all simmer for three hours, and pass through a fine sieve. Let there be more leeks than other vegetables so that theft flavour may predominate. Yorkshire Parkin. •</p>
        <p>Ingredients: Vb. medium oatmeal, :lib. flour, Vb. treacle, 3oz. sugar, 3oz. dripping, teaspoon- ful of ginger, half-teaspoonful of mixed spice, teaspoonful of carbonate of soda, one break- fast cupful of . boiling water. Method: Put the flour and oatmeal into a basin, mix in the dry ingredients, rub in the dripping, melt the treacle, dissolve the carbonate of soda in the boiling water, add it to the treacle, then stir in the contents of the basin. Thoroughly mix, grease a flat dripping tin, and pour the mixture in. Bake in a moderate oven about an hour, or until it feels quite firm on the top. When cool, cut in finger lengths oz squares.—" Hull News."</p>
        <p>•:.4.4•4.4,.4.4.••:•••+ • 4- Children' s Corner. 4. •</p>
        <p>; el. Conducted by UNCLE JACK. • 4* +44 +4* • .44* •C. C. 4.4 C.</p>
        <p>Children, Jorn This Cornerl Enter The Competitions! Write Your Best!</p>
        <p>Always Be Glad! Uncle Jack, "Examiner," Warrington,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>ROVING COMMISSION.</head>
        <p>A ROVING COMMISSION.</p>
        <p>Mr DEAR CHILDREN,— You have had a roving commission during the holidays. That is, you have been able to select your own subject and deal with it in the way you like best. I am giving two first prizes this week. Niece Edna Fellows, 44, Bewsey-road, who attends Evelyn-street School, wins one prize with what she calls a "rather queer" letter, in which she tells me of an imaginary conversation between an apple and an orange. It is a pleasant and profitable occu- pation for children to write imaginary atones of this kind, and many teachers encourage such exercises. I suppose Niece Edna has learnt the art at Evelyn-street School, and she has learnt it very well indeed for a girl of twelve, as you will see when you read it. Nephew Ernest William Sankey, 99, Kimberley-street, wins tho other with a fine soldier-story, also an imaginary story, showing that St. Barnabas' School, where he attends, is also well up in that class of work. Ernest is only ten, and, if I remember rightly, his father is at the war fighting for , his country. This week I want you to write me an account of your holidays and your return to school under the title</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>" Holidays and Back to School"</head>
        <p>" Holidays and Back to School"</p>
        <p>i know all my nephews and nieces will have some very fine things to say about the holidays. as well as some interesting things respecting the return to duty!</p>
        <p>I have had a long letter from Uncle Ned re- specting the promotion and title scheme, which I print below. You will see he is full of sug- gestions. We will take a while to consider before finally deciding upon so important a matter.</p>
        <p>Your loving UNCLE JACK</p>
        <p>THE WINNING LETTERS. By Niece Edna Fellows (No. 768).</p>
        <p>Conversation Between an Apple and an Orange. "Oh, dear! Excuse me, please. I couldn't help bumping into you then, Miss Orange. I'm very sorry, but the people are awfully rude here. They knock us about any way." "Oh ! it's all right, Mr. Pippin; you didn't hurt me, but the manners here are abominable, I think. Why! they take no notice of rank either. Just fancy, the very first day I arrived here I was treated such as I had never been treated before. Me! a Spanish lady of rank, too."</p>
        <p>"Yes! I wish I was back in Canada again." "Oh! is that your native country? What a difference to mine; I was born in Spain, you know." "Is that só!" "Yes! And I had a very happy time there, too." _</p>
        <p>• 'Well, I was grown in a very beautiful garden in Canada, and I lived happily until I was taken away and packed with some other apples into a box. It WAS so very dark and uncomfortable!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Barley POdding.</head>
        <p>Barley POdding.</p>
        <p>Soak half a box of gelatine for two hours in enough cold water.to cover it. Turn over it a cupful of boiling milk, and stir until the gela- tine is dissolvtd. Heat one pint of milk to boiling point, and stir into it three tablespoon- fuls of sugar and the well-beaten yolks of fouz eggs. Do not allow the mixture to boil aftel the yolks are added, but take from the fire and stir frequently until partly cool; then add the dissolved gelatine. Flavour with a tablespoon- ful of vanilla, maraschino or sherry, and finally add the, whites of the eggs beaten light. Line a lightly buttered pudding-mould with fitted fin. slices of rich, black fruit cake. Fill the lined mould with a custard, and set in a cool place for a number of hours or overnight. When serving turn the mould of pudding out upon a dish, and heap round it whipped cream that has been suitably flavoured and sweetened. Sleeping In The Dark.</p>
        <p>Mothers who begin to rock and sing their babies to sleep in a light room discover, when too late, the folly of the practice. A child so treated will insist that watch shall be kept by his bedside until he goes to sleep. The mothers and nurses of spoilt children are sometimes forced to sit for one or two hours by a child of three or four years,. and even older than that, who refused to be left alone. The cry of an infant in pain is unlike that which is baby's only way of exercising his lungs, and of this no notice .may be taken, unless it is much pro- longed. He will soon understand the futility of unheeded tears, and will often lie peacefully awake in his cot, instead of insisting that he shall be taken out of it as soon as he wakes. No infant will sleep if the extremities are cold, and the small feet must be kept warm.</p>
        <p>I was quite delighted to see the daylight once more."</p>
        <p>"Yes; your experiences are something like mine, Mr. Pippin."</p>
        <p>"Well, here. Is a young lady asking-for apples, and the shopkeeper is coming towards me, so I must say 'Good-bye.' Good-bye, Miss Orange." "Good-bye, Mr. Pippin."</p>
        <p>By Nephew Ernest William Sankey (766), 99, Kimberley-street; St. Barnabas' School; aged 10. HOW A SOLDIER WON THE VICTORIA CROSS. As Written By Himself.</p>
        <p>The Germans were giving us a hot time. Nearly every two minutes a shell would burst over our heads and sometimes kill one of us. Two of my pals went to their listening pest, situated near the German trenches, to get some information. Unfortunately the Germans spotted them and got their machine gun to work. They tried to get into a hole made by a shell, but were too slow. One of them got hit in the thigh and the other, seeing what a difficult position he was in, ran back, only he just got half-way when he was hit in the jaw and ankle. All this I saw through my peri- scope. Private Minshall and I went to their assistance, but Minslaall got wounded. I car- ried him back and then went to the other two. I told them to get over a little hole and then get hold of my rifle, which they did. I had dragged them about 200 yards when I was hit in the calf, but still I kept on. I dragged them in the trench, where we were all gladly welcomed. We were sent to hospital, where I was made better, and then went to London. The King welcomed me most heartily and he said it was a pleasure to him to pin the V.C. on so brave a soldier. When I was going back he made me a present of a gold watch, which I gladly accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Florence Nightingale.</head>
        <p>Florence Nightingale.</p>
        <p>Niece Isabella Sankey, 99, Kimberley-street (St. Burnabas' School), sends me an interesting account of thc :"r3 and work of Florence Night- ingale, to whon. I,et rightly says we owe a great deal foi .nt state of our hospitals at the present tim,</p>
        <p>"She was about eighteen when she began to get ready for her progress as a nurse in the Crimea. She went to Scutari, where she found the hospital in a horrih:e state. 'The dying soldiers were lying on filthy straw, just as they had come from the fighting zone. With her money she had beds erected and bought clean clothes for the wounded. Mahy blankets which had been sent from home were kept under lock and key, moulding. Despite the fact she was doing wrong she went and took the blankets. At night she took a lamp into the ward and spoke cheerful words to the dying. Later on she caught a fever and was taken home, where she reformed workhouses and established institutions for the benefit of •the poorer classes of people. She died when she was an old lady, in the year 1910." Delighted With Friend's Success.</p>
        <p>Niece Elsie Johnson (777), 323, Lovely-lane, who attends Wycliffe School, writes me a 'tang and exceedingly good letter on the holidays. the morning of Good Friday, she says, she went for a walk and pictured to herself the day of Crucifixion. In the afternoon slat went to Stock- ton Heath and saw a large ship drawn along the Canal by a tug. Saturday she enjoyed, and when she wrote she was hoping that the remain- * days of the holidays would be as fine and enjoyable.</p>
        <p>she concludes, " to see my 4.1 was delighted," best friend's name in 'The Examiner' on Satur- day last, and I hope she will make a great suc- cess. Niece Lily Scarishrick and I are chums, and have pleasant times at school together."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Easter.</head>
        <p>Easter.</p>
        <p>Nephew Arthur Roundel, (770), 32, Gorsey- lane, Beamont Council School, writing on "Easter," says that we keep it "in memory of the Crucifixion of Jesus Christ. Jesus died to save us from our sins. On the third day He rose again, and ascended into heaven. After the</p>
        <p>lined with vivid colours that match the trim- mings of the dress. In the case of some tailored suits the hems of basques and skirts are faced up with coloured lining that matches the revers or collars.</p>
        <p>Fashionable Neckwear. In the matter of collars it would indeed be difficult to keep pace with the new ideas that !spring up daily. Among the latest is the cape collar, used alike for bodices or coats. It is generally of silk or satin, and stands out from the neck after the manner of the vestment from which it derives its name. Very charming ar3 the new net collars, mounted on bands of black moire ribbon. The cape ruffle is another pleas- ing novelty, designed apparently for those women with long and slender necks, since no others could wear it with comfort, or good effect. The new muslin collars trimmed with Valenciennes lace and further adorned with fine veining and mounted on black velvet are most becoming. The collar that consists of three fussy frills, forming a point at the back, stiffly wired, at the top, or rather the upper frill is wired, but the latter does not meet in front. A silk cord finished with tassels or velvet ribbons is fixed at the side, and tied in bows, making a less cumbersome finish in front than the fussy frill which goes all round the neck. Novelties In Sunshades.</p>
        <p>Parasols are always an important feature of spring and summer, and this season's novelties already on show, prove that designers and makers, the war notwithstanding, are in a position to produce their wares as usual. For many generations the shape of the parasol differed only slightly from that of the umbrella, but of late novelty has been sought in curious and quaint designs, such as the dome, or pagoda shapes. This year there are many variations in these two, some of which are too bizarre for general use. The coverings are in many in- stances curious also, for instance stripes figure largely, but those that appear to radiate from the sticks are novel. Makers have drawn inspiration from Early Victorian models—with the result that the sticks of many new parasols can be doubled and bent in almost any direc- tion. In size parasols are decidedly smaller than last season, while the sticks are much longer. The frilled model is much in evidence, and fringe edges some of the quaintest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Paper Clothes for Warmth.</p>
        <p>Paper, being one of the worst conductors of heat, is a boon to the flying men. Already, the French aviators have adopted the innovation extensively. They find that the paper outfit prevents cold from entering, and the heat from escaping. We Must Sweat To Sleep.</p>
        <p>The man who will not sweat cannot expect to sleep. The average man is hunting about to find some way in which he can get a living without sweating. A man who gets his living by the work of his hands is undeservedly looked upon as a menial. The higher classes are supposed to be people who do not have to work. Now, one of the greatest privileges of mankind is the opportunity of working. Labour —healthy, honest labour—is the greatest pos- sible blessing to the human race. And so we find that it is the labouring man in the country whose circumstances are happiest. Away from the congested cities, where the polluted soil and smoky air menace life, country folk can actually lie down and embrace mother earth without danger. The people of the country are healthy. The healthy man, the labouring man who lives outdoors and works with his hands—he is the man who can sleep. And why? Because the process of work introduces into his body a large amount of air. It is exactly like the bellows to the blacksmith's forge, which blows the fires,' increases the draught— hard breathing pumps oxygen into the body and so bunts up the waste material and keeps the body functions in good condition.—Dr. Kellogg.</p>
        <p>winter has passed, people look forward to the Easter holidays. Nearly all the works and schools have a holiday at Easter."</p>
        <p>If It Were Only• True!</p>
        <p>Niece Dorothy Wright (730), 68, Winwick- street, tells me where she would like to spend her summer holidays. "I should like very much," says Dorothy; "to go'to Blackpool with several of my friends, and have a jolly good time playing on the'sands. A month is a long time to wait, but when you go to a place that you like very much it soon passes, and you are very sorry when your month is up. There are plenty of amusements at Blackpool, and you can amuse yourself on every- thing. Would not that be nice if it were only true? To think you can spend a month enjoy- ing yourself, with no work to think of and no- body troubling you."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Prettiest Flower.</head>
        <p>The Prettiest Flower.</p>
        <p>Niece Mabel Wright (731), 68, Winwick-street, tells me which flower she thinks looks prettiest in the summer. -</p>
        <p>"I prefer the rose," she says, "because it has so many different shades, and it looks so pretty as you gaze at it. It has a most beautiful per- fume, and droops its head. The rose has a great many- petals, but they soon drop."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Evils of War.</head>
        <p>The Evils of War.</p>
        <p>Niece Edith Pugh, 44, Glaxebrook-street, who is away for the holidays, writes all the way from Brecon to say that she is pleased to know she can choose her own subject. "I have wanted to write on 'The Evils of War," she explains. "When people know war is declared they are often very depressed. Valiant men die for their king and country. Then their loved ones mourn over them; chil- dren are very sorry at losing their parents. Ships are stopped coming from friendly coun- tries, and food generally gets scarce and dear. Many poor people nearly starve when the food gets dear. When. war is going on everything is at a standstill, and prosperity cannot go on as usual. Many beautiful and magnificent build- ings and monuments are shattered to ruins by the enemies, also paintings and drawings which cannot be replaced." , A Practical Boy.</p>
        <p>Nephew J. Webb, 27, Catherine-street, is evi- dently of a practical turn of mind, for he writes a model letter to an imaginary works manager, asking for employment, in answer to an adver- tisement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>UNCLE NED AND TITLES.</head>
        <p>UNCLE NED AND TITLES.</p>
        <p>Uncle Ned writes to say :</p>
        <p>I have read with very great interest the many ideas of the children as to the ritics which I KNOW they are going to earn very soon; ..nd now, with your perukission, I .! id li_! to suggest one or two myself. I ol aese out .as suggestions :--</p>
        <p>The titles I have thought of are: President, Leaders Winner-Pioneer, Winner, Pioneer, Niece and Nephew'. Of these, the ranks of Niece and Nephew are equal, and so are those of Winner and Pioneer.</p>
        <p>How to become—Wiliece or Nephew.—Join the Corner</p>
        <p>How to become a Pioneer.—Obtain fifteen new members, all at once or gradually. How to become a Winner.—Win two prizes. How to become a Winner-Pioneer.—Get fifteen new members and win two prizes. How to become a.Leader.—Get forty-five new , members or win three prizes. As soon as you have got fifteen new members you will be pro- moted to the rank of Pioneer, and the fifteen ' will count towards the forty-five as well !k The same rule applies to the prizes. How to become a President.—Earn the title of Leader twice. (This is easier than it looks, if you think it over.) In addition to these fixed rules, Uncle Jack is alloWed to promote any member who has entered the competitions regularly, but has never won a prize. He may promote a member for any special service to the Corner. For instance, if i School or School d Art examination, or won a prize in the a member passed the Secondary Beautiful Warrington Society, that would be a</p>
        <p>„ •</p>
