        <p>..:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:••:-:-:•.:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:••:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:••:-:.•:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:. •:•.:....:.++4.-:-:-:-:-:-:••:.:-:-:••:.-:-:-:••:-:-:••:-:••:••:-:••:-:-:-:••:-:••:-:-:-:•.:-:-:-.:-:-.:-:-:••:-.).:-:-:-:-:•.:.-:-:-:-:-:-:-:-:••:. " • " •:.: •:-:. •:•::: •:-:. •:...: •:-:. + S •:••:. •:, f 4-) •:•••• e. •:-:. •:.: 4-:. + v •:.</p>
        <p>positions of danger and difficulty, and the deal- ing with juvenile delinquents. That the aim of the promoters of the , movement ,bids fair to be, realised, may be gathered by the statement lately made by Sir Edward Henry, besides which Scotland Yard is, to a man, in sympathy with a system of co-operation with women.</p>
        <p>Communal Housekeeping. Thousands of homes will be broken up when all the men have .gone on active service, for with the elimination, or reduction in the hus- band's incomes, ,and the increased price of food, the wives cannot possibly maintain their families and keep a house going. - This has appeared to a number of patriotic and practical ladies to afford an opportunity for adopting ts scheme that has met with great success in America. An organisation has been formed to open hostels for soldiers' wives and families, on a basis of co-operative housekeeping. Common dining, sitting, reading, and writing rooms will be provided, to which a creche and a small hos- pital ward will be added, and there will ample bedroom accommodation. The whole will be under the care of a lady trained in house- wifery and the domestic arts. It is hoped that the scheme will be self-supporting, as in no sense are the hostels charitable • institutions. They are rather object lessons in communal housekeeping, and then success may lead to widespread change in that art, in addition to helping housewives to solve the servant prob- lem.</p>
        <p>This Season's Trimmings. The silhouette still exercises the minds of fashionmongers, but it must be admitted that when properly adjusted the outline this season is so far attractive and pleasing. Grouped flounces are often placed at the side of a skirt to give the bouffant effect, and sleeves play a very important part in the adjustment of the silhouette of the moment. The puffed variety has an astonishing success. The chief features of last year, namely the short full skirt, the lighter bodice, and the definite waist, remain unchanged. But dressmakers insist on variety, consequently trimmings are to be the outstand- ing feature of this season's modes. Linings too, are more important, and are used as colour con- trasts in the case of neutral shades, or dark tints. Basques and panniers are frequently</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARS AT P.S.A.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARS AT P.S.A.</p>
        <p>"Good Templars' day" was held at the Wycliffe P.S.A. on Sunday. There was a fair attendance, and the Rev. T. T. James presided. Mr. John T. Quinton, D.0.T., of Liverpool dis- trict, gave an address upon the work of the Good Templars. Fifty-one years ago, he said, the Knight Templars were formed in America. After a time a 'Birmingham gentleman paid them a visit, and in 1868 formed the first Good Templars' Lodge in England. It soon grew, and had now become a very strong organisation for trying to save the fallen, and prevent others from falling. If the Government had been firm prohibition would have been brought about, he declared.</p>
        <p>MlBB Olive Dale sang "I know that my Redeemer liveth " in a most pleasing manner. Mr. T. Tanner presided at the organ. Mr. Yearsley took part in the meeting. A message was gives from Privates Yeates and Payne, who are in hospital.</p>
        <p>An Egg Question. Mr. Smith, who kept the village store, prided himself on his widespread knowledge, and when- ever an argument was to be settled he was called in.</p>
        <p>One day Mrs. Jones' cook rushed into the store with her basket under her arm and asked. breathlessly:</p>
        <p>_ "How Cong have dodos been extinct, Mr. Smith?"</p>
        <p>"About two hundred years, I should reckon," he replied, meditatively. "I thought it was about that. The missus called it three hundred. Anyway, she asked me to tell you it was hens' eggs she ordered, not dodos'," she added, as she laid the eggs on the : counter.</p>
