      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, APRIL 29, 1916,</p>
        <p>OUR RECRUITING PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday the Prime Minister laid before the House of Commons, in "secret session," the Government's proposals for dealing with the recruiting problem, and the results of the, inquiries conducted by the Government "in order to examine all considerations relevant to" that problem. These -considerations include the demands made on our supply of labour for the needs Of the Navy, Mercantile Marine, Munitions, and other essential national services; and the bearing of finance, and in particular of the financial aid rendered to our Allies, upon the question of the number of men that can be taken from the resources of labour after provision has been made for the services already specified. To put the second consideration in a somewhat different form, the industries engaged' upon producing ,com- modities for export must be kept at a certain level, or our credit will not be sufficient to maintain the necessari financial assistance which we are rendering to our Allies, and also the purchasing power of the money employed to buy munitions and other supplies in the Unitea States for our Allies will be seriously diminished. From the results of this examina- tion has been deduced the number of men that could be safely spared from industry during the present year.</p>
        <p>There are obviously grave reasons why the data which the Prime Minister supplied to the House of Commons, awl which were also placed before the House of Lords in a parallel "secret session," should not be made public, and thus give to the enemy information which would be of great value for the adjustment of his future plans and dispositions. But, as Lord Crewe pointed out, it was desirable that Parliament should have these data before it in order to form a well-considered opinion upon the Government's proposals. While there is complete unanimity in the country upon the necessity of carrying the war to a final and triumphant conclusivn, there is a cleavage of opinion in regard to the number of men; and the kind of men, who are available fdr service in the Navy and the Army. The proposals which the Government have put for- ward have been arrived at after keen discus- sion in the Cabinet, and on Wednesday of last week, it will be remembered, Mr. Asquith had to announce that there were still points of disagreement which, if not settled, would lead to a break-up of the Government. This dis- aster—as the great majority of thinking people in this country regarded it, and as the delight of the German Press at the prospect shows that it would • have been—was happily averted. • •</p>
        <p>The Prime -dinister stated in the House of Commons, according to the official report, that recruiting up to date has not furnished what is necessary to "fulfil our proper military effort," and this failure is due, not to an over- estimate of the men available, but to the length of time which must- be occupied in sift- ing individual cases. The Government adopted four " relatively minor proposals " to, meet the situation :</p>
        <p>(a) Prolongation of service of time-expired men till the end of the war; (b) the Military Authority to be em- powered to transfer men enlisted for Territorial battalions to any unit; (c) exempted men to be liable for military service immediately on expiry of cer- tificate; (4) all youths under eighteen on August 15th last to be brought under the Military Service Act as they reach that age.</p>
        <p>But what may be called the "major</p>
        <p>posals of the Government went much further.l They were : (1) If by May 27th, after special efforts to obtain the required men by voluntary • enlistment from unattested married men, 50,000 have not been obtained, Parliament will be asked forthwith for compulsory powers; (2) The same course will be taken if in any week after May 27th, 15,000 men have not been obtained by direct en- listment, any surplus above 15,000 • being. carried over to the next week; (3) These arrangements will be continued until 200,000 unattested men have been obtained.</p>
        <p>When it was sought to introduce the first portion ;of these proposals in .the form of a Bill on Thursday 'night; it met with such general- opposition in the House that the measure was withdrawn. This hostility, com- ing after the secret session, at which presum- ably the needs of the country were made clear, is an indication that the • period for temporising has gone by. The country is with the Commons, and the Government may rely upon the country if they will make a final clean-cut demand for all the help they want. What the country does not want is further dallying with, or partial treatment of, a matter that calls for vigorous action and final settlement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>PRIVATE JOHNSON AND HIS BRIDE.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHNSON AND HIS BRIDE.</p>
        <p>:costumes with picture hats, and carried bouquets of tulips and narcissi; the gifts of the bride- groom. The bride was given away by her father, and Mr. A. Charnock, uncle of the bridegroom, acted as best man. After the ceremony a recep- 'tion was held at the home of the bride. Mr. and Mrs. Johnson were the recipients of many presents, including a gift from the office staff of Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co., where the bridegroom was employed previous to joining the Army.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>FAIR GROUND.</head>
        <p>THE FAIR GROUND.</p>
        <p>To Be Closed Except For Half- Yearly Fa!irs.</p>
        <p>The Watch Committee have resolved: "That in consequence of frequent complaints of the noise and the unruly behaviour of persons fre- quenting the Fair Ground, the Markets Com- mittee be requested to discontinue the use of the Fair Grouhrt as far as possible, for shows, swings, roundabouts, or other similar amuse- ments."</p>
        <p>The Markets Committee considered this re- quest and resolved: "That all the present holders of stands receive notice to quit, and that the ground be not again let for shows, swings, roundabouts, or other similar amuse- ments, except at the usual half-yearly fairs, when all applications for stands must be made in writing and submitted to this committee."</p>
        <p>It was -a-,lso resolved: "That the tolls for the use of the Fair Ground be continued at one- half until the next meeting of the committee."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>FELL SEVENTY FEET.</head>
        <p>FELL SEVENTY FEET.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Boy's Death At Appleton Quarry.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Borough Coroner last night held an inquest On the body of George Milling- ton (6), son of George and Elizabeth Millington, of 5, Kimberley-drive, Stockton Heath, who died at the Infirmary on Thursday from injuries re- ceived through accidentally falling down the quarry at Appleton. Deceased's father is at present serving as a driver in the Royal Field Artillery. The evidence showed that along with his brother James and another boy, the boy left home early on Thursday morning with the object of visiting the Golf Links at Appleton. They remained there till 5.15 in the evening, when they wended their way homeward. They had to pass a wall protecting the quarry, which is used as a rifle range. The boy climbed on to the wall and slipped, falling into the quarry below, a distance of 70 feet. His brother James gave the alarm, and when picked up he had a fracture of both of his arms and internal in- juries. He was removed to the Infirmary, where he died a few hours after admittance. James Millington, who is 9 years of age, in- formed the Coroner and jury that when his brother climbed cin the wall witness called out to him to come back, and he was in the act of doing so when he appeared to slip and fell into the quarry. Mr. H. J. Westbrook, who represented the owner of the estate, Colonel Lyon, said at holi- day times children and women were a nuisance at the quarry. They got over the wall for ferns, &amp;c. Mr. IT. Beresford Melville, agent to the estate, was also present at the inquiry. The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death," and Mr. Westbrook expressed sympathy with the parents of the deceased.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>DEAR BEER.</head>
        <p>DEAR BEER.</p>
        <p>Licensed Victuallers Increase</p>
        <p>Their Prices.</p>
        <p>Commencing on Monday, the prices of beer ee will be increased. This decision was arrived ajt. at a meeting of the Warrington Licensed Victuallers' Association, at a .m g held t the Roebuck Hotel, on Thursday, Alderman similarEan s adopted tiendc rei na setdh e pricesetin will be surrounding districts. The fixed prices are :_Bitter beer ad. per half pint, sd. a pint, .11d, a quart; best beer. 21d. half pint, 44d. pint, and 9d. quart; ordin- ary beer. 2d. half pint, ad. pint, and 7d. quart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>BRITISH REPLY TO AMERICAN NOTE.</head>
        <p>BRITISH REPLY TO AMERICAN NOTE.</p>
        <p>In the reply of the British Government to the American Note of November sth last, re- garding this country's conduct of the block- ade, the French Government has associated itself. The chief points of the Note are: The evidence of Admiral Jellicoe that it is impos- sible to make an efficient search of vessels for contraband at sea, and that the attempt to do so would expose the neutral vessel to danger from German submarines; the British Govern- ment and the Allied Governments declare their adherence to the principle that the ultimate destination of a cargo, and not merely the port it is bound for, must be con- sidered in determining its liability to seizure, and for this the law of the United States itself affords indisputable precedent; the principle adopted by the Federals in the American Civil War that, "hostile destination being a matter of fact," all relevant considerations should be taken into consideration in determining it, is declared once again by the -Allied Govern- ments; it is shown by comparative statistics of the first nine months of 1913 and 1915 re- spectively, that the trade of the United States with the Scandinavian countries and Holland has not suffered injury; by the test of results the blockade is shown to comply with the rule that a blockade, to be legal, must be " effective " ; the British Government desire to assure the United States Govern- ment "that they will continue their efforts to make the exercise of what they consider their belligerent rights as little burdensome to neutrals as possible."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>EFFECTIVE BLOCKADE AND NEUTRAL HICHTS.</head>
        <p>EFFECTIVE BLOCKADE AND NEUTRAL HICHTS.</p>
        <p>Those critics of the Foreign Office who have accused it of lengthening the war by interfer- ing with the Fleet Er blockading •activities,., show some disposition to seize upon the soli- citude which the British Note expresses for neutrals' inconvenience, and on the conten- tion that American trade has suffered no injury, as confessions of weakness. The proofs given that the blockade is " effective " afe judiciously slurred over. We entered into this war in defence of the rights of neutrals, and we have conducted it in a manner which justifies us in expecting the moral support of the 'United Sfites, the Government of which' has announced its intention to undertake the task of championing the integrity of neutral rights. Our Government has no need to stand in a white sheet before the self-consti- tuted tribunal of our domestic " ginger " groups because it has striven, not without success, to combine regard for neutral inter- ests with an effective blockade of Germany.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>MAJOR CROSFIELD.</head>
        <p>MAJOR CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Decidedly Good News Of The</p>
        <p>Gallant Officer.</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur Crosfield, writing to us yesterday, says _ _</p>
        <p>"The news we have from Miss Truda Cros- field since she went to join my brother in France is decidedly good news. Of course, my brother's condition varies, as condition always does in such cases, but since April 6th there has been no serious set-back.</p>
        <p>"On the contrary, he ,has made as good pro- gress since then as could be expected in such a case of septic poisoning, and there seems, therefore, to be the best possible reason for hoping that my brother is now on the high road to complete recovery, though that recovery will necessarily still take some considerable time."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>CALL TO MARRIED.</head>
        <p>CALL TO MARRIED.</p>
        <p>Notice Of Appeal Not Later Than May 9th.</p>
        <p>Yesterday the green poster in black ink, call- ing the attested married men between the ages Of 27 to 35, made its appearance on the hoard- ings of Warrineon. The proclamation was dis- Wayed on several local stations. The groups summoned to report themselves as from May 29 comprise the following:— " Group. Age. 33</p>
        <p>28 35 36 37 38 39 40</p>
        <p>41 The War Office has notified recruiting officers that all enlistments under the group system are to cease forthwith. Married men of military age, not already accepted, can now only be accepted for immediate military service. We are informed by Mr. A. T. Hallaway, clerk to the local Tribunal, that all attested married men who are included in the call and wish to appeal must do so on or before May 9, which is the last day for receiving appeals at the Town Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Crosfield's Boy Scouts.</head>
        <p>Crosfield's Boy Scouts.</p>
        <p>About 28 members of the Crosfield's Own (Second Warrington) Troop Boy Scouts, accom- panied by the band, spent the Easter holiday in camp at Glazebrook Hall Farm, by kind per- mission of Mrs. Gratrix. They left Warrington on Saturday afternoon and returned at five o'clock on Monday. On Sunday morning a church parade was held, and a special service conducted at Glazebrook Church. In the after- noon a route mareh to Irlam took place, while 'Monday was spent in Company Drill. Scout- masters R. Garner and J. McCauleii we charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 29, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 29, 1916.</p>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>Last week "The Examiner ". published a special appeal from the Bishop of Chester,stat- ing the reasons why this year the Easter offer- ings to the clergy should suffer no falling off owing to the many war claims.</p>
        <p>The war has made its abnormal claims upon the ministers of religion of all kinds. Labour in this -.Particular vineyard is scarce.as else- where, priCes are up and reven4ii is down. It is—pliiitsing,-therefore, to record that-the reports of the Easter vestiges show that ;these special Easter, offerings made in recent' years to the heads of parishes have been above ratlier than below the average.</p>
        <p>Alderman Arthur Bennett. and Councillor R. Renshall were at Stratford-on-Avon during the Easter Holidays. They took part in the Sunday Tercentenary precession •-and placed flowers on Shakespeare's grave. Warrington could scarcely have been better represented.</p>
        <p>As a C.L.C. train was crossing Winwick- street for Liverpool on Easter Saturday a Manchester artisan remarked to - his fellow- seaside-resorter, "This is the • place where the streets are paved with gold."</p>
        <p>He is wrong. Our streets are paved with cobble-stones as in London in the days of Dick Whittington. But, maybe, he had heard of our war bonus.</p>
        <p>The charges of the Warrington Fire Brigade for attending the Highfield Tannery fire (Run- corn) is £52 17s. 4d., and for• attending the Mersey Fish Meal and Glue Co.'s fire, £3O 2s. ad.</p>
        <p>• Mr. C. Jackson Holmes has been re-elected a representative member of the Education Com- mittee, on the recommendation of the Associa- tion of Unsectarian Elementary Schools.</p>
        <p>A considerable number of Padgate boys belonging to the Church Lads' Brigade wen - engaged on military manoeuvres not far from home recently, and one of the phases was the carrying of dispatches.</p>
        <p>This, of course, is most important and risky work—sometimes it requires a Member of Par- liament to do it! How to "get through" was the problem, and the other side heard a whis- per that the dispatch bearer was to be disguised as an old man.</p>
        <p>Hiding in a ditch, one of the boys eventually saw an old man trudging along with *a bundle under his arm. Foot overtook foot wearisomely and the strain of waiting and watching was terrific.</p>
        <p>When the approaching figure got within a few yards of him the lad .sprang forward :Bid bowled him over, snatching the bun Ale from his hands. With alarm he -discovered, by tug- ging at the old man's beard, wb.en too late, that. the bundle bearer was really an old man, Whose protests at molestation were warranted.</p>
        <p>Later the disguised one came along and the boys manoeuvred for certainty before they pulled him up, They had profited by their Sergeant's experience. The disguise, which was a trifle overdone, was discovered, so the dis- patch failed to reach the officer coinmanding.</p>
        <p>Germany has decided to adopt a daylight saving scheme, which comes into operation May Ist. France is investigating a proposal to save daylight during the period of the war. There are rumours of something of the kind being attempted in England.</p>
        <p>But we can all be our own daylight savers and gas bill reducers if we will: if the gas meter man—or woman P—does not read the meter to the same effect whether we use the gas or not.</p>
        <p>Daylight saving is a British idea—and it has been put into operation by Germany! I think this ,is some small proof of the statement .made in a recent "Examiner" leader that the Eng- •lish ;people .have the brains, while the Germans have sense to apply what we have found out for them.</p>
        <p>Perhaps Mr. Holman Kingdun and his light saving friends may take occasion by the hand and—perhaps—they will succeed now where they failed before.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Stewart Royston informs me that his walking stick fund for wounded soldiers is making fine progress.</p>
        <p>Sir Chas. Russell, chairman of the Collection Committee of the British Red Cross Society, who has read Mr. Royston's story in "The Examiner," "A V.C. worth remembering," writes to Mr. Roystpn to say he f els confident the article has already had a good effect. "I am sure," he says, "you are doing a tremen- dous amount of work for the sick and -wounded, and I can assure you that all this is very much appreciated."</p>
        <p>VIGILANT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>HORSE FEEDER'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>HORSE FEEDER'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>A Post-Mortem Examination Reveals Fractured Skull.</p>
        <p>The peculiiir circumstances attending the death of William Sheridan (15), horse feeder, a single man, living at 7, Academy-street, Warrington, were inquired into by the Borough Coroner (Mr. S. Brighouse) last night. Sheridan was employed by Mr. H. F. Green- ough, cab proprietor, Heathside. Mr. E. A. White represented Mr. Greenough at the inquiry. 41. Miss Greenough stated that on Monday after- noon she was in the office, at the Heathside Livery Stables, when she noticed a horse leave the yard. She called out and it stopped. Soon after she found Sheridan lying on the ground and was bleeding at the nose. He appeared to be unconscious and after being placed on a chair and his face sponged he recovered. He was removed to the Infirmary, where he was medically treated. She understood that the man received strict instructions to go home and rest. -He however got out of the cab at the corner of the street in which he lived.</p>
        <p>Later she was surprised to find him back at the yard, nad she spoke to him about the accident, but he said he could remember noth- ing about it. He added that he was all right. Witness was of the opinion that the noise from roundabouts close to the yard caused the horse to become restless and leave the yard. She, however, could not say whe- ther the man was knocked down by the horse. Attempted to Jump Through Bedroom Window. John McCarthy, a fellow-lodger, informed the Jury that during the early hours of Tues- day morning deceased walked the bedroom floor and seemed to be rambling. He at- tempted to jump through the bedroom window. A nurse at the Infirmary gave evidence to the man being attended at the institution on Monday. He had bruises at the back of the neck and hands. He was told to go home and rest. The following day he was brought back to the Infirmary and he was found to be suf- fering from a punctured wound at the back of the .neck- „lie sank gradually and died, on Thursday. She was told that Sheridan had had another fall during the night.</p>
        <p>Dr. Binns gave evidence to conducting a post-mortem examination and said he found a small fracture of the skull. Death was due to hemorrhage of the brain set up by the fracture.</p>
        <p>witness• McCarthy, recalled, said during the time he was with deceased in the bedroom he had not a fall. The Jury returned a verdict in accordance with the medical evidence, but added whether death was brought about by the fall in the yard there was not sufficient evidence to show.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Brick Street Mission.</head>
        <p>Brick Street Mission.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above Church, divine service was held Good Friday morning. Mr. John Sherlock preaching on the Crucifixion In the evening a tea party and sacred coneeri: was held. About 130 sat down for tea. The concert was greatly enjoyed, a very good programme being given. The soloists were Miss Elsie Hewitt, Miss Hockenhall and Mr. F. Moores. The chcir gave four anthems in excellent style. The minister from Elmwood-avenue Congregational chureh, the Rev. C. P. Pitt, presided, and also gave a very practical address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>DR. JAGGER MARRIED TO HOSPITAL MATRON.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, at Brunswick Wesleyan Chapel, Earlestown, the Rev. T. L. Climber officiating, the marriage was solemnised of Lieut. J. R. Jagger, M.D., R.A.M.C. (T.), now, with the Forces, eldest son of Mr. and Mrs. J. Jagger, Moss Side, Manchester, and medical officer of the Warrington Union and Whitecross Military Hospital, and Miss L. Emertob, eldest daugh- ter of Mr. H. Emerton and the late Mrs. Emer- ton, Market-street, Earlestown, and superin- tendent nurse of tboWarrington Union Infirm- ary and matron, of the WhiteoroSts Military Hospital. The bride, who was given away by Mr. R. I. Partridge, assistant clerk to the Warrington Board of Guardians, wore a travelling dress of dark mole colour with hat to match. Her sister, Miss A. Emerton, was bridesmaid. Mr. E. R. Jagger (brother of the bridegroom) was best man.</p>
        <p>Lieut. and Mrs. Jagger left later in the day for London. The former will shortly leave for duty in Egypt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>JOHNSON-TURNER.</head>
        <p>JOHNSON-TURNER.</p>
        <p>Private James W. Johnson, South Lancs. Regiment, son of Mr. J. W. Johnson, 16, Edgeworth-street Warrington, was on Saturday married to Miss Emily Turner, eldest daughter of Mr. and Mrs. W. G. Turner, 36, Thewlis- street, at the Holy Trinity Church, Warrington. The officiating clergyman was the Rev. E. L. J. Roberts. The' bride was attired in a fawn costume with hat to match, and she carried a sheaf of lilies. Her bridesmaids were Miss E. Turner (sister) and Miss Hallam (cousin of the bride), Longton, Staffs, Nsho were dressed in blue</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>WAR-TIME SALES OF ORI.</head>
        <p>WAR-TIME SALES OF ORI.</p>
        <p>The Deputy-Mayor On The War's Practical Lessons.</p>
        <p>ST. ANN'S.</p>
        <p>Promoters of sales of work have always had a keen eye for spectacular effect, and it is only natural that in war time the ,emblems of patriotism should be seen to advantage at such functions. It was, therefore, not surprising to find that the sale of work in tonnection with St. Ann's Church, which was opened by the Deputy Mayor (Dr. G. W. Joseph), on Wednesday after- noon, embodied in the tasteful decoration of the stalls the flags of the allied nations. A very pleasing effect was obtained, and the ladies of the church are to be congratulated on the artistic transformation effected in. the school- room, where the sale was held. The object of the sale was• to raise money for the curate, lay /reader and other 'parochial funds.</p>
        <p>The Vicar (the Rev. James Davies) after thanking Dr. Joseph for his kindness in attend- ing, mentioned that the balk money for the sale of work amounted to £6O, which was £1.3 more than last year. Time For Action Rather Than Words.</p>
        <p>In declaring the sale open, the Deputy Mayor said they werl. engaged in a good cause—the carrying on a the spiritual needs of the country in spite of the war. This work was just as urgent as ever, and the needs more so, because they had to help and encourage those who were fighting and console those who were wounded, or perhaps worse. It was impossible at a time like this to avoid talking about the war. It was a time for deeds rather than words, and the war had taught them one lesson, the comparative uselessness of words in relation to action. They had been told that example was more powerful than precept, and deeds more powerful than words, and he believed that this war had taught them a practical lesson, which would be handed on to future generations. 1-Te hoped the sale would be a success, and that the receipts would exceed their anticipations. On the proposition of Mr. T. S. Jolley, seco,,ded by Mr. W. Thomas. a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Deputy Mayor.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR AND THE PRESENT STRENUOUS TIMES.</p>
        <p>The second and concluding day's proceedings were opened by the Mayor (Alderman Peacock), who was accompanied by his niece; Mrs. E. Mee:, . . _</p>
        <p>The Vicar, in weleoFaing him, pointed out that of the many Mayors who had occupied the honoured position, Alderman Peacock had proved himself one of the best; and he never, spared himself to fulfil he duties of his office. He (the Vicar) had pleasure in announcing that he had received a cheque from the Mayor of £sss.</p>
        <p>_ The Mayor, in opening the sale, expressed the pleasure it afforded him to attend to help the efforts of the Par.ish of St. Ann's. In these strenuous days people had many calls upon them for various things in connection with the war, but they ought to recognise that if there was a time in the history of the world when the Church and other organisations needed assistance, it was the present. He hoped the sale would meet with the success it deserved, in order to give that encouragement to the church workers, who now needed it more than ever before. (Ap- plause). On the proposition of Mr. T. S. Jolley, seconded by Mr. W. Thomas, a vote of thanks was passed to the Mayor, who, in reply, said that any effort in that district which was for the good of the community he was only too pleased to support. The proceeds of the • sale on Wednesday amounted to £57 14s. 4d., in addition to which there was £32 subscriptions. Each evening Mr. W. H. Rigby's concert party was a source of enjoyment to the visitors. The proceeds of the sale amounted to a little over 4130.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Stall holders.</head>
        <p>Stall holders.</p>
        <p>The stallholders were:— Vicarage Stall.—Miss Davies, Mrs. Corbett, Mrs. Jolley. Church Stall.—Miss Bunch, Mrs. Harrison, Mrs. Thomas.</p>
        <p>Sunday School Stall.—Misses Evans and Mrs. Parker. Crockery Stall.—Mrs. Gee. Sweets.—Misses D. and I. Smith. Flowers and Fruit.—Miss Bradshaw and Miss Jolley. Hardware, &amp;c.—Mr. W. Thomas.</p>
        <p>Advertisement Stall.—The Vicar. Confectionery, Pounds and Cafe.—Mrs. Savage, Mrs. Brown, Mrs. Downie, Miss D. Fryer. Bran Tub.—Misses W. and E. Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>ST. PAUL'S.</head>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S.</p>
        <p>Opened By Canon And Mrs.</p>
        <p>Smith.</p>
        <p>In order to help the parochial organisations in connection with the St. Paul's Church, a sale of work was held in the Kendrick-street School on Thursday afternoon. The opening ceremony was performed by Canon and Mrs. Gibson Smith.</p>
        <p>In the course of a few opening remarks, Mr. J. H. Smethurst (Vicar's warden) said he wished to apologise for the absence of the Vicar, who had hoped to get leave, but owing to the troubles in Ireland, the regiment to which the Vicar was attached had been ordered to "stand by," in readiness. The Vicar was doing his duty, and the people at home were putting up with all inconveniences that he might do so. Referring to , Canon and Mrs. Smith, Mr. Smethurst said Mrs. Smith's family had been connected with the church for a long period, and they had done much for the parish. Even though Canon and Mrs. Smith had left the neighbourhood, they still showed much interest in St. Paul's, and this was evidenced by the ready way they had consented to attend that day. He remembered Canon Smith coming to the Boteler Grammar School many years ago. He hoped he and his wife would long continue to enjoy the best of health, and be interested. in their parish for many years to come. (Ap- plause.) _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>St. Paul's Model Vicar.</head>
        <p>St. Paul's Model Vicar.</p>
        <p>In reply, Canon Smith thanked all for their kind welcome, and went on to speak of much good work that had been done in the parish by old members in bygone years. He was glad to hear that that work was still being kept up, and he. willingly gave his loyal support. Their Vicar was not only a model Vicar, but was a true Englishman, and knew where his duty lay in the country's hour of need. (Applause.) Mrs. Smith then declared the sale open. On the proposition of the Rev. E. Downlora, a hearty vote of thanks was accorfled Canon and Mrs. Smith.</p>
        <p>The proceeds of the sate amounted to £l5B 9s. 74d., which included a donation cf £lO from Lady Greenall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>ELECTRICITY WORKS. Nearly £lO,OOO For Extra Boiler</head>
        <p>ELECTRICITY WORKS. Nearly £lO,OOO For Extra Boiler</p>
        <p>Accommodation.</p>
        <p>A letter was read at a recent meeting of the Electricity Committee from the Local Prepared to ern- ment Board with regard to the atphpalitcaptuioripiofgeor, and sanction to the borrowing of 41,536 for extra boiler accommodation at the Electricity Works, and stating that, having consulted the Ministry sanction ttihoant Munitions,itnheo r ldoteahrne t o rßoardesgavueir time forwmie the work may bel l be put in hand. It was resolved: "That the Engineer be authorised to advertise for plant, &amp;e._, _. and that M he .be also authorised to. engage r. Percy Siloeck to prepare the necessary plams.J.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Ambulance Whist Drive.</head>
        <p>Ambulance Whist Drive.</p>
        <p>aßreall on May ision of ron eivolding a the whist te t d. mr.lieovmhe nbaenAr ds ndobafuniteaheneci enWßt ahrrei rgi Pandas: ert sth, under' the patronage of the May9r (Alder. man Peter Peacock), and by kind permission of Colonel Moggridge, the 2nd South Lancs. Band will play for dancing.</p>
        <p>A.B.C. Railway Guide for May. . _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Warrington X.B.C. Railway Guide for May, containing many alterations, will be pub- lished on Monday morning.</p>
        <p>Fruit And Vegetables Wholesale. The Markets Committee have approved regu- lations for the holding of a Wholesale Fruit and Vegetable Market to be put into force on and after the 15th May next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Births and Deaths.</head>
        <p>Births and Deaths.</p>
        <p>In the week ended April 22nd there were 35 births in Warrington against 36 in the corre- sponding week last year. The deaths numbered 21 against 26 in tne corresponding week.</p>
        <p>Six of the deaths were- from bronchitis or pneumonia, and three from heart disease.</p>
        <p>Infectious Disease Still Decreasing. There was a further decrease in cases of in- fectious disease in -Warrington during the week ended April 22nd. There were 46 local cases of scarlet fever in hospital, and only three new eases are reported. Six cases of diphtheria are in hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Conditions and Allowances for</head>
        <p>Conditions and Allowances for</p>
        <p>Corporation Employes. The Mayor, Alderman Tinnion, and Coun- cillors Chrimes, Henshall, Lindley and Roberts have been appointed a sub-committee to con- sider and report on the conditions and allow- ances affecting employes of the Corporation who have or may attest in H.M. Forces.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Paving. from Clinker.</head>
        <p>Paving. from Clinker.</p>
        <p>The Paving arid Sewerage Committee have appointed the Chairman, Deputy-Chairman, Alderman .Tames Evans and the Borough Sur- veyor a sub-committee to consider and report upon the paving of roads with material made from clinker, and authorised them to inspect work in progress at Hornsey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Ship Canal Dredgings.</head>
        <p>Ship Canal Dredgings.</p>
        <p>The Boroua Surveyor repoTted-to a recent meeting of the Rivers Committee upon his interview with the Manchester Ship Canal Co. with regard to the deposit of dredgings upon the land adjacent to the old river bed. He also reported that the company had promised to communicate with the Corporation before commencing to fill in the old river bed.</p>
        <p>Sudden Death in a Warrington Surgery John Wardle (61), a widower, who lived at 2, Alfred-street, Warrington, was taken suddenly ill while out for a walk in Liverpool-road on Saturday. He called at Dr. Gandhi's surgery for treatment, but died while sitting in a chair. Deceased contracted a chill about six weeks ago, which developed into bronchitis. An in- quest was not considered necessary, death being attributed to heart failure.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. T. Reed Renews Acquaintances. The Rev. E. T. Reed, of London, renewed his acquaintance with the congregations of the Emmanuel (Reformed) Church, Bold-street, on Sunday, when he. preached at both the morning and evening services. Some fourteen years ago, the Rev. E. T. Reed was the incumbent of the church, and is now assisting the Young Men's Christian Association in its noble work among the troops. Mr. ,Reed, during his stay in War- rington, was very popular, and was the first to introduce into the borough the Young People's Society of Christian Endeavour. At each ser- vice the reverend gentleman spoke of the pleasure it afforded him to meet old acquaint- ances.</p>
        <p>Cadet Corps Disappointed. The Latchford Cadet Corps had looked for- ward with evident pleasure to Eastertide, when it was hoped they would be able to go into camp at Frodsham. It was, therefore, a keen dis- appointment to the Cadets that the climatic con- ditions were against camping out, and the pro- posed camp at Frodsham had to be abandoned. Under the circumstances, however, they made the best of the holidays, and on Good Friday morning, headed by the newly-formed bugle band, marched to Lymra and district. Lieut. H. Kitchen and S'ergt.-Major W. S. Cartwright were in charge. The band paraded under the orders of Quartermaster G. B. Starkey. On Easter Monday the Corps paid an unofficial visit to Frodsham.</p>
        <p>Easter Services at Parish Church. Beautiful and inspiring were the Easter Day services at the Parish Church, which had been decorated with flowers for the occasion. There were large numbers of communicants at the early morning celebrations of Holy Communion. Matins, at which the Rev. S. G. Stanton preached, was followed by a choral Eucharist, the music being rendered in an impressive man- ner by the choir. The Rector was the cele- brant. In the afternoon the whole of the Sun- day school children attended a service, at which an address was given by the Rector. At the evening service the preacher was the Rev. Father Puller, S.S.J.E. The solo "I know that my Redeemer lireth" was effect:iv-Ay sung by Master Nevi/le Davies, and it ivis followed by the chorus "Since by ma.- came death." from "The Messiah." At the cl‘lse the IT:dleinjah Chorus was rendered.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Lines Workers' Providence. The forty-first annual meeting of the Cheshire Lines Platelayers' and Workmen's Provident Society, which consists of the workmen employed in the Engineer's Department of the Cheshire Lines Railway, was held at the Works, War- rington, yesterday. The chair was taken by Mr. Harry Blundell, the chief engineer of the com- pany, and the meeting was largely attended by officials and delegates from Lancashire and Cheshire.</p>
        <p>I The Secretary's annual report said that a large sum had been expended in aid of 100 sick 'members who had received assistance from the funds of the soeiety; 10 death claims had also been paid.</p>
        <p>Th.e report and statement of accounts having been passed, Mr. Thomas Pratt was re-elected secretary for the ensuing year, and Messrs. Robt. Jones and Harry Buckley were elected auditors.</p>
        <p>Wood's College Successes. The following pupils have been awarded Junior Typists' Certificates in connection with the National Shorthand Association Inc., Typists' Section: Hilda Booth, Alice Higham, Constance M. Kisby, Ida Cooper, Bessie Par- sonage, Nellie Griffiths, Ethel M. Burgess, Mary Mclntyre, Hilda Merrington, Edith L. Green, Hilda Cartwright, Maud Forster, Dorothy G. Bennie, A. Dykes, Emily Broady, Ellaline New- combe, I. M. Lyon, Elsie Charnock, Winifred Arrowsmith, Katie L. Done, E. Pittaway, Edith Bate, Gladys Powell, M. Whittaker, Ann Cal- land, Dorothy M. Vernon, Sarah Woolley, Marian F. White, Edith Withenshaw, Lucy Newton, May Longshaw and Madeline S. Burke. Gregg Shorthand.—Speed Certificates: 80 words per minute, Katie Murtagh (99.25 per cent. accuracy), Ethel Oakley (99 per cent. accu- racy), Ivy Shaw (98.85 per cent. accuracy); 60 words per minute, Lily Bailey (98 per cent.), Alice Higlaam (97.6 per cent.), Dorothy Burthem (96.6 per cent.), Mary Tanner (95.4 per cent.), and Mabel Bagshaw ; 40 words per. minute, Madeline S. Burke, Lucy Newton. Primary Theory Certificates (in order of merit): May Ashley, Caroline Ashley, Ada Lloyd and May Speakman.____</p>
        <p>Brilliant Deputy-Surveyor. Mr. M. A. Piercy, who has been deputy sur- veyor for Warrington for the last three years, has just proved himself to be an expert in housing and town planning, a subject that will occupy the very serious attention of muni- cipalities in the near, future. In competition with well-known men and firms, architects and surveyors in various parts of the country, he has more than held his own, coming out win- ner of a substantial prize. In August, 1915, the York City Corporation in- vited architects and surveyors to submit in open competition schemes under the Housing and Town Planning Act, 1909, in connection with the Ilaying out of some 3,000 acres of land within and without the York City area, and also observa- itions on additional areas of 6,000 acres. Professor Abererombie, of Liverpool, the assessor appointed by the York Corporation, has just issued his award, and out of the 15 schemes sent in, that of Mr. Piercy has been placed , second in order of merit, and is awarded the second premium of £5O. I The first prize of £lOO was won by Mr. Dann, of Crawley, Sussex, and the third prize of £25 lby Messrs. Stephen and H. 0. Burroughs, of Liverpool. The plans have been placed on ex- r•hibition at Yo*, . '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Accident to Judge Scott-Fox.</head>
        <p>Accident to Judge Scott-Fox.</p>
        <p>Judge Scott-Fox, K.C., ol Warrington County Court, met with an accident this week while hill climbing in the Lake District. His Honour fractured the fibula of his right leg.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>British Red Cross Society.</head>
        <p>British Red Cross Society.</p>
        <p>The Vice-President invites the Volunteer Aid Detachments of the Daresbury Division to u presentation of first-aid certificates by the Mayor on Saturday, May 13th, at 3 o'clock, iir the Town Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Red Cross Ambulance.</head>
        <p>Red Cross Ambulance.</p>
        <p>The effort being made locally to provide a. motor ambulance for the Front is progressing. In addition to the ..2100 from an anonymous donor announced last week, Mr. J. C. Parr haft 4veno.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Presentation of Certificates.</head>
        <p>Presentation of Certificates.</p>
        <p>Past an,d present students of Wood's College who have gained certificates or other awards are requested to be in attendance at a prize and certificate distribution, which is to take place at 7.30 on Tuesday in the Patten Hall, when Alderman Arthur Bennett will preside.</p>
        <p>Pound Day at Thelwall Heys. Thursday was pound day at Thelwall Heys Military Convalescent Home; and for gifts of money and goods for the general and comforts funds, to the valve of over £ll, Mrs. F. A. Frost (the hon. housekeeper) wishes to thank the donors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Markets Early Closing.</head>
        <p>Markets Early Closing.</p>
        <p>To-night the Warrington Market closes at le o'clock. This restriction will be enforced until further notice. On Wednesday the closure will be applied at 8 p.m., and an hour earlier on other days, with the exception of Thursday, when the hour will be one o'clock.</p>
        <p>New Incumbent for Emmanuel Church. The Rev. F. L. Grosvenor, of Uraston Lodge, Marlow-on-Thames, Bucks., has been appointed incumbent of the Emmanuel Reformed Episcopal Church in succession to the Rev. W. T. Jaye, and will take up his duties very shortly. Wycliffe Sunday School Anniversary. The Sunday school anniversary in connection with the Wycliffe Congregational Church is to be held to-morrow (Sunday). The preacher will be the Rev. T. S. Taylor (Eccles). In the after- noon selected hymns will be rendered by the scholars and choir, and an address given by the Rev. T. S. Taylor.</p>
        <p>For Grappenhall Volunteers. In connection with the Grappenhall Detach- ment of the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment, a concert will take place in the Large Room, Grappenhall, on Thursday, at 7.30, the pro- ceeds to go towards raising funds for the equipment of the Corps. Several well-known artistes will appear.</p>
        <p>Bank Holiday Sunshine and Rain. Although the climatic conditions on Easter Monday were not altogether genial, holiday- makers were early astir, and both the Central and Bank Quay Stations presented busy appear- ances, the exodus being chiefly to Liverpool, Manchester, New Brighton, Southport, Black- pool and Frodsham. The sunshine was of a fitful character, with occasional sharp showers, while a nice breeze made outdoor recreation exhilarating. Many people contented them- selves with walks round the rural outskirts, and in the evening flocked to the theatres and picture houses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Leather.</head>
        <p>Leather.</p>
        <p>The leather trade, says the Warrington corre- spondent of the "Leather Trades Review," is still as brisk as ever, but buyers, although plentiful, have not been able to buy very large quantities. • No doubt tanners are taking great precautions with regard to the gigantic Russian order and dividing their stocks as far as pos- sible. There is still a good demand for bends not suitable for the Russian order, and spot lots in the warehouses are readily bought up at firm prices. The prospect of a brief respite from the increasing pressure of business was uppermost in everyone's mind; consequently there is little of real importance to report.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Comforts for Soldiers Concert.</head>
        <p>Comforts for Soldiers Concert.</p>
        <p>On Saturday next, in Parr Hall, a grand evening concert will be given, under distin- guished patronage, •in aid of the Warrington District of the Voluntary Organisation Scheme that has been approved by the Government for Providing comforts for soldiers. A really fine musical programme has been arranged. The band of the 2nd South Lancs. Regiment will he in attendance, by kind permission of Colone: Moggridge. Mr. R. E. Cahill will conduct the band. The artistes include Miss Marie TiciugMnit (sonranol. Madame Annie Hargreaves (con- tralto), Miss Marie Xenaky (solo pianist), Mr. Wentworth Minty (tenor). Captain J: Stoft (tenor), and Mr. Arthur W. Pearce (beritnrel. Seats may be booked at Mr. Pearson's, Bridge- street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Keep Your Registration Card: Warning to Men.</head>
        <p>Keep Your Registration Card: Warning to Men.</p>
        <p>, On and after to-day the Warrington police, acting in accordance with the regulations recently issued,under the Defence of the Realm Act, will be entitled to demand the production of registration cards from all men of apparently ,military age. Severe penalties may be visited on those who do not possess a certificate, or have one that is out of date. For this reason it is advised that anybody who did not register, who has lost, or mislaid his card, or who has not notified a change of address, should seek to put himself right with the authorities and make an appli- cation for a new ca rd at the Town Hall. It is probable that the police will not make a systematic house to house canvass, but will visit various houses at random.</p>
        <p>It is possible that in this way the police might be able to trace men who through non-registra- tion in August are escaping service in the Forces. For the honour and good name of War- rington it is to be hoped that the police in that matter will be unsuccessful in their quest. Mr. Benjamin Farrar Retires.</p>
        <p>Although Mr. Benjamin Farrar, of 5, Willis- street, the well-known assistant manager in the wire-drawing department of Messrs. Rylands Bros., is only 68 years of age, he has been work- ing for sixty years, and he celebrates the com- pletion of that long period to-day by retiring from active labour. We and his many friends will wish him much happiness in his welt- earned retirement. Mr. Farrar has been em- ployed by Messrs. Rylands Bros. for a period only four years short of half a century, during which time he has retained the respect and confidence of all around him.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Farrar is well known in local religious life, being a prominent member of the Wycliffe Congregational Church, of which he has been a deacon for many years. He is a vice-presi- dent of the P.S.A., and has been its secretary for about twenty years. Fortunately, his wife is still bearing him company in his journey towards old age, and he has one daughter at home. .Two sons and three daughters. are married, and all live in the neighbourhood, except one daughter, the wife of a Sheffield wire works manager.</p>
        <p>To-night's Dardanelles Lecture. 'this evening, at the Parr Hall, Dr, John Grimshaw will deliver the lantern lecture on his experiences during a visit to the Dardanelles. We how that many Warringtonians will take this opportunity of showing theft interest in the v;ork of our brave soldiers ind sailors. The lecture, which has beet, arranged by the British Red Cross Society, will be illustrated by some eighty lithelight views, and should prove both interesting and instructive.</p>
        <p>As well as spending a pleasant evening, those who are able to be present will have the gratifying knowledge that they have given some practical help to the men who have been wounded or captured, as the proceeds are to be divided between the British Red Cross Society and ,the Prisoners of War Fund (South Lancs. Regiment).</p>
        <p>The British Red Cross Society's (Warrington Branch) appeal for funds to provide an ambu- lance for the Frorit will, we trust, be well met to-night. It is obvious to all that the more ambulances available to ensure the quick re' moval of the wounded to where they can be treated, the greater will be the hope of recovery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>EASTER VESTRIES.</head>
        <p>EASTER VESTRIES.</p>
        <p>I' UtISII CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Need For Assistant Clergy—And The</p>
        <p>Probable Cost.</p>
        <p>The Rector (Canon Willis) presided over the annual Vestry meeting of the Parish Church 'held in the Choir Vestry on Thursday night. The attendance was a very representative one, though not so numerous as in previous years. Offertory Slightly Declines.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Isherwood, in presenting the accounts. Pointed out that at the commencement of the Year they had a balance in hand of 12s. 6d., and, they closed with a deficit of £2O 9s. 4d. The collections for church expenses amounted to £258 65., which was £26 19s. Bd. less than The previous year. For special objects their collections totalled £261 15s. 5d., which was • a 'decrease of £3O 4s. id.. The income from other sources amounted to £llB us. Id., which was less by £3 is. id. on the previous year. The church expenditure figured at .2325 6s. 3d., an increase of £2 Os. 3d. as compared with the Previous year; whilst the fund• for special ob- -Jects stood at £334 Bs. id., showing a decrease of £64 6s. 9d. In all, the expenditure .amounted to £659 14s. 4d., leaving a deficit of £2O 9s. 4d. The average weekly expenditure Was £6 ss. 11d., as compared with £6 4s. 4d. the previous year. If the church offertory for church expenses had been the same as last Year thty would have had a balance in hand 'of £6 10s. 4d. It showed that the deficit was not due to any excessive expenditure on the 'church, but entirely owing to the falling off in the offertory for church expenses. On the proposition of Mr. R. Ant-wiss, seconded by Mr. J. R. Squire, the accounts were adopted. Mr. J. H. Houghton suggested that the ac- counts of the St. Clement's and St. George's Missions ought to be confirmed at the annual Vestry meeting. It was now too late to bring The matter forward this year, but he hoped this course would be adopted at the next meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Twenty Years Rector's Warden.</head>
        <p>Twenty Years Rector's Warden.</p>
        <p>The Rector, in proposing the re-election of Councillor Craik as his warden, paid a high tribute to that gentleman's services. He dis- liked to make a change in the office. He (the Rector) was getting an old man and his time there would not be very long. He was anxious not to lose the services of his warden.</p>
        <p>In accepting the office, Councillor Craik re- called the fact that lie entered on his twenty- first year as Rector's warden. In looking back on those years he could conscientiously say it was indeed a pleasure to him to have served such a long time. He thought it was a record for a churchwarden in Warrington. The church, at the present time, internally and externally; was in a better condition than he had ever known it. Mr. E. Isherwood was re-elected people'S warden, on the proposition of Mr. S. Clough, seconded by Mr. J. T. Whittle. In returning thanks, Mr. Isherwood said he had consented because he did not wish to leave when there was an adverse balance, although that was not his fault. He would always con- sider the welfare of the parishioners. • Ccuncillor Taylor Resigns.</p>
        <p>. Councillor Craik nominated Councillor Locker as his sidesman, and Mr, Isherwood appointed Mr. R. Antwiss, in place of Councillor S. Tay- lor. In a letter resigning the position, Coun- cillor Taylor stated that he did not want them to think that by taking mich a step he was severing his connection with the church, but when he was well he trusted to resume his usual attendance. •</p>
        <p>The Rector remarked that they were sorry to lose Mr. Taylor, but knowing what had befallen him, they knew it was difficult for him to attend and take that part in the life of the Church as he otherwise would have done.</p>
        <p>The following sidesmen were elected: Messrs. T.Chidlaw, 11. Coates, R. T. Harding, Joseph Hill, T. J. Hopkins, G. E. Knott, Robert Lee, J. R. Squire, Henry Taylor, J. T. Whittle and John Brookes. St. George's' Mission: Mr. -Robert Sumner. St. Clement's Mission, Mr. J. H. Houghton. Messrs. P. C. Price and J. T .Whittle were re-elected auditors, and Mr. E. A. White vestry clerk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Rector's Praise for Curates.</head>
        <p>Rector's Praise for Curates.</p>
        <p>The Rector, in reviewing the work of the parish, said he would like to remind them of the special offertory which was to be taken on -May 24th for the Diocesan Finance Scheme, when he hoped, notwithstanding the many calls Upon them, they would be able to raise £5O. With regard to parochial affairs he could not .Praise too highly the work of his assistant Clergy. They were aware that they were about to lose the services of Mr. Vickers. Of the splendid work he had done at St. Clement's Mission they all knew, and his loss would be keenly felt. In his new sphere at Liverpool he</p>
        <p>would have more responsible work and -a more 'difficult post, but he hoped it would be to his advantage to accept the position. Both Mr. Vickers and 3lr. Buda:l:raster might have been incumbents if they had cared to be. When the Position of Vicar of Padgate was vacant he offered it to them, but they both preferred to live and work in the town. Referring to the :needs of the parish .with regard to assistant 'clergy, he felt that they required at least four assistant clergy. A man could not work with- iout means or money, and he thought they Would recognise that a sum of £6OO was neces- sary for those positions. He had been pro- mised the help of four clergy by the Church, and he wanted them to consider a proposal that if he provided 4400 to that fund, whether the parish could not provide the remaining AIM per annum. He desired them to realise the importance of making such provision. His time .might not be very long and the kind friends who now , helped him might not be Willing to help his successor. It had been suggested to forin an endowment fund. Sup- posing they could raise- .elOO per annum, in ten years they would have &lt;21,000, which, if invested to return five per cent., would result in .X5O per annum for a fund for the assistant Clergy. Alluding to the services, he said they had every reason to be thankful for the ad- dresses given to them all through Lent. As a Tesult they had derived great benefits. Over 1,000 Communicants. . All through Holy Week they had the largest 'number of communicants. For a long time they had striven to get 1,000, but this year they reached a total of 1,027. With regard to the Proposed National Mission, they had decided to hold the mission for this district during the , last week in October. With the object of attaining simultaneous action they had ap- Pioached the clergy of the Roman Catholic Churches and the Nonconformist ministers. They did net ask for co-operation, as they did not think the time had arrived for such action. A reply was received from the Roman Catholic Clergy to the effect that they ,could not have simultaneous action, because they held their Mission in Lent; whilst the Nonconformist ministers replied that they would do every- thing possible to further the objects of the Nationai Missim</p>
        <p>Parish With 20,000 Inhabitants.</p>
        <p>A comprehensive vote of thanks to the organist (Mr. F. M. Darby), the choir, and the ladies who assisted at special services was un- animously passed. The Rey. T. Vickers, replying to a vote of thanks for his past services, and the expression of good wishes for his future, said the kindness ehown• to him by the Rector and parishioners had helped him to overcome many difficult Positions. Whatever he had done was for the i!ood of the parish, not for man's glory, but tor the glory of God. He would like to sup- Pert the suggestion of the Rector for additional assistant clergy. In that parish there was a Population a 2000, and it was in the midst of a great working-class community. They Sight to have" more working men connected With the church and its kindred organisations, and the only way to reach them was by per- •sonal contact with the clergy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Sunday Morning Services.</head>
        <p>Sunday Morning Services.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. E. Knott suggested that the morning Services on the firSt and third Sundays in the 30, onth were too much curtailed. It appeared 'to him that it was a race against time in order Complete the service at the appointed hour. ".0 preaoher could do himself justice in the '4llle allotted for hi g 'Darman. 14. Isherwood thought thst'it the sermons</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <p>were curtailed during the summer evenings he was sure many more people would attend. • The Rector: Very well; we will think about it.</p>
        <p>Mr. Coates pointed out that the sermon on the first and third Sundays in the month should take its proper place, after the Nicene Creed.</p>
        <p>At a subsequent meeting of communicants Messrs. E. H. Coates and T. J. Hopkins were re-elected lay representatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>HOLY TRINITY.</head>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY.</p>
        <p>Response To Country's Call By The</p>
        <p>Youth Of The Parish.</p>
        <p>Presiding at the Holy Trinity vestry meet- ing, the Vicar (the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton) referred to the noble response made to the country's call by members of the choir, Sunday school, Young Men's Club, and members of the congregation. There was indeed, he said, cause for congratulation in the way helpers had come forward to fill the places of those who had gone. The vestry placed on record its sense of the loss sustained by the Church and parish in the death of Mr. Wm. Gill, a member of the congre- gation, and warden for many years, and passed a resolution of sympathy with Mrs. Gill and her family. The ehurchwarden's accounts were presented, and showed the total income to he £267 us. id., which included £lB3 19s. 11d, as general offer- tories. Special offertories amounted to £55 3s. 2d., which was a considerable reduction on the previous year. The expenditure amounted to £267 is. 9d., leaving a balance in awl of 9s. 441. The Vicar considared that in the circum- stances, the Church was in a satisfactory futan- cial condition. A fair amount lind been con- tributed to local war funds, and economies had been practised which had been an iavantage to the church. The curacy fund was in a much better position than he had ever known it. The accounts were adopted.</p>
        <p>The Vicar avpointed Mr. T. Welsby as his warden, in place of the late Mr. W. Gill, and the vestry elected Mr. John Hesketh as people's warden. The sidesmen appointed were Messrs. J. Rowcock, Ernest Dyke, Fred Taylor, James Harding, Wm. Gandy, Geo. H. Gill, J. J. Hesketh, Abe Walsh, T. Emmett, L. R. Rowson, W. W. Leigh, W. Turner, J. W. Dyke, W. E. Worthington, A. E. Hill, J. A. Dawson, T. Horrabin, J. Brown, W. Eddowes, Joseph Hig- nett and Weston. Messrs. G. H. Gill and Weston were appointed lay delegates. The thank § of the 'vestry were accorded to the wardens and choir for their services during the past twelve months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>ST. ANN'S.</head>
        <p>ST. ANN'S.</p>
        <p>Roll Of Honour Containing Over Three</p>
        <p>Hundred Names.</p>
        <p>The St. Ann's Church has a Roll of Honour, and presiding at the annual vestry meeting on Tuesday night, the Vicar (the Rev. James Davies) made the interesting statement that over 300 men who were doing their duty to King and country had their names ,recorded on the roll. In thanking the wardens, sidesmen and other church officers for their services during the past twelve months, the Vicar remarked that they had done their work admirably. They had cause to be thankful for their position. They were under a war cloud, but they had not suffered as had the people of Belgium, Flanders and parts of France.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. IL Corbett was re-appointed vicar's warden, Mr. T. S. Jolley people's warden, and the following were chosen as sidesmen: Messrs. G. Armstrong, A. Bradshaw, G. Bradshaw, T. .T. Boswell, J. Glover, R. Randall, W. Catterall, A. Fryer, Welch, R. Smith, H. Lea, J. E. Harrison. C. A. Lea, A. Owen, G. Stringer, Fred Taylor and W. Thomas.</p>
        <p>The annual statement of accounts showed the total receipts to be £lO7 6s. Id., which was made up of 11,098 coins. The general offertories end- ing April 21st, amounted to £53 _l4s. 9d.. There was a balance in hand of £4 12s. 7d. The Vicar explained that the statement did not include a sum of about £25. which was raised for the workers' fund, and the curate and lay readers' funds. The accounts were adopted. Messrs. G. Armstrong and T. S. Jolley were appointed lay representatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>ST. JAMES'.</head>
        <p>ST. JAMES'.</p>
        <p>A New Mission Church Opened Free</p>
        <p>Of Debt.</p>
        <p>The Vicar (the Rev. H. B. Firth) presided over this vestry meeting on Thursday evening, and in reviewing the work of the past year, spoke of the loss to the parish occasioned by the absence of so many young men on military duty, the choirs, Sunday school, silesmen, all feel ing their loss. During the year the new Mission Church was opened free of debt, a solid, con- venient building well adapted for divine service. In December the electric light was installed in St. James', and had proved to be a great im- provement. The acoustics were, however, bad, the echo being difficult to overcome, and a sounding board over pulpit would be an im- provement. The services on Good Friday were well observed, there being a great congregation at 10.30-565 people were pre-sent, and there was not room to accommodate all cont.rs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Accounts.</head>
        <p>The Accounts.</p>
        <p>The accounts showed income £2ll 15s. 6d., in- cluding £ll4 ss. sd. from general offertory, £77 4s. from special offertory, and £2O grant from the Church Council. There was a balance due to bank of 7s. 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Elections.</head>
        <p>Elections.</p>
        <p>The Vicar spoke of great help received from the churchwardens. Mr. Acton was retiring after three years' excellent work, worthily assisted by Mr. Eckersley as people's warden. The Vicar appointed Mr. J. Fargher as his warden; and the people elected Mr. W. Eckersley as their warden.</p>
        <p>Messrs. S. Acton, F. .Astley, J. Eckersley, C. George, B. Hall, A. Harding, E. Sykes, P. O'Brien, A. Todd and H. Woodward ware elected sidesmen.</p>
        <p>Lay Representatives.—Messrs. S. Acton and W. H. Stansfleld.</p>
        <p>ST. HILDA'S MISSION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rathbone, after being 15 years warden, retired from office. He had, said the Vicar, per- formed admirable and devoted service, and would be greatly missed.</p>
        <p>The Vicar appointed Mr. .T. W. Reynolds in his stead, and St. Hilda's congregation elected Mr. J. Jenks as people's warden. Sidesmen : Messrs. A. Livingston and A. Jenks.</p>
        <p>The total receipts were £35 2s. 11(1., and the balance in hand 15s. 6-kl.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>ST. PETER'S.</head>
        <p>ST. PETER'S.</p>
        <p>Rev. R. Percival On The Coming Of</p>
        <p>New Conditions.</p>
        <p>The Rev. B. Percival (vicar) presided over St. Peter's Church vestry meeting, held in the Vestry on Wednesday evening. Mr. T. Reddish (people's warden) presented the churchwardens' accounts, which showed that the year was started with a balance in hand of gn 4s. 9d. From that amount, however, ex- plained Mr. Reddish, had to be taken an out- standing amount for the painting of the church, and they thus really only started with a balance of V. 9s. 9d. This year the expenses had been much heavier owing to the extra price of fuel and gas and extra repairs to the church. The 'painting of the church had cost £l3 11s. 7d., and the insurance of the church against air- craft £7, making a total of £2O 11s. 7d. for extra expenses.</p>
        <p>There was an adverse balance at the bank of £3 3s. 21d. The contribution from the sale of work was £l4, and the grant from the parochial tea £3 ss. , Collections for special objects had realised £93 13s. 11id.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel pointed out that the heavy expenses were chiefly caused by the large gas account, which this year was twice as great as last year's. In 1915 the gas account amounted to £3'Bs. 3d., and in 1916 to .E 6 17s.</p>
        <p>Vicar On The Need For Unity. The Vicar said the year had been a very try- ing one, and they had every reason for congratu- lation. The people had always responded</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Workers' National League Formed In Warrington.</head>
        <p>Workers' National League Formed In Warrington.</p>
        <p>MR. SEDDON'S VISIT.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. A. Seddon on Thursday night visited the Dock Labourers' Union Office, Sankey- street, Warrington, with the object of forming a local branch of the British Workers' National League, which has for its purpose the success- ful propagation of the Allies' cause and victory for the Allied arms.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance of representa- tiyes from various trade unions, in the borough, and apologies were received from other sup- porters regretting inability to attend owing to the calls of business, but giving the .Object their hearty sup Port.</p>
        <p>After My. Seddon had explained the objects. of League, it was unanimously agreed te form a Warrington Branch, on the motion of Mr. J. Elwell, seconded by Mr. Si. Mall. Mr. F. Hodson was appointed secretary, to whom all communications should be addressed. Mr. Seddon was asked to arrange for a public meeting to be held in the town in support of the League, on or about May 21st. It is hoped that Mr. Walsh, Mr. Seddon and other speakers will be able to attend.</p>
        <p>The founders of the League are Mr. J. A. Seddon (en-president of the Trades Congress), Mr. A. M. Thompson (the well-known journalist), the Right Hon. Will Crooks, M.P., Messrs. James O'Grady, M.P., C. B. Stanton, M.P., Stephen Walsh, M.P., A. Wilkie, ALP., H. G. Wells, and other, prominent members of the Labour party who are strongly opposed to the policy formulated by Messrs. Philip Snow- den, W. C. Anderson, F. Jowett, and other mem- bers of the Independent Labour Party.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>CONSOLIDATION OF FORCES.</head>
        <p>CONSOLIDATION OF FORCES.</p>
        <p>The following manifesto to the workers of Great Britain has been issued:—</p>
        <p>"Fellow countrymen,—The war's frightful up- heaval and the industrial reconstruction which will have to be undertaken when peßce returns urgently demand an immediate consqlidation of the national forces of Labour to pi epare for the problems which will have to bo faced. Things can never t e the same again for any of us. Men £l7'P learning that they must readjust th..ir s and replan their activities. The old shibboleths, the old pr.rty cries, will be meantn.c.ess in zhe new world which the war is bringing forth. Labour will be called on to take its full share in the Imperial and national government, as already it does in Australia and New Zealand. To do this effectively, organised political Labour must have its own principles and programme on national and Imperial problems. The British Workers' National League has come into being to help in forming those principles and pro- gramme.</p>
        <p>"In war the national interests override all sectional interests. In peace, also, national in- terests must be supreme. Our national solvency in the present crisis has not depended on the resources of our banks, but on. the credit of the • State. Our whole elaborate commercial system would have fallen like a house of cards but for the patriotism, and public spirit, and the courage of the millions who have rallied to the defence of hearth and home. Competition and private profiteering have led to waste, in- efficiency, and fraud. The nation has only been saved from destruction in so far as it has de- pended on patriotic national solidarity. It can. only be saved in the future by the full expan- sion under national control of the vital national resources.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>In National Brotherhood.</head>
        <p>In National Brotherhood.</p>
        <p>"As Britons have proved liiemselves brothers in arms on the field of battle, so let us remain banded in national brotherhood for the achieve- ment of national purposes in the days of peace that lie before us. Let us realise the Federation of the Free British States. Let us knit together in unbreakable bonds the democracy of the Motherland, and the democracies of English speech, which have rallied in memorable and glorious fraternity to the defence of our com- mon liberties and civilisation.</p>
        <p>"The State, which, in the moment of peril, invoked the aid of all her children, must not be allowed to forget the eternal debt she then contracted. The workers who then rushed to</p>
        <p>her succour earned by their sacrifice an irre- futable claim on her protection. Men hence- forth must not be regarded as mere profit-earn- k ing tools in the hands of a master class, but free citizens in a free Commonwealth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Ultimate Aims.</head>
        <p>The Ultimate Aims.</p>
        <p>"The ultimate internationalism cannot be brought abopt by the pious resolutions of little sects of visionaries; it can only be realised by the complete vindication of national rights and bonds of agreement between independent and mutually respecting nations. "The most solid basis of such an interna- tional understanding is the integrity of the British Empire. To maintain and strengthen that integrity the commerce and industry of the Empire must be defended against the pro- ducts of the sweated labour of our present enemies.</p>
        <p>"Drastic agricultural reform under national direction must be. so shaped as to secure the full scientific development of the land in the national interest. Education reform must attain for the nation greater technical efficiency combined with a more popular access to know- ledge. Our political institutions must be so democratised irrespective of sex, as to destroy for all time the sinister influence of money and machine government.</p>
        <p>"Britain has been good enough to fight for; it must be made good enough to live in for all Britons."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</p>
        <p>Before leaving for .home thanks and 'rousing cheers from the lads were given. to Mr: Pen- nington and ~family for their kindness during the afternoon, - •.' • - •</p>
        <p>SOUTH-WEST LANCASHIRE REGT: Warrington Company. Headquarters, Drill Hall. ORDERS FOR WEEK ENDING MAY 6th, • by W. PETER RYLANDS. Officer Commanding. Parades. To-day.—Composite Company Field Training and Outpost. Geieral assembly Cinder-lane. Thelwall, 3.20 p.m.; disperse 5 p.m. Officers commanding units conference 3 p.m. on site. Warrington Company parade 2.45 p.m. Latch- ford Swing Bridge. Return Thelwall. Rail fare paid. Uniform, rifles. Monday and Thursday, 8 p.m. Saturday next, May 6th, 3 p.m., Bank Park. Fire Brigade Drill.—Thursday, 3 p.m. Sec- tion-Commander J. Wilding in charge. Musketry.—Tuesday, from 7.15 p.m., Leglf- street range. Orderly-Sergeant for the week, Section-Commander H. Davies. Group B Reserves. No. 1 Section.—All as detailed, except Fire Brigade drill. Recruits' Class.—Applicants may join any drill night.</p>
        <p>Memoranda. Brassards must be worn on all parades except when in uniform.</p>
        <p>BItITISII BATTLESHIP SUNK.</p>
        <p>Strikes A Mine In The Mediterranean. The Press Bulvau issued the following state- ment yesterday afternoon : " The Secretary of the Admiralty makes the following announcement: H.M.S. Russell (Captain William Bowden Smith, R.N.), flying the flag of ,Rear-Admiral Freemantle, struck a mine in the Mediterranean on Thursday and sank. The admiral, captain, 24 officers, and 676 men were saved, and there are about 124 officers and men missing." The Russell was a battleship of the Duncan class, launched in February, 1901, and had,a displacement of 14,000 tons. Her length was 814 feet; her breadth, 75.5 feet; horse power, 18,000; speed, 19 knots; mounted with four 12-inch guns, twelve 6-inch, twelve 3-inch, six 3-pounders, twcr machine-guns, and tour torpedo tubetk— She cost £1,010,995. •</p>
