      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 29, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 29, 1916.</p>
        <p>readily, and he would also like to thank the church officers for their regular attendance at church. The church could not get on well with- out the church officers. He wished to thank the choir for their valuable services. They had had Mr. Martin with them as curate for almost two years, and he had proved satisfactory in every way, being very willing, and a good visitor. In a parish like St. Peter's there were very few• people who understood how difficult it was to get in touch with everybody. mere would have to be four clergymen working to do so easily. What could not be done in that way could, be done by the members of the church, and in this respect they were rather lacking. They should realise that they could all do something. The country was on the verge of anew epoch, for one age was passing away and another was coming in. Things could not possibly remain as they were. God had, for some great reason, per- mitted this country to enter into the great war, 11 but he (the Vicar) believed that there was a great blessing behind it, if people would make up theit •minds to do their best. There had been too much "ism," and • Christians would have to come together more. There were plenty who could unite them, and the things which kept people apart were trivial compared with those which could unite them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Appointments.</head>
        <p>Appointments.</p>
        <p>• The Vicar then renominated Mr. T. S. Steel to act as his warden, thanking him for the help he had rendered to him in the past. Mr. T. Reddish was appointed people's warden, on the proposition of Mr. G. E. Gre.aves, seconded by Mr. W. H. Dickens, who thought there was not a better warden in Warrington. The following sidesmen were elected</p>
        <p>By the Vicar: Messrs. R. Knight, J. J. Hands, J. Taylor, G. W. McQuillan, T. Ince, D. Armi- tage, H. Hardy, J. Clare and C. Rigby. By the Vestry: Messrs. G. E. Greaves, R. Brownbill, W. Pinson, W. H. Dickens, R. Ben- nett, J. Armitage, E. Bothwell, J. Shaw and W. Warburton. Messrs. T. Reddish and G. E. Greaves were reappointed lay representatives, Messrs. W. H. Dickens and E. A. Padmore, auditors, and Mr. T. S. Steel vestry clerk. Mr. Reddish reappointed Mr. G. E. Greaves as his warden, and thanked all the sidesmen for their help during the year.</p>
        <p>The Vicar returned thanks for the Easter offering, which was slightly ahead of laA year's. The number. of communicants in attendance en Easter Day was 250--about the same as last year. A small committee was appointed to arrange a collection in the parish for the Bishop of Liverpool's fund.</p>
        <p>Mr E. A. Padraore thanked all for their kind inquiries during his recent illness. On the- prooocition of Mr. T. S. Steel, seconded by Mr. T. Reddish, a vote of thanlis was accorded the Vi .tar for presidin4.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>ST: BARNA BAS'.</head>
        <p>ST: BARNA BAS'.</p>
        <p>Pleasure To Work With Church Officers.</p>
        <p>The St. Barnabas' Church Trestry meeting was held in the Vestry on Thursday evening, the Vicar (the Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth) pre- siding. a Mr. B. Dimmelou- presented the church- wardens' accounts, whicha showed that there was a balance brought forward of £4 is. QL The receipts totalled £147 17s. 5d., and the expendi- ture £l4l 10s. 2d., leaving a balance of £6 7s. 3d.</p>
        <p>The Vicar re-elected gr. B. Dimmelow as his warden, and Mr. W. Boyle was reappointed people's warden. The following sidesmen era elected:—Messrs. W. Hind, F. Kimberley, T. A. Jones, H. V. Maihew, E. Rotherham, R. Smith, J. E. Morgan, W. Turner, T. Oxley, E. Wakefield, G. Wakefield and J. Garner. Votes of thanks were accorded the Wardens and sidesmen for their services,, and also to the 'Vicar for presiding. • Replying, the Vicar said it had been a great pleasure to work with the church officers, who, (luring a rather trying year, had been a great help to him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>EMMANUEL CHURCH.</head>
        <p>EMMANUEL CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Presentation To The Late Incumbent. The annual Easter Vestry meeting was held on Thursday evening, in the Church Institute, the late Incumbent (the Rev. W. T. Jaye) pre- siding over a fair number of membtrs of the congregation</p>
        <p>_ The People's Warden (Mr. Robert Lees) pre- sented the accounts for the year, showing a very creditable balance in hand. The accounts were unanimously adopted. The various secretaries of the organisations connected with the church gave their reports, each showing that the church is being fairly well maintained.</p>
        <p>During the evening Mr. R. Lees presented to the Rev. W. T. Jaye a beautiful canteen of cutlery, subscribed for by the members of the congregation and friends as a taken of appre- ciation and goodwill whilst he was the minis- ter. Mr. Lees spoke in high terms of the work Mr. Jaye had accomplished during his ministry. _</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. T. Jaye feelingly thanked the congregation for their hearty appreciation of his services, and hoped his successor would have an equally pleasant time with them*, The nomination of Minister's Warden- was left in abeyance until the new curate-in-charge enters on his duties, Mr. William Atherton acting as such in the meantime. Mr. Robert Lees was unanimously elected People's Warden and also heartily thanked for his past services.</p>
        <p>Messrs. J. W. Jowett and Frank Lloyd were elected Synodsmen.</p>
        <p>The following were elected members of the Select Vestry: Messrs. W. Brown, A. Dickens, John Bibby, Thomas Lloyd, R. Armstrong, A. Johnson, W. Atherton, T. Moon and H. Heald.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE DAVID POTTS.</p>
        <p>Killed.</p>
        <p>11th. "The enemy," he explained, "exploded a mine and, a heavy piece of timber, which was thrown up in the air, fell on your hus- band's head and killed him on the spot. . • • He was a good worker.' and always did his best."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE. Local Cadet Battalion In Field Day Operations.</head>
        <p>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE. Local Cadet Battalion In Field Day Operations.</p>
        <p>Following the practice of former years, the Warrington Battalion C.L.B. Cadets devoted the whole of Easter Monday to field day opera- tions. The orders were to parade at Arpley Station at 9.30 a.m.; dress, full marching order, with haversack, lunch, and carbines. St. Elphin's Company to supply the ambulance section and St. Paul's Company the signallers. There were 130 officers and lads on parade, including Major J. A. Brookes (commanding), Major Ford, Captain Bunch, Lieuts. N. J. Mor- ris and R. P. Gibson, and Sergt.-Major Essen- high. On arriving at Stockton Heath, Lieut. Morris set out at once to take up an outpost" at flillfoot Farm, Walton (by kind permission of Mr. Ralph Tickle), intending to resist all at- tempts to dislodge him. The place was well chosen for the purpose, being practically un- assailable on three sides, a factor, no doubt, in the stubborn defence which the little force put up later on. The opposing force, under Major Ford, opened the attack with small detach- ments at various points. • During these minor engagements his main body, in charge of Captain Bunch, were making by forced marching a wide detour to get into their position. Their arrival was exceedingly well timed, and having orders to take the "out- post" at all costs, they pressed forward, and in the final rush compelled the little garrison to give way before superior numbers. The ambu- lance had a large number of "cases," and after these had been inspected, the whole force was dismissed for mid-day rations. In the afternoon similar work was under- taken on the way to Mr. Pennington's farm at Appleton, the idea being that Captain Bunch and Lieut. Gibson with half the battalion would intercietpt and, if possible, prevent a force under Lieut. Morris reaching its objective. Fine skirmishing tactics were shown by both sides in these operations, the other outstand- ing feature being the excellent disposition of the force under Captain Bunch and the dash and energy displayed by the other side.. The interval _before tea was taken up with platoon and battalion &amp;lin under Sergt.-Major Mannering. Tea was provided by the Acting- Quartermaster (Lieut. Morris) and his staff at fie o'clock.</p>
        <p>In the evening a football match took place between st. Paul's and the rest of the battalion, honours going to St. Paul's with a comfortable margin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</head>
        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</p>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE DAVID POTTS.</p>
        <p>Mersey Street Tanner Killed By Mine Explosion.</p>
        <p>Private David Potts, of the Royal Welsh Fusiliers, has been killed in action. He was 24 years of age and was a married man, with one little girl. An employe of the Mersey- street Tannihg Co., he enlisted for the period of the war and had been at the Front for about six months. ,His widow and daughter are staying with his father-in-law at 44, Lord Nelson-street.</p>
        <p>The late Private Potts's father, Mr. Charles Potts, galvaniser, was a well-known Warring- ton Rugby footballer in the days of "Fair" ?Turner and Harry Ashton, and he himself, was well known in junior club football. He had a brother, now time-expired, who served in the Territorials, and was wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Always Did His Best.</head>
        <p>Always Did His Best.</p>
        <p>The Officer Commanding Private Potts's Company wrote to Mrs. Potts, saying that her husband was killed on the morning of April</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>PARENTS' UNCERTAINTY.</head>
        <p>PARENTS' UNCERTAINTY.</p>
        <p>Rifleman Who Has Been Reported Killed, Wounded, And Prisoner.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. James Griffiths, of 8, Stevenson- street, Battersby-lane, have received a letter this week from Sergeant A. E. Taylor, of the Rifle Brigade, concerning their son, Rifleman James Griffiths, who has been variously reported as killed, wounded and missing. First Griffiths was reported as wounded September 25th, but an officer wrote to his home saying that after he had been wounded he was killed between the German and the British lines. Later, official intimation was received that he was missing. The letter from the sergeant, received this week, which we reproduce, says that Rifleman Griffiths was alive on September 26th, a day after he was said by the officer to have been killed. Mr. and Mrs. Griffiths are naturally upset by the un- certainties in the case, and they will be glad to receive any information regarding their son from those in a position to give it. The letter from Sergeant A. E. Taylor, says:— "As his old platoon sergeant I may say he was a man who did his duty to the fullest extent, and as brave as a lion. During an engagement he received a nasty wound in the muscle of MS left arm, and it was so bad I had a big job, in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>RIFLEMAN J. GRIFFITHS</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN J. GRIFFITHS</p>
        <p>the excitement, to stop the flow of blood. I was lucky• enough to capture a few Germans, and one by luck was a hospital stretchier bearer, so I immediately got him to attend to your son, and he stopped the bleeding.</p>
        <p>"At the time I had forty wounded men in the German lines, and Jim was in an unfit state to be moved, so I was obliged to leave him behind, and as far as I know he was taken as a Wounded prisoner of •war. I fully expected that you would. have heard from him before this. Any, way, do not despair. I have had a good talk with a lot of his chums, including Jim Swift. There are hundreds more people missing who keep turning up. But I can vouch that your son was alive when I left him on the 26th September, 1915, but was too far gone with loss of blood or I would have taken him with me. I like to give all the news I can concerning our brave boys, who have done so well, and Jim was ~ne of the best We have still a lot of Warrington boys, and they all know me, and I expect you will hear of me very often if the war ever does finish, as all the lads from Warrington came into my platoon. 7-. . Rifleman Griffiths is 26 years of age, and was a moulder at Messrs.. Richmond's. He has a brother in the King's Royal Rifles. His father was at Messrs. Pearson and Knowles.</p>
        <p>• AN „ • - „ , • „ .• • • .• . • • . • • • •••••:•li, - •:•:•• • . , • • .• •• " • . . : • • 1im:141.4/4.%.*/**.41•44;.L.A: : • • ' ••••:* " • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>GERMAN RAIDS AND INTRIGUES.</head>
        <p>GERMAN RAIDS AND INTRIGUES.</p>
        <p>IMllliftu</p>
        <p>_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Two Warrington Lads.</head>
        <p>Two Warrington Lads.</p>
        <p>Private J. Thomason, writing on. April 14th to the widow, says:— "Just a few lines of sympathy concerning the 'great loss of your husband. We were the only two lade in our regiment that .belonged to. Warrington and we were greatly attached to each other. I was near him when he was killed and I may say that he died smiling and did not suffer. He was a good soldier and was liked by everyone; and all his comrades wish me to express their deepest sympathy. The enemy blew a mine up in front of our lines and he was hit by a piece of flying debris and he died instantly. . . . He died and did his duty bravely." • Another comrade, writing to console the widow, says . f` We shall miss him now he has gone, as he was so jolly with all the boys of the platoon.</p>
        <p>. . . • He died like a soldier and a man. He is buried all right and his grave shall be well looked after."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>PRIVATE J. HEESOM.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. HEESOM.</p>
        <p>News Received That He Has' Attack On The East Coast And Died Of Wounds In Hospital. Visit To Ireland.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. John Heesom, 8, St. Mary-street. Latchford, who last week were officially in- formed that their son, Private John Heesom, was in hospital dangerously ill, have now re- ceived official news that he has died of wounds in hospital. Private Heesom, who was 25 years of age, was the elder of two soldier brothers, the other one, who is 21 years of age, and a regular soldier, having been wounded, and gone back to the Front. Private John enlisted soon after the outbreak of war, having been previously employed by Messrs. R. A. Naylor, Ltd.. at the Walton Lay-by. As a boy he attended St. James' Day School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE G. T. YEATES.</p>
        <p>Turks Are Gentlemen Compared With</p>
        <p>Germans.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Yeates, New Cottages, Orford, has re- ceived news to the effect that her husband, Private G. T. Yeates, has been wounded in Mesopotamia. Writing from a hospital ship on the 17th March, Private Yeates said: "I sup- pose you have heard I have been wounded, but don't worry, for I am going on all right. France is nothing compared with here. I would rather be in France ten times over, although the Turks are gentlemen compared with the Germans. But there are also the Arabs, who are terribly cruel men, while the heat has to be contended, with. There is hardly any water. We were five days on land before going into the battle, which was for the relief of Kut-el-Amar. There are no trenches here, or anything to take cover behind, like there is in France. It is one stretch of flat land. You have- no idea what we had to go through before we reached the Turkish trenches. They lay level with the ground, and we could not see them until we were on top of them. I was hit about 5.30 in the evening, after we had taken two lines of Turkish trenches. I struggled to the first dressing station, about two miles away, where I got my wounds dressed, and then I had to walk to the Base Field Hospital, about another two miles away. Here I got a little rest, but was called up again, and had to walk farther on, because they were expecting a Turkish counter attack. After we had walked about six miles we pitched camp, but were up again at six o'clock the following morning. We had to tramp back to the extreme base, a dis- tance of 17 miles, without a mouthful of any- thing to eat, and no water, only dirty water we found in'the ditches, which flows there from the snow on the mountains.. I was feeling almost dead, owing to the heat and loss of blood. An airman dropped a bomb on the column, but we waved the Red Cross flag, mid he did not drop any more, so you can see that they respect the Red Cross. . • • We get everything we want on this hospital ship—it is like coming from hell into heaven."</p>
        <p>Writing to Mr. B. Farrar, secretary of the Wycliffe P.S.A., Private. Yeates said: "This place is different from France. We are miles away from anywhere, out on the plains, and all the water we can get is brought by pack mules from the river Tigris. . . . I am glad to hear you are getting Mr. James back, although he will be missed by the 'boys.' Please remem- ber me to all the members." "P.S.—Since writing this / have been shot in the chest and am in hospital." Private Yeates has been wounded twice pre- viously, having served for some months in France. He joined the Army as a drummer boy when quite young, and served his time. He was employed at the Orford Tannery, and was a well-known member of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Ile is a native -of Devonshire, awl is 29 yea-rti , of age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>SINN FEINERS ASSIST.</head>
        <p>SINN FEINERS ASSIST.</p>
        <p>During last week-end and the beginning .of this the Germans made an attempt to upset us by what appears to hare been a concerted plan —by attacking Lowestoft on the East Coast with men of war, by attempting to land arms in Ireland, and by stirring up the disaffected in Ireland to revolt. With the exception of the rising in Ireland, which is to be regretted, the scheme was a fiasco.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>ATTEMPTED LANDING.</head>
        <p>THE ATTEMPTED LANDING.</p>
        <p>The Secretary of the Admiralty made the following announcement:— _</p>
        <p>"During the period between p.m. April 20th and p.m. April 21st, an attempt to land arms and ammunition in Ireland was made by a vessel under the guise of a neutral merchant ship, but in reality a German auxiliary, in con- junction with a German submarine. "The auxiliary sank and a number of prisoners were made, amongst whom was Sir Roger Casement."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>HOW SIR ROGER WAS CAPTURED.</head>
        <p>HOW SIR ROGER WAS CAPTURED.</p>
        <p>Lord Lansdowne volunteered to the House of Lords an interesting account of the attempted German landing on the west coast of Ireland. A German vessel disguised as a Dutch trading vessel, and a German submarine, brought the invaders. From the submarine there landed in a collapsible boat three individuals, of whom two (one Sir Roger Casement) were made pris- oners. The disguised German ship was stopped by one of his Majesty's ship, and while she was being taken into Queenstown Harbour, no prize crew having ,been put on board, she exhibited the German flag and sank herself. All the crew were 'welled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>ABORTIVE ATTEMPT ON LOWESTOFT.</head>
        <p>ABORTIVE ATTEMPT ON LOWESTOFT.</p>
        <p>The Secretary of the Admiralty issued the following announcement through the Press Bureau</p>
        <p>"About 4.30 in the morning a German btittle cruiser squadron, accompanied by light cruisers and destroyers, appeared off Lowestoft. The local naval force engaged it, and in about 20 minutes it returned to Germany„ chased by our light cruisers and destroyers: On' shore two men, one .woman, and a child were killed. The material damage seems to have been in- significant so far as is known at present. Two British cruisers and one destroyer were hit, but noneorwas sunk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>REVOLT IN IRELAND.</head>
        <p>REVOLT IN IRELAND.</p>
        <p>Mr. Birrell, speaking in the Commons, made the grave announcement that a rising took place in Dublin at noon on Monday. The Post Office was seized, and the telegraphic commu- nication was cut off, and the rebels took posses- sion of four•or fiVe parts of the city. In the courge of the day soldiers arrived from the Cur- ragh, and at the time of the Chief Secretary's statement the situation was well in hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>SINN FEIN MOVEMENT.</head>
        <p>THE SINN FEIN MOVEMENT.</p>
        <p>The Sinn Fein movement, which from the start has aimed at destroying and supplanting the constitutional movement conducted by the Irish National party, has, since the war began, violently assailed Mr. Redmond and his fol- lowers for the patriotic stand they have made, and especially, for the zeal they have shown in obtaining recruits for Irish regiments. Writing last month to one of his supporters, Mr. Red- mond made a stern reference to "men hostile to the, Irish party and to the constitutional moire- ment in Ireland, men who are either avowedly pro-German or are at least opposed to recruit- ing in Ireland anil committed to the monstrous doctrine that-Ireland should remain neutral in the war."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>MR. CHARLES DUKES.</head>
        <p>MR. CHARLES DUKES.</p>
        <p>Makes Unsuccessful Appeal To</p>
        <p>Tribunal.</p>
        <p>"PERSONAL" CLAIM TO BE MADE IN CHESHIRE. ,</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Dukes, secretary of the War- rington Trades and Labour Council, applied for exemption from military service before the Manchester Tribunal yesterday afternoon and was refused.</p>
        <p>The claim was based on indispensability to his trade union—the Gasworkers' and General Labourers'. He also stated that he had con- scientious objections, but the Tribunahzefused to heat= these, stating that they would have to be considered by the Tribunal sitting in his residential area—that in the Runcorn Rural District, Mr. Dukes occupying a , house at Stockton Heath. .</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls) and Alderman D. Tinnion were interested 'spectators of the proceedings. The appeal of indispensability was lodged by Mr. J. R. Clynes, M.P., general secretary of the union, but that gentleman being unable to be present, Mr. Fleming Eccles, district secre- tary, appeared in his stead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>"INDISPENSABLE."</head>
        <p>"INDISPENSABLE."</p>
        <p>I• Mr. Clynes' statement of claim was read by rthe Chairman (Mr. E. Brierley, who is also ' the Manchester stipendiary magistrate), this ' stating that Mr. Dukes was a district organiser and each week had to attend to a variety of questions relating to trade disputes. He was a considerable factor in their speedy settle- ment. He had also to visit workmen and meet employers regarding accident and compensa- tion claims. More than 6,000 members of the union in the district had joined the Army, and numerous questions put by. them had to be dealt with by him. He also had to settle ques- tions relating to the National Insurance Act His duties could not be performed by a woman or youth. Mr. Dukes' work was of national importance, because constantly he was con- cerned with the settling of disputes in con- nection with the production of every kind of munitions. He was responsible for and had charge of a part of the Lancashire district, covering a membership of 15,000 workers chiefly engaged on munitions. The purpose of Mr.' Dukes' work gave an advantage to the country, I which was greater than it would be if he were engaged on military service. It should also be stated that whilst Mr. Dukes was unwilling to render military service he was not averse o doing work of national importance. He considered that his present duties came in that category. WHY HE WENT TO MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>This statement having been read, the mili- tary representative turned to Mr. Dukes, who was seated within a yard of him, and re- marked: "You have taken rather a strong anti-war action "</p>
        <p>"Yes, very extreme, I. believe," Mi. Dukes repl:ed. The Military Representative: You have held meetings to stop the war?—No. _ _</p>
        <p>The -Chairman: Has that anything to do with thp4l, This is not an .appl.l.3a;ion Mr. Dukes, but just from the general secretary of his union. On your Personal grounds, Mr. Dukes, you will have, to appeal in the 'district wbere you live.</p>
        <p>Mr. bukes : May I state my position with regard to why I am here? I have acted purely under the guidance of the authorities. At Runcorn they told me I could choose between Manchester and Runcorn, and I have chosen Manchester.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: The present claim is only for your employment. We have nothing to do with your .personal A Member of the Tribunal: Mr. Fleming Eccles, who represents the union, is the gentle- man we want to hear. Mr. Eccles: We really cannot get on without Mr. Dukes.</p>
        <p>The Military Representative: How low,' has Mr. Pukes been with you in the Manchester office? Mr. Dukes: Seven months. I was at War- rington for six or seven years and was then transferred to Manchester.</p>
        <p>Mr. Eccles: The point I would like to em- phasise is that we understood Mr. Dukes' per- sonal claim and that of the union would be taken in a dual capacity and not separately. The Chairman: We have no jurisdiction in Manchester on the personal grounds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>MR. DUKES' RESPONSIBILITIES.</head>
        <p>MR. DUKES' RESPONSIBILITIES.</p>
        <p>Continuing, Mr. Eccles remarked that of the f 0 branches which the union had in their area, Mr. Dukes was solely responsible for 17, and he had no fewer than 15,000 members under his direct control. At present there were in dispute 46 wage questions, covering 13 different branchee. By wage questions he did not merely mean just the matter of increased wages, but working conditions. A man like Mr. Dukes, who was handling matters like those every day, was doing most useful work of national import- ance.</p>
        <p>The Chairman asked what would be done if Mr. Dukes fell ill or went away—got a better berth, for instance—but the reply was inaudible.</p>
        <p>A Member of the Tribunal: How many branch secretaries have you 2—About ninety. Couldn't you promote one of these ?—Our difficulty, especially in the last six months, has been in finding men to fill the branch secre- taries' positions. This is due to the war. We have a number of branches in suspense and chaos because of that.</p>
        <p>The Military Representative: Mr. Dukes seems to spend a good deal of time on other work in Warrington. I have here an advertisement about advice which was to be given to conscientious objectors by the Independent Labour Party. Have you, Mr. Dukes, anything to do with that? Mr. Dukes: I am not a member of the 1.L.P., but I am an executive member of the British Socialist Party.</p>
        <p>SUBSTITUTE WOULD NOT DO. The Chairman: What was Mr. Dukes before coming to Manchester seven months ago ? Mr. Eccles: He was the Warrington branch secretary. Surely among your branch secretaries you have got a man who is worthy of promotion to Mr. Dukes' position? If anything happened to Mr. Dukes you would have to promote some- body ?—Yes, but it would dislocate the whole organisation. We had an instance some time ago of a man throwing up his position and the whole district went down; and, really, when so many of our men are on munition work The Chairman: I think it is very necessary that you should have the post filled—l quite agree—but the only question is whether you have got someone who could fill the post; I don't say that he would fill it as well as Mr. Dukes does, but sufficiently well, at any rate. A Member of the Tribunal: A good many unions have no difficulty in finding suitable men for these posts. Mr. Dukes again broached the question of his personal claim, remarking that he had a receipt that it had been lodged all right, whereupon the Chairman again replied "We have no juris- diction on the personal claim."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>DISALLOWED.</head>
        <p>DISALLOWED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes and Mr. Eccles having retired, the Tribunal considered their decision, and the Chairman having signed a form stating, "The claim is disallowed," a clerk slipped outside the room and handed it to Mr. Dukes, who was standing within easy access.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>R.A.M.C. MEN.</head>
        <p>R.A.M.C. MEN.</p>
        <p>Social To Lord Derby Hospital Men On Leaving.</p>
        <p>A social was held on Thursday in Winwick Chapel as a farewell to a number 'of R.A.M.C. men Who are leaving-the Lord Derby Hospital for the Front. A musical programme had been arranged by Private F. Richmond. Songs were sung by Messrs. G. Richards, "Mate o' mine," "Echo" (as an encore), and "Little grey home in the west"; J. Gibson, "The skipper" and "Rollicking, rolling stone" (encore). Private F. Richmond contributed the following violin solos, "Moto perpetus," "Somewhere a voice is calling," and "tin soir Portici." Humorous recitations were given by Private A. Knott "The wife who sat up" and "Wayward Kitty." Miss F. Ormeston'and Mr. T. Gibson were the 'accOri3panists. "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>JOY IN ONE'S WORK.</head>
        <p>JOY IN ONE'S WORK.</p>
        <p>The idea of joy in one's work has been often ridiculed, but nevertheless it is fast taking root in the minds of many and proving its value and merit. To-day many things are done, many principles accepted, which were con- sidered absurd or not considered at all a generation ago. To perform the day's work joyfully and joyously may not be possible, in cases, without effort, but the fact is being realised more and more that it is very much worth while to develop_ the habit.</p>
        <p>This plan has no limitations of environment. Whether in the home, in the office, in the fac- tory, or at recreation time, you cal take cheer- fulness and willingness and eagerness with you. The day's burdens are lightened, fatigue if, kept at a distance, depression is not given a , chance to get you in its clutch. Not only are you good company for yourself, but for others. Your joy in what you are doing surrounds you and spreads to your associates. Disease is not the only thing which spreads. It has been proved that anger and violent emotion cause for the time being a poisonous condition of the blood, which is injurious. It must be equally true that facing the passing hour with a mouth turned up at the corners and a mind alert for the best that the day offers is beneficial. This is a very good world after all, if we but treat it right. And surely if we face our obligations and our work, not with frowns 134 with gladness, it will give us a more kindly greeting and a helping hand in return.—" Great Thoughts."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>WOULD BE UNEXPLAINABLE.</head>
        <p>WOULD BE UNEXPLAINABLE.</p>
        <p>It Occurred In Earlestomi And Doubt</p>
        <p>Couldn't Be.</p>
        <p>When so many in our own district have again and again given just the same assurances how could doubt of an Earlestown case be explained? It could not.</p>
        <p>_ The very earnestness of the message in ques- tion will convince the most sceptical. Read every word and then pass judgment. On March 14th, 1911, Mrs. M. Haste, of 232, Earle-street, Earlestown, said :—" After my last confinement kidney complaint crept upon me. I gradually got worse, and about fife years ago dropsy set in. My body filled with water and I had swellings all over me. There were swell- ings, too, under ray eyes, and my back caused me fearful pain. I could not think of bending down. If I stooped the pain was unbearable, it seemed as if a sharp knife was being driven into my back. I used to come ove^ so dizzy that I dared not go out. My head a,thed fit to burst. I went to Liverpool Royal InfLanary, where I was told I should have to undergc an operation. I refused, however, and started to use Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, which I had been advised to try.</p>
        <p>" I noticed- an improvement almost from the first, so I took the pills regularly, determined to give them a fair trial. As I continued with the medicine all my aches and pains left me. The swellings gradually disappeare-.4 and I became normal again. Now I feel lam quite cured, end can honestly say Doan's Pills Faye made a new woman of me. I shall always prais, the medicine and hope my experience with Doan's Pills will induce others to try this excellent remedy." (Signed) "M. Haste." On February 16th, 1916—nearly fire years later —Mrs. Haste said :—" I am .keeping wonder- fully well at present. I shall never know how to fully express my gratitude to Doan's Pills for the welcome change they made in my health."</p>
        <p>Of all dealers, or 279 a box, from Foster- McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Don't ask for backache or kidney pills—ask distinctly for Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mrs. Haste had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>Out of Date.</head>
        <p>Out of Date.</p>
        <p>"Bury Married Men and Lord Derby."—Pro- vincial Paper. "A tempting solution of the Government's problem, but perhapS%a little too mediawal these times."—" Punch."</p>
        <p>Investors, 3danufacturers and Merchants should consider the resources of Canada's Largest Province,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>QUEBEC.</head>
        <p>QUEBEC.</p>
        <p>MINERAL PRODUCTS include Molybdenite, Feldspar, Magnesia, Graphite, Copper, Iron Ore Iron Band.• Steel, Asbestos. Hardware, Enamelware, WOodwexe, Paper and Pulp offer excellent opportunities. AGRICULTURAL PRODUCTS include Wheat, Oats, Potatoes and ether Field Crops. Other products include Batter, Cheese, Apples, Tobacco, Live Stock, tic. For particulars apply: Lt.-Col. the Hon. P. PELLETIER, Agent-General for Quebec, 136, Kingsway, London, W.C.</p>
