      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>LOCAL FACTS AND FANCIES.</head>
        <p>LOCAL FACTS AND FANCIES.</p>
        <p>By CRITIC.</p>
        <p>'</p>
        <p>• - •, .. • , ••,.- •</p>
        <p>The Council have vqy thoughtfully—and very rightly—placed on record their appreciation of the services of their 'Clerk, Mr. bitUla.rd.</p>
        <p>Mr. MuHard has been connected w;th the local governing body under its various names since 1875, that is for 41 years. He was made clerk to the old Local 'Board in 'lBBB, and he still survives as clerk to the Urban District Council. May he long SO aurvive</p>
        <p>The scenes at the Council ar not so wise as they are- entertaining, But that is wit Mr. As.. Mr. Gotobed said, members who are ruled by him are "usually on pretty safe ground."</p>
        <p>As I anticipated last week, Lymm was quite a centre of attraction for Easter holiday- makers. •</p>
        <p>And a very good-humoured crowd they were. It was evident by their peculiar manner of speech that most of them came from Lanca- shire. " Tha' knows."</p>
        <p>Warrington was well represented, and the principal road between Lymin and Warrington was kept very busy until quite a late hour each evening.</p>
        <p>Women are now taking a prominent part in the cultivation of the land. "More armlets."</p>
        <p>One of our Easter visitors confessed to me that Lymm, as a beauty spot, was "simply rip- ping." I sincerely hope that it does not rip too much.</p>
        <p>Last Tuesday morning's motto: "Business as usual."</p>
        <p>Never before has a Lymm andiendb been treated to a better display of vocalism than that provided at the Blue Cross concert on Wednes- day evening.</p>
        <p>Miss Ada McHugh was at her best—she is get- ting very well known in Lymm as a charity singer, having appeared at the Drill Hall on several occasions.</p>
        <p>Mr. Tom Green, the fine tenor, who delighted the audience with a fine rendering of "My dreams," confessed to me that this was his first appearance in Lymm. He was the winner of the Albert Hall tenor competition in 1912.</p>
        <p>Mr. Walter Cliffe, who took the duet with Miss Moulton, was the winner of the Botanical Gardens' competition in 1912. In fact, all the artistes seemed to haven record.</p>
        <p>Humour came to its own when Arthur Scho- field took the boards, and the audience were soon rocking in their seats.</p>
        <p>A hint to would-be charity organisers: "Wanted, a whist drive!'</p>
        <p>Why does a Lymm audience always insist on Mr. John Brazendale singing "The water melon "P</p>
        <p>I see that the Council's workmen have been busy this week tarring the roads. More "dirty work"!</p>
        <p>Workers' Social Gathering.—Messrs. W. T. Moss &amp; Co.. of the Lancashire Tool Works, Henry-street, held a aoCial .gathering for their employes on Monday evening, at the Fox Tavern, Booth's Hill. About 40 workers sat down to a hearty repast, and a most enjoyable evening was spent. Amongst the guests could be noticed several soldiers from the Brookßeld Military Hospital. The evening was made quite free and easy and was chiefly devoted to games. Lady Cyclist Collides With Station Gates.—On Monday afternoon a party of cyclists from Newton-le-Willows were proceeding down the station brow. when the braki s of one of the n•achinek failed to act, arJ urfartenately the station gates were closed. She collided with the gates with considerable force, the result being that she shattered her mazhine, and she herself had a miraculous escape. Luckily, how- ever, no serious damage was done, and the lady, after receiving medical attendance, was able to proceed home by. train.</p>
        <p>, lymm Soldier Awaiting Discharge.- Driver G. Leigh, of the A.S.C., who is at present an inmate in the Belton Park Hospital, :31rantbam, has this week informed his sister, who resides at West Hyde, Newfields, that he is awaiting his discharge. Driver G. Leigh is a well-known local man, and enlisted at the outbreak of war, shortly afterwards being drafted to "somewhere in the East," where he fell ill. He was eventually sent back to England. In civil life he-is a steel worker, employed by Win. Robert- son, Latchford. He is also well-known in foot- ball circles, and was a member of the local club. Easter Services.—Special services were held at the Parish Church in Easter week, conducted by the Rector and the Rev. John Waterfield, of Wellington, Salop. The church was beautifully decorated. The usual Good Friday night service was :illustrated, and -Mr. W. T. KOSS worked the limelight.—The Congregational Church ser. vices were conducted by Mr. T. Hall, of Man- chester, and the Sunday afternoon service was thrown open, when the young men of the first class gave a sacred concert. The choir rendered special aiathems. The collection at the concert was devoted to the Y.M.C.A. huts funds.</p>
        <p>Heart Fallure.—Deputy-Coroner A. E. White held an inquest at Dog Bridge, Oughtiington, on Monday into the death of Peter Dodson (49), of Dog Bridge, Oughtrington, who died very suddenly before medical assistance could be obtained.—Evidence was given to the effect that the man had complained of a cold two days prior to his death, but had not consulted a doctor.—Dr. Leonard Thorpe spoke of making a post-mortem examination of the body and said that he .was of the opinion that death re- sulted from heart failure, following pneu- monia.—The Jury returned a verdict to that effect.</p>
        <p>An Easter Wedding.—A very pretty wedding was solemnised at the Congregational Church on Saturday morning, the respective parties being Mr. Bertrand Heyward, of Grove-avenue, late of Birizin3".-am, and Miss Eva Millet, eldest daugE ter of Mr. and Mrs. William Miller, of Booth's Hi:l. The bride wore a crepe-de-chine biscuit-coloured dress and car- ried a bouqu:!*. of orchids. She was conducted cl.own the aisle by,hes- father to 'the strains of the 'Wedding Marc," brilliantly executed by Mr. Albert Leather, the chapel organist. The bridesmaids—the Misses Hilda and Elsie Miller (sisters of the bride)—wore cream-coloured dresses and carried bouquets of pink roses and lilies of the valley. Mr. C. Heyward (brother of the bridegroom) acted as best man. The Rev'. J. D. B. Smith officiated. Flowers were supplied by Mr. George Willan, of hedcot. After the ceremony there was a reception and breakfast was served at the bride's residence. The newly-married couple shortly afterwards proceeded on their honeymoon to Birmingham.</p>
        <p>Found Drowned.—Deputy-Coroner A. E. White held an inquest on Monday on the body of Harold Hampson (37), of The Rookery, Booth's Hill, whose body was found floating in the Bridgewater Canal on Saturday afternoon. —William Hatnpson stated that his brother left his house about 1.30 p.m. on April 6th and never returned. He had been in very bad health for some time and seemed greatly de- pressed:—Thomas Shaw, a youth, who is em- ployed by the _Manchester Ship Canal Co. as a: boatman, stated that while going along the towing-path he saw something floating in the water, and on going back found it to be a human body. He at once communicated with the poliee.—gharles Gatley, licensee of the Bull's Head Hotel, spoke of deceased being in ' his house on the night of April 6th. He had , never at any time heard him say anything ' about doing away with himself.—Acting-Sergt. G. Griffiths gave evidence as to the finding of the body.—ln the absence of further evidence as to how the deceased got into the water, the Suty,TeturneU a- verdict of "Found-dciwn.ed.'l</p>
        <p>• , • V.)14 • . -r,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>TWENTY-EIGHT YEARS CLERK.</head>
        <p>TWENTY-EIGHT YEARS CLERK.</p>
        <p>Lymm Councillor's Tribute To Mr. W.</p>
        <p>sluHard.</p>
        <p>A WONDERFUL RECORD.</p>
        <p>Mr. W:Mullard having completed twenty-five years' service as clerk to the Lymm Urban Dis- trict Council, the members of the, Council, at the annual meeting on Wednesday night, took .e opportunity of paying complimentary tributes to his invaluable work on behalf of the Council and township; and passed a /resolution placing on record their appreciation of his services: Mr. Gotobed, referring to the occasion, said Mr. Mullard had not only been 25 years clerk, but his clerkship went back as far as 1888, as clerk to the Local Board, while the minutes</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>MR. W. MIILLARD.</head>
        <p>MR. W. MIILLARD.</p>
        <p>of the Local Board had been in his hands since 1875, which he described as a wonderful record. The only member of the first District Council who was a member of the present Council was Mr. Mclntyre, and the only surviving member of the first Local Board was Mr. Robert Old- field. Besides Mr. Mclntyre, four other mem- bers of the first District Council were living, namely: Mr. S. Gould, Mr. R. Dewhurst, Mr. Welford and Alderman J. R. Smith.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gotobed went on -to refer to the courtesy with which Mr. Mullard treated every member of the Council, and said he always endeavoured to give to every member, and especially those who occupied the chair, his most valuable services. Although sometimes they chafed under his restrictions as to what was in order, and what was not, they found, as a general rule, they were on pretty safe ground. He moved a resolution'. placing on record• the Council's appreciation of Mr. Mullard's services.</p>
        <p>-Mr. Holt said Mr. Mullard had been a real good servant to the Council for many years, and they hoped he would long be spared to serve them.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mullard, in reply, thanked the Council for its expression of appreciation. He was be- ginning to feel that the time allotted to man was not far off terminating, but he hoped he would be able to be of some service to them for some time to come.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>"BLUE CROSS."</head>
        <p>"BLUE CROSS."</p>
        <p>Concert To. Help Our Maimed And</p>
        <p>Wounded Horses.</p>
        <p>The Congregational Schoolroom on Wednes- day night presented a pretty appearance, when a concert was given to help to swell the funds of the Blue Cross Society. The large room, which was beautifully bedecked with flowers, was very soon filled with an eager audience, for rumours had got about that some noted artistes were to apirar; nor were they disappointed, for the organisers, Miss E. Wright and Mr. Arthur Schofield, had secured the services of some of the finest artistes in the North of Eng- land.</p>
        <p>The proceedings opened with a song by Miss Ada McHugh, " When .you come back," which was rendered in a most efficient manner, her delightful singing very soon putting the large audience in good humour. She was followed by the irresistible Mr. John Brazendale, a well- known local tenor, who delighted the audience with several songs, including "Animals' band," "Water melon," &amp;c. Then followed the recit. aria, "From the rage of the tempest "• (Han- del), by Mr. Walter Cliffe; humorous mono- logue, "How we saved the barge," by Mr. Arthur Schofield (of the Manchester, Liverpool and Birmingham concerts); song, "My dreams" (by request), by Mr. Tom Green, a well-known Manchester artiste; duet,'" The spider and the fly," by Miss Moulton and Mr. Cliffe; "Vianka's song," by Miss Tessie Townley; and a,recital, "How he fell among thieves," by Miss Annie Armstrong. The interval was devoted to the sale of choco- lates, Blue Cross flags, &amp;c., by eight girls— Misses E. Wright, C. Moston, E. Barnes, N. Pearson, N. Brookes, E. Bates and C. Bates— who did a roaring trade, especially• among the wounded soldiers from the Brookfield Military Hospital, who were the guests of the evening. The second part of the programme opened with a duet, "Comrades, to arms!" by Messrs. Green and Cliffe; followed by a humorous sketch, "Water scenes," by Mr. Arthur Scho- field; song, "Nirvana," by Mr. Tom Green; song, "A perfect day." by Miss Ada McHugh; song, "Captain Mac," by Mr. W. Cline; recital, "A reverie in churh,." Miss A. Armstrong; song, "The home flag," Miss T. Townley; and a sketch, "The serial story," by Mr. Arthur Schofield. Miss Bessie Wright officiated at the , _ _ _</p>
        <p>At the qnd of the programme Mr. J. Summers, of Hill Cot. nroposed a vote ef thanks to the artictos tar the PflieiPr war in which they had Conducted the evening's entertainment. This was carried with a rousing cheer from the audi- ence, followed by the singing ef the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>The total profits of the Bine Cross concert realised somewhere in the neigtibourhoO3l of £7 155., and that sum has been handed, over to the society's funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>QUIBBLE OVER A CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>QUIBBLE OVER A CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Lymm District Council Elect Publican For Position.</p>
        <p>There was no unanimity among the members of the Lymm Urban District Council at the annual meeting held on Wednesday night, on the question of the appointment of a chairman for the ensuing year. The names of Mr. Lock-yer and Mr. Holt were proposed. The former's claim to the position was moved by Mr. Parker, who pointed out that Mr. Lockyer had been a member of the Councillor many years, and had honourably filled the posi- tion of chairman on various committees. Mr. Warburton seconded.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Cowper proposed Mr. Holt for the Fosi- tion, and said he had done more for Lymm than any other man. He was a straightforward man, and no one was better suited for the chair. Mr. Gotobed seconded, and when it came to the voting there seemed to be some misunder- standing, and a member wanted to know if they were assembled for the purpose of electing a chairman or not. Eventually, Mr. Lockyer was declared elected to the position. On taking his seat, Mr. Lockyer thanked those members who had.supported him in placing him in that honourable position, although it was not</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>a very thankful office at the present time. He thanked them with the exception of Mr. Cowper, and he could not include him in the thanks. Mr. Cowper had his own ideas and he</p>
        <p>(Mr. Lockyer) had his. It was gratifying to find that he (Mr. Lockyer) had the confidence and respect of the majority of the Council. There was an old saying that a publican was the black sheep of the flock, but after twenty eight years in their midst, vAtilout a 'flaw in his character as regards the Licensing Law, it was most creditable to find that he had met such gentlemen who had placed confidence in him. He hoped that they would be able to carry on the work for the ensuing year in the interests of the ratcpayers,a2?.d fo,r the benefit,of the town- ship-. At the close of his year he trusted that he would have justified the confidence reposed In him, and he concluded with the earnest hope that their deliberations would be harmonious. The Vice-chairman.</p>
        <p>The meeting then proceeded to the election of vice-chairman. Mr. Ingham proposed Mr. Holt mkt Mr. Groves seconded, the propOsition, after being supported by Messrs. Critchley and Parker, was agreed to. Returning thanks, Mr. Holt threw out a hint that they should set themselves out to 'econo- mise during the current year. At Manchester" they were saving on all undertakings. , . The " Extravagant " County Council.</p>
        <p>Then the speaker went on to refer to the Cheshire County Council, and said they ought to try to get that body to curtail their expenditure a little bit. They were very ex- travagant people; were wasting good money, and employing labour which could be utilised elsewhere. The County Council was spending money on education.</p>
        <p>"We have education fanatics on the County Council," declared the speaker, amid "Hear, hears," "and the results are not in keeping with the expenditure. We hope we shall be able to get the County Council to curtail their expenditure on district roads, where money," he contended, "was being unnecessarily spent." Help For Dependents.</p>
        <p>He thought the amounts paid to the depend- ents of soldiers required their consideration, and' that the allotments should only be suffi- cient to make up the wages of the men When in civil life. They had been generous in the extreme in a few cases, and he thought they were justified in .seeing that the wages were made up and equal in every sense, but not in excess.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cowper was in entire agreement with Mr. Holt, and said there was a lot of money spent on the roads which was not necessary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>COUNCIL SCENE.</head>
        <p>COUNCIL SCENE.</p>
        <p>New Chairman Upsets The Harmonious Spirit.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED PERSONALITIES AND INSULTS. The newly-elected Chairman of the Lyram -Urban District Council (Mr. Lockyer) did not commence his year of office under the happiest of auspices on Wednesday night. After nis election to that position he expressed the hope that the business of the Council would be ~n-ducted ducted in a harmonious way, but his wish wah by no means fulfilled, for when it came to the question of the appointment of a chairman id the Finance Committee he upset the harmonious spirit which had hitherto prevailed by making a certain reference to a member.</p>
        <p>The names of Mr. Cowper and Mr. Ingham had been submitted for the position, whereupon the Chairman considered that it was absurd for the Council to.elect a member as chairman oi the committee who had thrown the cheque book across the table. He did not think that that member should hold the position. If they elected that member they would have nothing but unpleasantness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>Mr. Cowper's Attack.</head>
        <p>Mr. Cowper's Attack.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cowper sprang to his feet, a.ecl speaking with• evident bitterness, said: "You have brought me into this. You have been personal to me. I say you are no more fit ro 113 in that chair than a dummy." Mr. Cowper concluded by making a statement regarding the Chairman, who replied that Mr. Cowper had no need to be so .personal. He added whatever was-Mr. Cowper's business, it had nothing whatever to do with the Couneil. Mr. Cowper: I represent people here w ht. are property owners. Mr. Ingham: I should like the business con- ducted in a businesslike manner.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cewper : Why should! oe insulta.l? Mr. Gotobed: It is unfortunate that we have started in this manner.</p>
        <p>The incident then terminated, anl the Coun- cil got to business again, Mr. Irt,ham boing elected chairman.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cowper was asked to accept the vice- chairmanship, but replied that he did not want the position. The chairmen and vice-chairmen of, othe,. committees were elected as follows :—Water Committee: Mr. Groves. Rim&amp; Committee: Mr. Parker; vice-chairman, Mr. Ingham. Sanitary and Sewerage Farm Committee: Mr. Mclntyre; vice-chairman, Mr. Parker. Housing and Town Planning: Mr. Goto'bed. Fire ,Engine and Building: Mr. Warburton. Gas Committee: Mr. Critchley; vice-chairman, Mr. Groves. It was decided that the Council meeting should be held on the last Monday in each month at 7.30. Lights On Canal Boats.</p>
        <p>The Clerk (Mr. MuHard) read a letter from the police superintendent at Altrincham respecting the lighting restrictions, intimating that the Order in operation in the district did not empower the police to restrict the lights in use on canal boats.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Lymm Platoon.</p>
        <p>Saturday.—Manoenvres with Warrington Com- pany at Thelwall. Fall in Drill Hall, 245 p.m. Tuesday.---fl p.m., Shooting. 8 p.m., Platoon Drill and Bayonet Exercise. Thursday.-7 p.m., Shooting. 8 p.m., Platoon Drill and Semaphore Signalling. By order, G. R. WLLLIAMSON, Platoon Commander.</p>
        <p>In the first shooting competition Mr. Rose re- turned the winning card of 90 points, with .k handicap of 15, which equalled 105 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Wedding.—On Wednesday, at the Penketh Wesleyan Chapel, a marriage was solemnised be- tween Frances Mary, second daughter of the late Mr. J. Routledge, and Mrs. Routledge, The Cafe, Warrington-road, Penketh, and Robert James Elder; of Todhill, near Carlisle. Miss Alice Routledge acted as bridesmaid to her sister, while Mr. David Elder performed the duties of best man to his brother. Mr. Rielaard Rout. ledge, of The Woodwards, Station-road, uncle oF the -bride, from whose home the wedding took place, gave her away. The Rev. ,J. Gunnell officiated. During the afternoon 'the newly- married couple left for Cumberland, where the honeymoon is being spent. A large number of valuable and useful presents have been received.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Prize Distribution.—Pcnketh Wes- leyan Chapel held the usual and last Good Friday tea in the Schoolroom. After due con- sideration it has been decided for the future that this annual event a.nd prize distribution shall be held early in the year. 1 large number of friends sat down to tea with members of the Chapel and .Sunday school, and showed con- siderable interest• in the proceedings which fol- lowed. The Rey. H. T. Dixon was chairman. The programme was :—Pianoforte duet, the - Misses E. A. Dunabin and I. Routledge ; hymn by the children; secretary's report, Mr. W. Leigh; solo, Miss E. Teat; treasurer's report, Mr. H. Davies; dream of nursery rhymes by the chil dren; primary report, Mr. F. Davies; action song. "Grace Darling," girls; recitation, Master J. Pusi/l; pianoforte solo, Miss E. A. Dunabin ; librarian's report, Mr. A. Loxley, read by Mr. F. Davies; sketch„ "Annie's Holiday," by the senior girls; ahorus, "Soldiers and Sailors." by children; recitation, boys. The prizes for at- tendance, which require a high Percentage to attain, were presented by Mrs. Jenks. Great credit is due to the undermentioned workers for both time and trouble to rnak the programme such a success: Mrs. Ray, the Misses A. Davies and B. Shaw, Mr.' G. Carman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>PAD GATE.</head>
        <p>PAD GATE.</p>
        <p>Wesley an Chu rc h.—Special music was a feature of the services on Easter Sunday, when the anthems "Now is Christ risen" for mixed voices, and "Hear me when I call" (Barnet) for ladies' voices, were rendered by an aug- inented 'choir, under the conductorship ,t;)f, Mr. W. J. Bate. The soloist at the evening service was Miss Elsie Hewitt, who sang with much feeling, "When I survey the wondrous cross." The preachers were morning, Rev. A. J. Morton; evening, Rev. P. Meson. Collections were made on behalf of church funds. Whist Drive And Dance.—A whist drive and dance organised by the Church Lads' Brigade, was held in the C.E. Schools on Monday even- ing. The proceeds were for the purpose of pro- viding the Brigade with khaki. The prize winners were as follows :—Ladies, Mrs. Dring and Mrs. J. Weston; gents, Mr. W. Peters anti Mr. Povey ; consolation prizes for the longest at a table, Mrs. Booth and Mr. A. Proffit. The prizes were afterwards presented by Mise Turner. Refreshments were provided, the arrangements being in the hands of Mrs. Grounds. Dancing was afterwards indulged in, the pianist being Mr. Wainwright. The M.C.'s were whist, Mr. R. Simpson; dancing, Mr. W. Fryer. There was a good attendance. Providential Escape Of The Vicar.—The ser- vices on Easter Sunday were conducted by the Rev. J. Hirst, assisted by Rev. K. E. Khododad, of Liverpool. At the opening of the morning service, the Rev. J. Hirst read a letter he had just received from the Vicar, the Rev. H. F. S. Collier, in which he stated that on the previous Monday, when returning from the trenches, a shell dropped within 12ft. of where he stood. By the mercy of God it failed to explode or, as the doctor who was with him remarked, they would both have been blown to atoms. He was suffering from shell shock caused by the con- cussion made by the falling shell. In recog- nition of this providential escape Mr. Hirst asked the ..-congregation to join in singing "Praise 'God from Whom all blessings flow." The services were well attended, and the singing of the choir was very praiseworthy. The morn- ing anthem was Simper's "King of kings," and in the evening "As it began to dawn" (Miles B. Foster) was sung. The soloists were Mr. W. Fryer and Miss Ellen Critchley. The collec- tions amounted to nearly £9, which were given to the Vicar as usual.</p>
        <p>Vestry Meeting.—The Rev. J. Hirst presided at the Easter vestry meeting of Christ Church, Padgate, which was held on Wednesday night in the Church House. The general account (which was presented by Mr. Royston (people's warden) showed that from collections for church expenses there resulted £7O 95. 6d., while special collections and those for the sick and poor raised £B4 us. 4d. and £lB 7s. Id. _respectively. The payments showed a total of £B2 10s. 11d. At the end of the year there was a balance in hand of is. 5d., against a debit balance of 16s. last year.—Mr. Gorton proposed and Mr. Stott seconded the passing of accounts, which were adopted unanimously.—A letter from the Rev. H. F. S. Collier was read, who hoped that they (the Church) were getting on all right.—Mr. H. Houghton was elected Vicar's warden and Mr. Royston as people's warden for the ensuing year. The sidesmen appointed were: Messrs. R. Denham, G. Mather, E. Gor- ton, J. Critchley, A. 0. Hi'ditch, E. London, W. H. Houghton, Jump, Howard, Wright, Allen, Houghton, Potter and Antrobus.—Votes of thanks were passed to the auditors, wardens, sidemen, choir, and all other workers.—At the close of the meeting Messrs. Royston and Mather were elected ruri-decanal representa- tives.</p>
        <p>Boy Scouts. Raid By C.L.B.—The newly- formed troop of Boy Scouts in connection with the Wesleyan Church spent a very enjoyable Easter week-end in camp. This was situated in an old barn on B,adley Common, Houghton Green, which was very kindly lent for the occasion by Mrs. Stanier, of Peel Hall Farm. The Scouts paraded near the chapel at nine o'clock on Saturday morning with full equip- ment. A large handcart was packed with pro- visions, cooking utensils and bedding, and it was a very happy band of youngsters, accom- panied by the Scoutmaster and his assistant, that marched through the village, headed by the Union Jack. On arrival at the camp the boys were set to work to put everything in order. Clean straw was provided for the beds in the loft, huge bundles of sticks were gathered for the fires; some of the boys were put on mending the road, others clearing the yard and other minor duties, each one performing their allotted task satisfactorily. A rumour was spread abroad that an attempt was to be made by the Padgate contingent of the Church Lads' Brigade to seize the camp, and that plans had been made of the camp by the intended raiders. The report was disbelieved and, owing to the preparation of meals and a rollicking time in the woods Cluring the afternoon, the incident was practically for- gotten. About eight o'clock on the Saturday evening, however, news was brought to the sentries on duty that one of the Scouts had been captured by the C.L.B. On receipt of this news a picket, consisting of the Scoutmaster and four of the Scouts, was sent out to investigate the matter, which resulted in the release of the Scout and the capture of one of the raider's stal- warts, who was immediately bound with ropes and carried into the camp amidst much cheer- ing. News was brought to the camp regarding the situation and strength of the enemy, and each Scout was allotted to his post, so that in- formation could be given immediately the raiders showed any sign of approaching. All at- tempts to raid the camp, however, were frus- trated. and for over two hours the enemy were kept at bay. Owing, however, to an unfortunate incident, the whereabouts of the raiders was dis- closed, thus giving the victory to the Scouts. The raiders were then invited into the camp, and short congratulatory speeches were ,made by Captain Simpson for the C.L.8., and Mr. Plins- ton, Scoutmaster, for the Scouts. Captain Simp- son then called for cheers for the Scouts, which were heartily reciprocated by the troop. The Scouts then lined .up on either side of the entrance to the camn, and the C.L.B. went back to headquarters with the hope of "better luck next time."</p>
        <p>After breakfast on Sunday the boys attended church parade at the Wesleyan Church, return- ing to camp for dinner, the remainder of the day being spent in leisure. Numerous visitors insnected the camp on Saturday and Sunday, and on Monday morning the Rev. A. .T. Morton conducted a short service. The clearing up of the camp took a considerable time, but sham fights were arranged on the common and in the woods, which were entered into with spirit by the boys. After tea the boys returned home, after spending a most enjoyable week-end.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>PRIVATE G. TAYLOR.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE G. TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>Killed Whilst Leaving Front</p>
        <p>Line Trenches.</p>
        <p>Unofficial news has' reached Mr. and Mrs. G. Taylor, 12, 'Dundonald-avenue, Stockton Heath, that their son, Private George Taylor, of the Cameron Highlanders, has been killed in action. Lance-Corporal H. Marsh, of the same regiment, wrote on April 22nd, to Mr. T. Tay- lor, 30, Derby-road, Stockton Heath, as follows: "It is with very deep regret that I write to inform you that your brother, George, was killed on the night of the 20th inst. (Thursday).</p>
        <p>PRIVATE GEORGE TAYLOR. Killed.</p>
        <p>His _company was just leaving the Front line when the enemy commenced to shell the posi- tion, and your brother was one of the unfortu- nate ones to be hit He suffered no pain, death being instant. I know that this will be a very big blow to you, and I cannot express my feelings, for George has been my chum for many weary months out here. May God give you strength to bear this terrible shock, for it was God's will that he should die, and we must all submit to His will sooner or later. It might console you to know that he died a hero's death, giving his life that others might live. He was buried with full Church of Eng- land rites, in. a little cemetery set „aside for British 'soldiers. Please convey my sympathy to your parents in their sad bereavement, and -may God help and%comfort you all in your terrible loss."</p>
        <p>Private Taylor, who was 33 years of age, was employed for 16 years at the Stockton Heath Spade Works. He joined the Army in Septem- ber, 1914, and in July, 1915, was scalded whilst lathe trenches, going back to duty in October.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>EASTER VESTRY.</head>
        <p>EASTER VESTRY.</p>
        <p>Illness Of Mr. Lee, Retiring Vicar's</p>
        <p>Warden.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. Collins (vicar), presiding at the Easter vestry for St. Thomas' Church, Stockton Heath, on Tuesday, referred at the outset to the absence through serious illness of his warden, Mr. T. Jackson Lee. Mr. Lee had been present at every vestry meeting since he (the Vicar) came into the parish ten years ago; and had be- fore his illness expressed the desire not to accept office this year, as he thought honours ought to go round. He had been a strong Church sup- porter, and would be greatly missed. Those who attended the inaugural meetings in connection with the free will offering scheme would remem- ber the great amount of trouble Mr. Lee took in obtaining useful information, and a great deal of the success of the scheme was due to his efforts. They all deeply regretted his resigna- tion. _</p>
        <p>Mr. James Richardson (people's warden) said that only those who were closely associated with Mr. Lee knew the full extent of his interest in the church. Mr. Lee performed many acts of kindness, quietly and unostentatiously. Mr. I. Hallows moved and Mr. Richardson seconded a vote of sympathy with Mrs. Lee and family in their time of anxiety and sorrow, which was carried. Offertories Shou!d Increase. • Mr. James Richardson presented the statement of accounts, showing that the year began with a balance in the bank of £6 16s. 11d. The general offertories amounted to £209 17s. 9d., as compared with £193 2s. Id. last year, while the special offertories totalled £75 Os. 11d., as com- pared with £lO3 Os. 3d. The Hospital Sunday collections realised the record sum of £2O 19s. 3d., which was particularly gratifying, while £lO 7s. 11d, was contributed to the funds of the British Red Cross Society. The income (including £3O 16s. 10d. from the parochial tea) totalled £322 12s. 5d., as against £328 14s. 9d. last year. In- surance showed an increase of £l7 ss. 6d., which was accounted for by the taking out of a policy against damage by aircraft, and increasing the sum for which the church was insured against fire. While in the present circumstances the balance sheet might be regarded as satisfactory, they should aim at raising sufficient by means of the church offertories to cover the standing cha,rzes for church maintenance. 'Unless they did that, they could hardly consider they were on a sound basis. The collections for church expenses had kent up pretty well, but they were not adequate; They averaged .C 4 17s. 6d. a Sun- day, whereas, in order to clear themselves, they ought to raise at least £6 a Sunday.—On the nronnsition of Mr. T. Jackson, seconded by Mr. A. Worrall, the accounts were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>Elections.</head>
        <p>Elections.</p>
        <p>The Vicar nominated Mr. William Mclntyre to serve as his warden for the coming year. He said Mr. Mclntyre had been associated with him in many ways, and they had worked•most har- moniously together.—Mr. Mclntyre, in accept- ing office, said he shared in the sympathy and grief at the enforced retirement of Mr. Lee. He (the speaker) had been associated with St. Thomas' parish for about 30 years, and had always regarded it as a privilege to take some part in the furtherance of church work. Mr. James Richardson was re-elected people's warden on the motion of Mr. F. Berry, seconded by Mr. F. Acton.—Mr. John Hallows, as the oldest member of the congregation present, sup- ported, and said Mr. Richardson had given satis- faction to all.—Mr.. Richardson, in returning thanks, welcomed Mr. Mclntyre as his new colleague in office, and said that gentleman's work for the schools alone was sufficient to entitle him to the honour which had been done him.</p>
        <p>The following sidesmen were elected: Messrs. A. Down, T. R. Westbrook, A. J. Willett, J. Melbourne, Thomas Jackson, A. Worrall, J. Swinton, S. McDowell, Albert Broady, P. Gandy, F. Pearson, F. Warburton, F. Berry, 3. H. Fletcher, J. Hallows, R. H. Smith E. S. Taylor, H. G. Dowling, J. B. Partington, P. Broady, A. Stannard and F. Acton. Messrs. W. Mclntyre and F. Pearson were re-elected lay representatives. A Satisfactory Easter.</p>
        <p>The Vicar thanked the congregation for the Easter offering, which amounted to £lB, an advance on last year's. He also expressed his pleasure that the number of Easter communi. cants (517) was greater than 12 months ago. He also 'expressed his indebtedness• to the church,. chool s officers, choir, district visitors, Sunda)? teachers, subseril,era to the free will offering,</p>
        <p>and all church workers, for their kind and ready co-operation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing let May, 1916, issued by Mr. Frank Warburton, O.C. Monday.—Drill at Headquarters, 7.45 p.m.</p>
        <p>Tuesday.—Musketry Practice at Appleton Rifle Range, 5.30 p.m. Thursday.—Drill at Headquarters, 7.45 p.m. To-day (23th April).—lnter-manceuvres. Fall in Victoria-square, 3 p.m. Dress: Uniform, with kreat coats slung; and with haverSacks,rolled. Extract From Battalion Orders.</p>
        <p>Army Form 3250.—The Central Association of Volunteeer Training Corps intimate that the War Office, issue a certificate (Army Form W. 3256) to be completed by the Officer Commanding a unit in which attested men have undergone voluntary training prior to being called up for service. This form provides for particulars to be furnished of the total hours spent in volun- tary training, including musketry; and ask whether his Commanding Officer recommends the man called up for Non-commissioned Rank. Attested men are cordially invited to join th.?. Stockton Heath Detachment with a view of qualifying for privileges outlined in the above form. Recruits squads will be formed any night for which there is sufficient demand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>VVALTON.</head>
        <p>VVALTON.</p>
        <p>Vestry Meeting.—The Vicar (the Rev. L. J. T. Darwall) presided at the Easter vestry meeting, which was held on Easter Monday. The church- wardens' accounts showed .a balance of .211 19s. 2d. in hand.—The Vicar nominated Sir Gil- bert Greenall, Bart., as his warden, and Mr. John Cooper was re-elected as people's warden. The six sidesmen were re-electea.—The sum of £3 19s. sd. was handed to the Vicar as an Easter offering.—Mrs. Darwall was thanked for occa- sional help at the organ, and votes of thanks were also accorded Mr. 'Booth (organist and choirmaster) and the choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>Vestry Meeting.—The Rector (the Rev. H. V. Pigot) presided at the Easter vestry meeting, held on Tuesday.—The Rector appointed Mr.,H. S. Layland as his warden, and the vestry se- lected Mr. 0. Lofthouse. Mr. R. W. Wilde was appointed the Rector's sidesraan, and the fol- lowing were appointed sidesmen by the vestry: Messrs. J. E. Ashcroft, T. Brierley, J. H. Darby- shire, W. W. Dimmelow, A. Evans, 3. Leigh, H. N. Morrey, H. T. Nicholls, W. Peacock, T. Sharrocks, N. Smith, H. A. Thompson, T. Whyman, W. Winstanley and W. A. Wooding.— Mr. A. Evans and Mr. H. A. Thompson were appointed lay representatives. A vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Arthur Down for his ser- vices as organist on Sunday afternoons and on many other occasions.—The regret of the vestry at the death of Mr. William Lomas, a sidesman of the church, was recorded in the minutes.</p>
        <p>Concert—At the Grappenhall Independent Methodist Church on Thursday, a good concert was given by the members of the Physical Culture Class, who were also helped by "The Jovials," quartet party. The concert, which took the form of a pierrot troupe, was a com- plete success, the proceeds being devoted to com- forts for " Tommies " now serving at the Front, members of the above Church. The opening chorus was taken from Tipperary Land," in which all took part "The. Englishman" was rendered very ably by Mr. F. Scarisbrick, who also sang "It did go," causing much laughter. "The Mountains o' Mourne" was sung by Mr. W. Fairhurst, and "Mary's Reply" by Mr. C. Leigh. The encore of the evening was given to Mr. Harold lienshall in his song "Richard of Taunton Dean." Mr. F. llBlt sang "'Listen to My Tale of Woe." Messrs. A. Davies and Is.. Griffiths sang the duet "Army and Navy." The latter also sang "The Tar's Lass" and "The. Longshoreman." After a brief interval and a few remarks by Mr. Henshall, "The Jovials " Quartet Party gave " Toothache,- " Regular Royal Queen" and Good Evening." Each member of "The Jovials " sang two solos, with the exception of Miss M. Blackburn, who sang "Little Grey Home in the West." Miss C. Rigby sang "When You Come Home" and "Annie Laurie." Mr. A. Davies sang "Phil, the Fluter's Ball" and "Up from Zummer- zet," and Mr. G. Walker sang "Tom o' Devon" and "Somewhere a Voice is Calling." The concert was much enjoyed, and to put a finish on the whole the troupe gave a sketch entitled "The Darky Parish Scounsil " very ably, and causing roars of laughter. The accompanists were Mr. Cyril Clayton and Mrs. Foreman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week ending May 6th:— Monday.—Musketry, at the Range. 7 p.m. to 8 p.m., Practice and Class Firing. 8 p.m., "Daily Express" Medal Competition. Open to all members. Not more than six men from each Section can be accommodated at a time. Section Commanders to arrange accordingly. The competition will be open until the 12t1 May. Tuesday.—Company and Squad Drill; fall-in at Headquarters at 7.45 p.m. sharp. Friday.—Musketry, at the Range. 7 p.m. to 8 P.m., Practice and Class Firing. 8 p.m., "Daily Express" Competition. See above for particulars. Saturday.—Parade (uniform) ; fall-in at Headquarters at 3 p.m. sharp. Orderlies.—Section Commander F. Short, Private W. H. Podmore.</p>
        <p>N.B.—The O.C. desires to call special atten- tion to the Concert on Thursday, 4th May, in aid of the funds of the detachment, and hopes everyone will do all possible to ensue its suc- cess. Members attending will wear their uniform.</p>
        <p>F. A. FROST, O.C. Headquarters, 29th April, 1916. VISIT TO ASTLE PARK.</p>
        <p>On Easter Monday the detachment, 23 strong, under the command of Platoon-Commander F. A. Frost, joined in the field day held in Astle Park, Chelford, the home of Colonel Dixon. The detachment, with whom were Quartermaster- Sergeant Read and Platoon-Sergeant *Hutchings, started shortly after 9 o'clock by motor lorry, kindly lent by Messrs. Peter Walker and Son. In the morning there was a sham fight and in the afternoon battalion drill. The Knutsford Battalion is 900 strong, but only 400 attended owing to a large number being engaged on war work in different parts of the division.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>BURTON WOOD.</head>
        <p>BURTON WOOD.</p>
        <p>Funeral of the late Mr. James Briers.—Very, much regret has been felt at the sudden death of Mr. James Briers, of Brook House Farm, Burtonwood. Mr. Briers collapsed suddenly whilst in the field from heart failure and passed away immediately. He had been under medical care for some time, but his death came as a shock Ito his many friends. Mr.itriers,who had reached the age of 57, was a native of Halewood. Some years ago ho removed to Liverpool, where he entered upon the business of a hay and straw merchant. He took up residence at Brook House Farm five Years ago, and has gained general esteem. The funeral took place at St. Mary's Burial ,Ground, Great Sankey. The Rev. George Hayward, of Hindley, son-in-law of the deceased, conducted a brief service at the house, and read the service at the burial ground. Members of the family present at the funeral were: Mr. James Briers, Mr. Thomas Briers, Mr. Henry Briers, Mr. Edwin Briers (sons), Mr. Richard Briers (brother), and the Rev. George Hayward (son-in-law). Among a large number of friends and neighbours present were: Mr. David Howard, Mr. Thomas Howard, Dr. McGibbon (Liverpool), Mr. Thomas Parker (Liverpool), Mr. Thomas Prescot, Mr. Herbert Allman, Mr. George Pickering, Mr. Thomas Pickering, Mr. John Coulter, Mr. Robert Mawdsley, Mr. John Cartwright and Mr. Nor. I cott. Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons carried out the funeral arrange!nente. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>FAILED TO REPORT.</p>
        <p>Length Of Residence That Qualifies</p>
        <p>For Soldiering.</p>
        <p>Two strangers to Warrington, Geo. Dickson and Pat-rick Darley, were brought up on remand it the Borough Court. on Saturday, charged with .failing to report themselves under, the Military Service Act. The Chief Constable said prisoners were 'arrested at the Workhouse, and as they said they did not come within the provisions of the Service Act, he (the Chief Constable) had made inquiries and had received the following tele- gram from the Under Secretary to the Home Office It is for the magistrates to decide on the evidence whether the defendants have in fact become ordinarily resident in Great Britain, within the meaning of Section I of the Military Service Act. It appears to the Secre- tary of State that as these men came here more than three months ago with the intention or working here, they must now be admitted to be subject to the provisions of the Act." Prisoners were handed over to a military escort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>CASUAL LABODRER.WHO PLEADED THAT</head>
        <p>CASUAL LABODRER.WHO PLEADED THAT</p>
        <p>HE WAS I LL.</p>
        <p>At the Itorough' Court on Thursday, Thomas Ryder (33), .8; Dolman's-lane, was handed over ta the military authorities on a charge of having failed to report himself under the Military Ser- vice Act.</p>
        <p>, Accused, who IVO employed as a casual labourer at a local works, was arrested by uetective-Sergeant Beddington, to whom he admitted he was a single man. He said he had not reported himself because he had been ill, and had been under a doctor. The Chief Constable said accused did not notify the military authorities that he had been ill and medically attended. Accused was also fined 40s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>HIS 102nd APPEARANCE.</head>
        <p>HIS 102nd APPEARANCE.</p>
        <p>John Heaton, of- no fixed abode, made his 102nd appearance at the Borough Court on Monday, charged with being drunk and dis- orderly on Saturday night. The Mayor reminded prisoner that ha Promised in the early part of the week to keep off the beer over Eastertide. One month was the sentence of the Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>WORKMEN'S THEFTS.</head>
        <p>WORKMEN'S THEFTS.</p>
        <p>What Inquiries_ By Warrington Police</p>
        <p>Led To.</p>
        <p>Thefts from the works of- Messrs. Rylands Eros.. Chnreh-street, led to the appearance of two workmen at the Borough Police Court on Monday. _ _</p>
        <p>Michael Bird, 14, Dial-street, was fined 20s. or 14 days for stealing within the past two months 31b. of soap and quantity of wire, value Is. 6d. The Chief Constable said Detectives Redding- ton and Summers were making inquiries respecting the theft of a dynamo valued be- tween £2O and £25 from Messrs. Rylands Bros.. and they visited prisoner's home, where they found the property mentioned in the charge, and which prisoner admitted having stolen from Messrs. Rylands's works. Edward Arnold Dykes, of 35, Hopwood-street, was fined 40s. or one month, kr stealing a vice, two electric switches and quantity of cable, value 405., from Messrs. Rylands.</p>
        <p>The circumstances were similar to the first case, the Chief Constable adding that prisonv had used the electric cable for experimenting Purposes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>NOT AN ACQUISITION.</head>
        <p>NOT AN ACQUISITION.</p>
        <p>Chief Constable's Opinion 01 Recruit</p>
        <p>For Army. Wm. Corcoran, a stranger, of no fixed abode, was brought up on remand at the Borough Court on Tuesday charged with failing to report himself under the Military Service Act. The Chief Constable stated that accused was before the Court last week and the case was adjourned in order to complete inquiries as to how long he had been resident in this country. It appeared that accused had worked in Scot- land from December 12th to the 31st, and consequently there could be no doubt as to his liability for service. . He (the Chief Constable) had received a communication from the Home Secretary, intimating that it was left to the Justices to decide whether the accu%ed came under the provisions of the Military Service Act. Accused had evidently come over from Ireland, and according to his record, he (the Chief Con- stable) did not think accused would be an acquisition to the Army. He was 33 years of ago and single.</p>
        <p>Accused as handed over to the military anthoxities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>IN THE PENITENTIARY.</head>
        <p>IN THE PENITENTIARY.</p>
        <p>Labourer Who Regretted Offensiveness</p>
        <p>To Police.</p>
        <p>Abusive to the police when they inquired about a light shining from a bedroom, James Rourke, labourer, 9, Dial-court, was penitent at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, but was fined 20s. on charges of using abusive language and for failing to obscure the lights at his house. In the early hours of Sunday morning P.C. Bartley found a brilliant light shining from a bedroom at defendant's house, and casting a reflection on a mission room in Cockhedge-lane. When defendant's attention was called to it he used abusive language. He refused to let his wife lower thought. In the evening the officer paid a second visit to the house to get defend- ant's correct name, and he was again very abusive.</p>
        <p>Defendant expressed regret, and the magis- trates told him that had it not been for his Penitent attitude that day they would have in- flicted a heavier fine.</p>
        <p>street. . Two further charges for ofiences under the Registration Act were preferred against accused.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said accused called at the house of Mrs. Coyne on the 17th inst. and repre- sented that she was employed by the Grovern meat in the Motor Transport Section. In consequence of a breakdown with the motor she was obliged to stay in the town and she required lodgings. Mrs. Coyne allowed her to stay at the hthise, but the next morning, becoming sus- picious, she discovered that prisoner was walk- ing about the Park, apparently with nothing to do. Prisoner gave a false name on the regis- tration form. In the case of Mrs. Morgan, prisoner told a similar story, and also gave a false name on the registration form.</p>
        <p>Visit To Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable further explained that accused visited Stockton Heath and represented herself to be a Government sanitary inspector. She was also engaged at a local cafe, but was dismissed. She had also stayed at a local clergyman's house. The Chief Constable described it as a. bad case. Accused had had every opportunity to behave herself, but would not do so. She had seen previously convicted at Llandudno.</p>
        <p>On hearing the sentence, accused curtly replied, "Thank you; good marting."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>OFFENDING MOTOR LORRIES.</head>
        <p>OFFENDING MOTOR LORRIES.</p>
        <p>Barrister Complains 01 Delay In Serving Summonses.</p>
        <p>Another batch of motor lorry drivers appeared at the Borough Court on Thursday, charged with driving to the public danger in the narrow por- tion of Sankey-street and Liverpool-road on the 28th and 29th ult. Fines ranging from 20s. to 40s. were imposed. The driver of a steam lorry was fined 10s. or a similar offence.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. H. Spafford (barrister, of Manchester), appeared to defend in the case of Henry Birt- wistle, of Bank Gate, Broadbottom, Dunkin. field, who was charged with driving a motor lorry to the public danger. Counsel at the outset asked that the police should be called upon to identify Birtwistle as, the driver of the lorry in question. The Chief Constable said it was a peculiar argument on the part of counsel. He men- tioned that it was the bounden duty of the owner of the car, when requested by the police, to supply the name and address of the driver of a ear on a particular date, and if the man was wrongly summoned, the owner was at fault. Counsel said the police had strictly to prove the identity of the man. •</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said they had a good many cases similar to that one. They could not say that it was John Brown or so and so who was driving the car.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spofford -Saki his complaint was that his client did not receive information respecting the alleged _offences until three weeks after.</p>
        <p>The Chid Constable: Will you tell the mini- trates that on oath? It is quite a false state- ment to make.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spafford said he made the statement on instructions received.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said if that was the de- fence to be put forward, he would tell counsel where he (the Chief Constable) got the informa- tion respecting the name of the driver and his address. He would also call witnesses clearly to establish the police's position. He had taken nu end of trouble to trace defendant. Counsel said he would waive his objection in order to avoid expense.</p>
        <p>Evidence was then called, and the police alleged that defendant drove a lorry along Liverpool-road at a speed of 15 miles per hour, which was dangerous to the public, considering all the circumstances. Counsel for the defence said he had never been accused before of making a false state- ment.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable here intervened and said he wished to convey that the statement was incorrect. He had no idea of conveying any- thing else. It was the last thing in the world he had thought of saying to counsel. Mr. Spafford, continuing, said Ms client knew nothing about the alleged offence until twenty days after the occurrence. He thought the summons should be served within a reasonable time in order that witnesses could be procured. When such a long period elapsed it was difficult for a man to remember if he passed through the town at a particular time on a certain date. The Bench decided to convict, and a fine of 20s. was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>TEA AND CHANGE.</head>
        <p>TEA AND CHANGE.</p>
        <p>A Female Anent With A Bad Memory. At the Borough Police Court yesterday, before the Mayor and other magistrates, Stella Davies, 23, Ashton-street, Pembroke-place, Liverpool, was remanded for a week on a charge of steal- ing Is. 3d. from Mrs. Blanche Wilkinson, 29, Amelia-street, on April 20. _</p>
        <p>• The Chief Constable said that the prisoner was employed as an agent by a Liverpool tea firm, who visited Warrington and surrounding districts. On the day in question accused called on a Mrs. Wilkinson, who was persuaded to pur- chase a quarter pound of tea for 71d. As she had no less change than 2s. 6d., the accused was given the coin to get change. Before leaving she left another packet of tea, value 74d., and absconded with the half-crown. The matter was reported to the police, and prisoner was arrested by P.C. McDonald when in Warrington yesterday.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable pointed out that the police had had numerous complaints from resi- dents about the business methods of tea agents, who, he alleged, sold an inferior quality of tea at top price, and offered money bonuses to pur- chasers.</p>
        <p>Defendant said she tried to get change for the woman, but failed to do so. The Chief Constable: Yesterday she denied selling any tea to Mrs. Wilkinson, or any knowledge of the half-crown.</p>
        <p>Defendant: I have a bad memory. I don't remember denying it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>WANTED HER CHILD.</head>
        <p>WANTED HER CHILD.</p>
        <p>DOMESTIC SERVANT SENTENCED. Posed As Transport Driver And Police Court Sequel To Rostock Street Government Sanitary Inspector., Scene. Alice Vernon, 47, Algernon-street, was fined A young domestic servant, Alice Evans, a ss. at the Borough Court yesterday for using stranger to Warrington, was, at the Borough obscene language in Bostock-street to Bertha Court on Wednesday sent to prison for six Ball, 88, Grafton-street. months on two charges of obtaining food and The trouble arose over the custody of the lodgings by false pretences from Enamel Coyne, defendant's child, which, owing to being neg- -44, Parker-street, and Agnes Morgan, 89, Bewsey- lected, was, on the instructions of Inspector Wainwright, of the N.S.P.C.C.' placed in the care of Mrs. Ball, for which she received ss. weekly from the defendant. The child was sent by Mrs. Ball on an errand to a shop, when the defendant came along and carried it away. Mrs. Ball succeeded in overtaking the defendant, and regained possession of the child. The defend- ant then became very abusive, and used threat- ening language to Mrs. Ball. The husband of the defendant, a sergeant in the Army, said he told his wife to apply at the Police Court for the child, instead of taking it by force. Later the defendant applied to the magis- trates for the custody of her child, and she was recommended to interview Inspector Wain- wright.</p>
        <p>A FAMILY SQUABBLE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>An Altercation About Kissing The</head>
        <p>An Altercation About Kissing The</p>
        <p>Baby.</p>
        <p>• At the Borough Police Court -yesterday, Thomas and Mary Barker, 26, Eldon-street, were summoned for assaulting Catherine Flem- ing, 10, Back Crossley-street, on Good Friday, whilst Elizabeth Ellen Cunningham, 19, Bank- street, was summoned for using abusive language.</p>
        <p>COmp-lainant stated that on Good Friday night she called at the house of Mr. and Mrs. Barker for her baby, whom she had left whilst she went for some chip potatoes. An altercation arose about kissing the baby, and words led to blows. Mrs. Barker struck her first and wit- ness retaliated. Whilst engaged struggling with Mrs. Barker, she was struck in the face by Mr. Barker, and the blow gave her a black eye. Defendants alleged that Mrs. Fleming was the aggressor. Mrs. Barker said matters were coming to something if they had to allow people to assault them in their own house. They might as 'well let the Germans come over.</p>
        <p>The defendants were bound over to keep the peace for six months, and pay the costs, ss.</p>
        <p>.• s .</p>
        <p>k • " '</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 29, 1916.</p>
        <p>son; Ashworth, Whitfield and Jardine; Connor, Illidge, Millward, Stubbs and Taylor.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. A. Clare. The visitors won the toss and defended the pavilion goal, having a strong cross wind slightly in their favour. Best set the ball roll- ing, and their advance was repulsed oy a good return from Cwkson. Then. after a few ex- changes in midfield, Cunliffe shot over the bar. The visitors led an attack on the home goal, and Hinde and Siddall cleared in turn. Broglen. securing the, leather, beat the visitors' backs, and had not much difficulty in beating Mather with a low shot after five minutes' play. Im- mediately following the centre-kick Cookson was Penalised for hands, and the visitors' goal 04 as placed in danger from the ensuing kick, Cun- liffe being a shade too wide with a low shot. A raid on the home goal resulted in a shot from Whitfield bouncing over the crossbar over one of the home players. The ensuing corner-kick was not improved upon. Good combination by the visitors enabled them to attack again, but Siddall relieved by good judgment, and a visit to the visitors' goal saw Mather make 2 fine clearance from a centre by Cunliffe. A little later Fallows was just too wide with an over- head.kick. The home team were keeping better control over the ball, and their forwards were giving the 'visitors' defence—plenty of wolk to do, and a good shot by Fallows was put over the bar by Mather. The visitors now had a turn at the home end, where Millward, when pre- paring to shoot, was neatly robbed by llinde, and directly after Millward missed an easy opening with a good centre by Taylor. Illidge and ;Whitfield.. had good shots at, goal, but. Mallinson sayed well on both occasions. Play was now trar:sferred to the visitors' end, and from a centre by Fallows, Leah attempted to head the ball away, and diverted its course out , of the reach of Mather, and the ball entered the net for the second time for the home team. Then the home team returned to the attack, and the visitors' goal had a lucky escape owing to several of the home forwards missing the ball in front of an open goal. The interval was called with the score :—Altrincham, 2 goals; Warrington, 0. After a lengthy interval play was resumed, and The visitors made the first advance, which was smartly repulsed ny Hinde. The home for- wards, set going with a good pass from Siddall, Drinkwater shot hard into the side net. A dangerous movement by the visitors was spoiled by offside against Connor. For it while the ex- changes were chiefly in the visitors' lkqlf, and they gained relief by a free-kir, tubbs nd Taylor transferring to the home end, the _hitter was easily outplaced by Hincle. Good play be- tween Illidge and Connor resulted in a fruitless corner-kick. Cunliffe and Brogden were promi- nent by their tricky play; but the final efforts of the former were charged down and cleared. After a spell of quiet play in midfield thg visi- tors assumed the aggressive, but their forwards did not appear able to take the opportunities offered to score. A corner-kick resulted in Brogden adding the third goal. Following this reverse, the visitors made a strong rally, and Connor made several good attempts to break througii, but his efforts were defeated by Siddall and Hinde in turn. After another spell of even play in midfield, Cunliffe and Brogden initiated an attack on the visitors' goal, but the ball was headed over the bar. Following good play by Illidge and Millward, Taylor kicked wide when well placed. Following a throw in near the home goal line, Drinkwater gained possession and broke away and scored the fourth goal. Result :—Altrincham, 4 goals; Warrington Town, 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION</p>
        <p>Bad Day For Visitors.</p>
        <p>Of seven matches played in the N.Th League on Saturday, five were won by home teams. Rochdale lost their last home match of the season, Leigh beating them by a penalty goal; while St. Helens also went down at home to St. Helens Recs. Wigan, at home, beat Hudders- field, after a fine game; while Leeds, Hull and Dewsbury all secured easy victories. Results Leigh, 2 points; Rochdale H., 0. Wigan, 15; Huddersfield, 10. St. Helens R., 11; St. Helens, 3. Bradford, 13; Hunslet, 4. Dewsbury, 10; Halifax, 7. Hull, 28; Swinton, 0. Leeds, 33; Barrow, 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>INTERMEDIATE RUGBY LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Widnes Avenge Good Friday's</p>
        <p>Defeat.</p>
        <p>Having beaten Widnes at Wilderspool. on Good Friday by 23 points to -7, Warrington Intermediate League team made a journey to Widnes on Monday. Their team differed in two instances from that which did duty at home, the two forwards, Dumbell and Harter, being replaced by Mathews and Sutton. A large and enthusiastic crowd travelled with Warrington and made themselves. heard throughout the match.</p>
        <p>Mathews started for Warrington, who were soon attacking, the Widnes full-back having his kick charged down. Play hovered on the Widnes line for some time, until the forwards broke away and forced a dead-ball. Upon the restart good combination by the Warrington lads saw the,i Widnes line again besieged, Broad- hurst .being 'tackled in the nick of time. Aided by the wind, Widnes attacked, but Brown re- lieved finely. Widnes again attacked, but the Warrington forwards ruslled the ball to mid- field. Hosker received; Sand, putting in a good kick, followed up and scored at the corner. The goal-kick failed. Griffiths was hurt and left the field for about fifteen minutes, Jones going full-back and Percival half-back. Half- time: Widnes, 1 try (3 points); Warrington, nil.</p>
        <p>Widnes restarted and were soon attacking. Hooker being all but through. Good forward work, however, relieved matters, and War- rington invaded their opponents' territory, Broadhurst being held by sheer weight of numbers. The attack was kept up, and ulti- mately Matthews went over at the corner, but the goal-kick failed. Later, Gilmour marked for Widnes, and Hooker placed a goal. From now until the end mid-field play was the order, and the final whistle sounded leaving Widnes the victors.</p>
        <p>Result: Widnes, 1 goal 1 try (5 points); Warrington, 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>TO-DAY'S FINAL.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S FINAL.</p>
        <p>St. Thomas' And Rylands's Teams Try Conclusions At Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>St. Thomas' and Rylands's meet to-day in tha final for the Intermediate League, and a fine game is sure to be witnessed. Both teams have played remarkably well lately, especially the first named, whose victory over Parochial in the replayed semi-final was well deserved. Rylands's victory over Heathside was no " fluke " either, and we may rest assured that each team will put forth every effort to win the match. Mr. R. Jonei, of Widnes, will be the referee, and the Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock) has kindly consented to present the watches to the winning team at the close of the match.</p>
        <p>Interviewed by an " Examiner " representa- tive, Stan Young, the Warrington half-back, who takes a great interest in the Stockton Heath team, said he was confident that St. Thomas' would win, if they would play their own game, for they had a strong team out. Wibberley, one of their best forwards, is now in the Army, but as he has obtained leave this week-end he will appear to-day. Lloyd also appears at left- centre, and he is always an attraction, having played for Rochdale N.U. team several times this season. Of this player, Stan Young expects "great things," and thinks he will turn out into a first class player.</p>
        <p>Of the Ryianils's team he thinks they are a fine sporting team, and looks forward to a great match between the two. We are informed that Parochial recently protested against the St. Thomas' team playing two players—Lloyd and Sankey—who they alleged were over age. The objection was, however, overruled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>Teams.</head>
        <p>The Teams.</p>
        <p>St. Thomas'.—Fitzgerald; Marsh, Lloyd, Cawdwell and Noden ; Berry and Leah; Glegg, Hooper, Sankey, Wibberley, Clarke and Rylance. Rylands's Rec.—T. Archer; Latimer, Little- wood, Britton and Hill; Savoury and Swanton; Dumhell (captain), Sutton, Barker, Lee, Mulvey and Butler. Reserve, Thomas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCH.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>B. JOLLEY'S TEAM v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Teams representing Runcorn and Ben Jolley's Warrington team met at Runcorn on Easter Monday. The home team were early attack- ing and were soon four points up, .T. Jolley kicking two penalty goals. Shortly afterwards the visiting backs handled smartly .and sent in Callon for a try, J. Jelley failing at goal. Just on half-time J. Jolley kicked another penalty goal, and the interval found Runcorn leading by six points to three. Soon after the restart some fine passing by Jolley's team saw Runcorn hard pressed, but the danger was soon cleared. Jolley's team came again, however, and Hayes scored a gcod try, J. Jolley failing. Baker and Tranter made good efforts for the visitors, which, how- ever, came to nought. Result: Runcorn, 3 goals (6 points); Ben Jolley's team, 2 tries (6 points). Jolley's Team: Praater; J. Baker, Flanagan, Moores and H. Baker; J. Jolley, and Callon; G. Fearnley, Townend. Jolley, Moores, Logan and Hayes.</p>
        <p>Runcorn: F. Jolley; J. Jolley, Lightfoot, Conlon and Woodward; Hoxworth and Banner; Richardson, Pickstock, Spruce, Rogerson', Wil- liams and IL Jolley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>TOWN DEFEATED.</p>
        <p>But Put Up Good Fight Against</p>
        <p>Stalybridge.</p>
        <p>WarringtOn Town have had a 'busy holiday, playing three matches over the Easter week- end. The club only managed to taste the sweets of victory once, that being on Good Friday, when they accounted for Glossop in an easy fashion, scoring eight goals to one.</p>
        <p>This runaway victory gave hope that the club's visit to Stalybridge on Saturday would not be fruitless, and that at least one point would be captured. The -Town's hope, how- ever, was not fulfilled, and at the end of ninety minutes' play they had to admit a heavy defeat by five goals to two.</p>
        <p>The Town found the Celtic in an irresistible mood, and at times the forwards gave a bril- liant exhibition of football, reminiscent of the days of their appearance in the Southern League. In meeting the Celtic the Warring- tonians were up against one of the best teams in the Combination, and, all things consid-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>r ...""' i..., t , , . • ... . . -, • • – • -.. .4...- '. ...... f• . i--.:*.i.„. .</head>
        <p>r ...""' i..., t , , . • ... . . -, • • - • -.. .4...- '. ...... f• . i--.:*.i.„. .</p>
        <p>CR ICKET.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>PARTINGTON C. &amp; I. CO. v. WARRINGTON TOWN RESERVES.</head>
        <p>PARTINGTON C. &amp; I. CO. v. WARRINGTON TOWN RESERVES.</p>
        <p>Teams representing the above clubs, met last Saturday at Irlam, before a good crowd of spec- tators. The home team were very strongly represented, including several First Division players, whilst the visitors were composed of local players desirous of making their mark in the higher circles of the game. An interesting game was witnessed, and the spectators showed their appreciation of the finer points of the Soccer game as shown by the more experienced players. The home team having the best of the exchanges, were leading at the interval by three goals to nil. In the second portion the home team con- tinued to have the best of the game, and scored on four more occasions before the final whistle blew. The visitors showed a weakness in their final efforts, and many good openings went abegging. The winners were best represented by Pimblott, Foster, Humphries and Green, the latter scoring four goals. The visitors were best served by Parr, Deakin, Meachin and Crozier. The receipts at the gate and from the sale of tickets realised about £l2, which was in aid of S. Birch, one of the home players, who was in- jured on March 26th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT MEDAL COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT MEDAL COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Replayed Tie.</p>
        <p>WINWICK ROVERS v. WIDNES ST. MARY'S This replayed tie was played on the grbund of the latter, before a good crowd of spectators. The visitors won the toss and played' with a strong breeze in their favour. From the kick- off ,they made the first advance, but the " Saints' " defence was on the alert and saved the situation. The " Saints " retaliated strongly, but the visitors cleared after hard pressure. Some clever combination was shown by the visitors' forwards, and the home defence had some difficulty in clearing their, lines. After a brief respite the Rovers returned to the attack, and the home custodian was defeated from close quarters. The " Saints " made a strong attempt to get on equal terms, but at length the visitors' defence cleared their lines and the forwards quickly transferred play to the " Saints' " goal, when a second goal was obtained. Strenuous play by the home for- wards was rewarded, a stinging shot being sent in from the left, and the visitors' custodian failed to clear. The visitors were not to be dis- heartened, and they immediately returned to the attdck and scored their third goal. The home forwards made furthei• attempts to reduce their arrears, but their shooting 'was very erratic. Ralf-time :—Winmick, 3 goals; Widnes, 1 goal.</p>
        <p>The home team, with the wind now in their favour, were expected to improve upon their previous efforts, and the final shot in their at- tack was yards wide of the goal. The Rovers then transferred to the home end, where the home goalkeeper made a splendid clearance. The " Saints " ,again put on pressure and gained several corner-kicks, but were unable to improve upon them. The Rovers then broke 'away and scored again, but the point was ruled offside. The home team were now making their' strongest efforts to score, but were met by a stubborn defence, and their goal was saved from further downfall. At the call of time play was in midfield. with the result :--Winwick Rovers, 3 goals; Widnes St. Mary's, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>TEAMS. FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS. FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL. INSTITUTE v. WIDNES ATHLET:C.</p>
        <p>Medal competition, at Sankey (Atherton's Quay).</p>
        <p>Institute Team: Knight; E. Crazier rind S. Hughes; T. Hughes, P. Parr and W. Ayres; H. Wainwright, Walter Eaton, J. Bromilow, A. Meachin and Wm. Eaton. Reserves.--G. Bailey, S. Banner, W Knight. Teatm to meet at Rylands-street 2.10 p.m. ear. 'Kick-off 3.15 p.m. prompt.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT MEDAL COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>A meeting in connection with the above com- petition was held last Tuesday night at the Mersey ,Hotel, Mr. G. Bamber presiding over a good attendance of delegates. The Secretary gave a statement of at:counts up to date, which was accepted as satisfactory. Then followed a report of the replay:A ties dur- ing the holidays, Widnes Athlettl ieating Sankey Atliletic, and .Winwick Rovers defeat- ing _Widnres St. Mary's.</p>
        <p>The semi-final tie; will be decided to-day and are as follows:</p>
        <p>St. Elphin's V. Winwick Rovers, ct Victoria Park. kick-off 3:15. Referee, Mr. Merriman, of Widnes.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Institute V. Widnes Athletic, at Atherton Quay. Kick-off 3.30. Referee, Mr. G. Ember.</p>
        <p>It was resolved that if these games are definitely decided, the final tie *ill be played next Saturday on the Atherton Quay ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <p>.</p>
        <p>labs.</p>
        <p>AT HOOD-LANE TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Stalybridge pay a return visit to Rood-lane to-day (Saturday), and a great deal of interest is centred in the match. The Celtic have to win this encounter to make them champions of the Combination, and no doubt they will make a bold bid to follow up last Saturday's victory. It is most likely that .Billy Meredith, the 'famous international, will play for the Celtic. The Town team will be: Kite; Leah and Cook- son; Ashworth (Manchester United), Whitfield and Bullen ; Connor, Ilidge, Millward, Taylor and Owen..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>CHARITY MATCH NEXT SATURDAY.</head>
        <p>CHARITY MATCH NEXT SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Just a reinainder to football enthusiasts. On Saturday next, May 6th, a big charity match has been arranged between Warrington Town and the 15th King's Liverpool Regiment. The proceeds are for the Mayor's War Fund, and his Worship, Alderman Peacock, has promised to kick-off. Football supporters will be in- terested to know that the 15th King's Liverpool Regiment. team includes several well-known pro- fessionals, and is a crack Army team. 'Don't forget May 6th; the cause is a worthy one, and deserves a bumping gate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM v. WARRINGTON TOWN. The above teams met in a friendly encounter on Easter Monday, at the Moss-lane ground, be. fore 500 spectators. The home team had the assistance of }Linde at full:ow:lc. mho is at home on furlough, and a trial was given to Fallows at outside-right. •For the visitors, Mather depu- tised for Kite in goal, and Ashworth and Jardine were played in the intermediate line, and Stubbs; a local player, was given a trial at inside-left. The following players turned out: Altrincham : Mallinson; Hind° and White- head;n Aldred, Siddall and Richmond; Fallows, Drinkwa ter, Best, Erogde and Cunliffe. Warrington Town : Mather; Leah and Cook-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>AMONG THE LOCALS.</head>
        <p>AMONG THE LOCALS.</p>
        <p>ered, the Town did very well in managing to penetrate the Celtic defence on tWo occasions.</p>
        <p>To the credit of the' Town let it be said that they drew one of the largest "gates" seen on tlie Celtic meadow this season, and it is some satisfaction to the Town Club managers that with a re-arranged team a very good genic bras placed on record.</p>
        <p>The score might lead one to believe that the whole of the exchanges went in favour of the Celtic, but this was far from being the case. The Celtic had the best of the exchanges in the first half, but when ends were changed a new lease of life seemed to come over the Town team and they turned the scales from a playing point, but had no luck from the scoring point of view. The Town had the, home team practically pinned in their own half during this portion, but were unable to wipe off the advantage gained by ,the Celtic in the firs' half.</p>
        <p>The Celtic had two or three breakaWays, and they were successfully, carried lout; two goals being notched by a really clever forward line, who knew how to take advantage of oppor- tunities when they came their way.</p>
        <p>Respecting the trend of the game it should be mentioned at the outset that the Warring- tonians had to make changes in the side that piled on eight goals against Glossop. Milling- ton and Connor came in in place of .Aspinalt and Bentley, while Cookson was drafted from the defence to the centre-forward position, which was certainly not a wise move. At the interval, however, Cookson changed and let Millward lead, with far better results.</p>
        <p>The whole of the first half was in favour of the Celtic, 'out the Warrington defence stood the series of attacks very well, and Kite in goal showed good form. The game was 15 minutes old when the Celtic, took the lead, and it was the result of a fine forward movement, Moseley 1 eing presented with 4 neat opening, of which Le ttok advantage.</p>
        <p>Continuing to show up well, the Celtic van- guard kept the Town defence, hard pressed, and Kite more than once distinguished himself. Moseley added the Celtic's second goal, fol- lowed soon afterwards by a third from Pike, the interval arriving with the Celtic possessing a comfortable lead.</p>
        <p>Resuming, the Town showed what they were capable of doing, and the Celtic defence was put on its mettle. They made things hot, the forwards taking up the attack wholeheartedly and with any amount of determination. Capital forward play resulted in Millward tak- ing up the running and from fifteen yards got a lovely goal. Soon after the Town had an- other chance of reducing the lead, a penalty being awarded against the Celtic, but Cookson failed to find the net from the penalty kick. The Warringtonians were not long, however, in making amends for the mistake, for Owen reduced the Celtic lead after a clever effort.</p>
        <p>Warrington failed to maintain their form, the pace having told its tale. ' This gave the Celtic their chance to make further headway, and they did not fail to do so, two goals being added in quick succession by Lockett and Beavers.</p>
        <p>The Town retired a well-beaten side, but ,the. players deserve congratulation for the bold try they made to pull the game out of the fire. Just a few words about the players individu- ally. Kite in goal played a good game. Leah and Cookson showed their true defensive qualities. Millward showed up best in the forward line, and it was- a wise move on Cook- son's part to return to his old position to make room for Millward in the centre-forward posi- tion. Whitfield was the pick of the half-backs, and all the forwards gave satisfaction.</p>
        <p>Warrington Town: Kite; Millward and Leah; Millington, G. WhitfieT3 and Bullen; Illidge, Taylor; Cookson, Owen and Connor.</p>
        <p>_ Stalybridge Celtic: Johnson; Lockett and Brookes; Dickinson, Robinson and Francis; Brindle, Pike, Moseley, Beavers and Berten- shaw.</p>
        <p>On Easter Monday the Town CluE were not at full strength in their friendly fixture at Altrincham. They were defeated by four clear goals, a result which was a fair criterion of the game. The home team showed far better com- bination than the visitors, Cunliffe and Brogden being very prominent with their tricky Play, which led to 'many attacks on the visitors' goal. Siddall, at centre-half, proved to be in good form, and his dispray in this position was very satisfactory to the home club's committee.</p>
        <p>On the visiting team Cookson was a very hard worker, especially in the first half, when he received little assistance from Jardine at right. half. The half-back line was only moderate.. The forwards at times combined well in mid- field, but their final efforts were weak and easily repulsed. Mather, who was deputising for Kite in goal, could not be blamed for the shots that beat him, and during the course of the game he made some fine saves.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District Medal Com- petition the preliminary tie and the first round have provided some keen games, and in three of the ties extra time has had to be played before a final issue could be arrived at.</p>
        <p>As a coincidence of the keenness and rivalry which exist between the Central and the Sankey Athletic Clubs, they were drawn to- gether in the preliminary tie and had to play three hours and a half before the Sankey Club came out of the contest as winners. Then the Athletic., had to meet• the Widnes Athletic on Good Friday. and after a keen and strenuous game the Widnes team won by two goals to mine. Thus at the conclusion of the first round the teams who were considered favourites for the competition were both dismissed, and the result of the semi-final ties, which are arranged for to-day are being followed with much in- terest by the supporters of junior football in the district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>Matches Wanted.</head>
        <p>Matches Wanted.</p>
        <p>Rylancles Recreation C.C. (good ground and dressing accommodaticn) wish to arrange matches with respectable clubs within a nine miles radius of Warrington. All dates open. Apply T. Knight, hon. sec., c/o Messrs. Rylands' Bros., Ltd., Dalton Bank Works, Warrington. Emmanuel C.C.—Walter Eaton, 22, Marson- street, Warrington, secretary Emmanuel C.C.,• will be pleased to receive dates for the coming season, commencing May 13th, 1916.</p>
        <p>NOTE.—The Editor of " The Examiner" will be pleased to hear from secretaries of Cricket</p>
        <p>Club 6 as to their arrangements for'the coming</p>
        <p>season. He will also welcome scores and notes on the game as soon as the season opens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>BOWLS.</head>
        <p>BOWLS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT BOWLING ASSOCIATION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>Fixtures for 1916.</head>
        <p>Fixtures for 1916.</p>
        <p>May 20th and 27th.—lst Round Championship.• June 3rd and 10th.-2nd Round Championship and Ist Round Consolation. Juse 17th and 24th.-2nd Round Consolation. July Bth, 15th and 22nd.—War Fund Handi- cap. _ _ _</p>
        <p>August sth and 12th.—Semi-final Champion- ship, Semi-final Consolation.</p>
        <p>August 19th and 26th.—Final Championship. September 2nd and 9th.—Final Consolation. Registration of players will close on Saturday, May 13th. Council meeting, King and Queen Hotel, Padgate-lane, Tuesday, 2nd May, at 7.30. Business: New clubs and draw for champion- ship.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>BEHIND THE COUNTER.</head>
        <p>BEHIND THE COUNTER.</p>
        <p>‘Var's Effects On Shop Assistants' ork And Wages.</p>
        <p>The twenty-fifth annual conference of the National Amalgamated Union of Shop Assist- ants, Warehousemen, and Clerks was opened on Wednesday at the Cory Hall, Cardiff, Mr. G. W. Burrows, of Wakefield, presiding over at attendance of about 140 delegates. In his address the President said that no less than 40 per cent. of the eligible members had already enlisted, and it was estimated that with the calling up of the last attested groups not more than 5 per cent. of eligible members would remain in civil employment. It would be folly to ignore or minimise the many diffi- culties that would surround them when those now with the colours again returned to civilian life. Whatever might happen in other spheres of life, women would be employed in largely. increased numbers behind the counter, and in this respect the operation of their minimum rate of wages must be their chief concern. The question of the proportion the women's rate should bear to the men's was a mixed one, and would remain so, he feared, for all time. The rates' question could not be permanently solved until the hours' question had been dealt with. To radius agreements and the living-in system they must continue their unalterable hostility. The annual report,of the Executive said that, despite the War, the past year had been a re- markable one from the trade union point of view. Though it was estimated that nearly. 20,000 of the association's members had been suspended from contributions owing to having joined the colours, the union had stood the financial strain exceedingly well, and was to- day in a sounder position than ever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>Poached.</head>
        <p>Poached.</p>
        <p>A tourist, travelling through County Galway, found it necessary to put up at a small farmer's house for a few days. On arrival in the dining room the first morning he found the daughter of the house in attendance, , who courteously in- quired what the gentleman would like for break- fast. He requested some poached eggs and bacon.</p>
        <p>The girl left the room, but returned in a few minutes, and,, leaning across the table and dis- creetly lowering her voice, she said: "My father is very sorry, sir, he has no poached eggs in the house, but he has a little poached salmon, if that would do!"</p>
