      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Egg Production.</p>
        <p>That amusing tit-bit in the poultry column last week sent my mind back over the winter through which we have come. At the outset of the period I had the good fortune to discover a poultry-keeper who proved to have "plenty of big brown eggs right through the bad weather," and who, moreover, made it a matter of con- science never to sell fewer than seven for a shilling. He said that if he were a buyer he should always insist on seven as the minimum, and the public at large ought so to do. Now one can more than tolerate that sort of con- scientious'objector these times.</p>
        <p>Science and the Fancy. I was in East Lancashire the other day, and, having to wait a while for a car, I stepped into a little wooden hut wherein was a clogger at work. In the course of conversation I asked the man. concerning his hobbies. and it turned out that these included gardening, poultry and bee keeping, photography, and star-gazing: He satisfied me that his attainments as an amateur photographer were really high, that he was an intelligent and well-informed student oT astronomy, and that in horticulture and the rest (intensive included) he was an old hand. He told me some amazing things about egg-pro- duction in the Burnley district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>"Careers for Educated Women."</head>
        <p>"Careers for Educated Women."</p>
        <p>tinder this heading "Marjorie" suggests that of newspaper reporter for one and that of hospi- tal almoner for another. It would hardly occur to an up-to-date writer on the subject to include that of governess. And yet the term may now- adays imply more than has been commonly understood by it. I know a young lady who has this very week sailed for Russia, having been engaged to teach English to the two daughters of a merchant at Petrograd. That is excellent, and the more we have of it the better. I am also acquainted with the foreign correspondent for a_ commercial house who is busily adding Russian to the half-dozen languages with which he is familiar. Assuredly he is wise in thus taking time by the forelock. Indescribable Meanness.</p>
        <p>The allusion in the same paragraph to the abuse of medical charities reminded me of an unspeakable person of whom my friends at the Manchester Eye Hospital have told me. On presenting himself for treatment as an in- patient, he certainly looked the poor body he represented himself to be. The secretary passed him, but subsequently ascertained that he owned considerable property. Nothing was said until, at the end of six weeks' doctoring and special dieting, the fully restored patient came to leave the institution. Then the grave official handed to him a bill for an adequate sum, and of course-it was in vain that the fellow protested; indeed, when quietly informed as to the alternative, he was glad to escape with pay- ment. School Baths.</p>
        <p>Last week's incidental reference (vide "The Children's Friend ") reminded, me of three of my old districts having bath's for school chil- dren. In the case of one small town the authorities arranged for the use of the baths by the general public out of school hours, and, there being no other local provision of the kind, the concession was keenly appreciated. Prior to that, swimmers who disdained the canal or other open-air water had either to take train nine miles in one direction or walk 51 in another to enjoy their favourite and exceedingly whole- some exercise.</p>
        <p>A Hint. In some hints for bathers which I cut from an old magazine this occurred :—" Never urge or drive children into the water against their will. Let them get used to the idea of bathing, and they will gradually learn to love it." As I read this I thought of a time, between 50 and 60 years ago, when my father took my brother and me to the Westminster Baths. He led us to the big swimming bath and forthwith pitched us both into deep water, leaving us to be " rescued "by other bathers. I made a great hullabaloo about it there and then, and, look- ing back at the incident over the long half-cen- tury. I am still of opinion that it was a mis- take on the part of my well-meaning old dad. "Air in Bedrooms."</p>
        <p>I cordially agree with the writer of last week's "Home Note" on this, but perhaps some older or ailing folk do not find it quite so simple a matter as the young and vigorous are apt to assume. I think of a sanatorium for the treat- ment of consumptives which I once visited while tramping through Yorkshire. The situation was ideal, and the provision for the sufferers was generous in the extreme; besides, the phthisis was in many cases unmistakably over- come, but the severe open-air treatment left several patients with rheumatism. It occurs to me that people afflicted with that complaint may not be able to sleep with bedroom windows open. Anyhow, they must find themselves be- tween Scylla and Charybdis.</p>
        <p>Preparing for Warm Reception. The cheerful paragraph in last week's "Gossip" respecting the gentlemeh who are able to flourish their own death certificates in the faces of the unlucky doctors who signed them prematurely and without due warrant, set my thoughts on a different tack. I know at least one medical man who quite seven years ago obtained the certificate and made other ar- rangements for his own cremation after death. As I long ago made up my mind that my body shall ultimately be thus disposed of, he urged me to do the like, pointing out in his light- hearted way that it meant a reduction of ,a couple of guineas in the fees.</p>
        <p>Sir Ryland Adkins. The characteristic letter which this M.P. for the Middleton Division (whose seat may one eay be contested by a Unionist from these parts) addressed to a Liberal meeting briefly reported in these columns last week reminded me how a few years ago a young lady friend (I suppOse the "r " in this might be omitted without serious impropriety) begged me to obtain his signature for her album. She Indicated the space reserved for it, and I-duly pointed this out on a page already signed by some other members of Parliament. But Sir Ryland took his time about it, and very deliberately examined the book from cover to cover. In turning over the pages (and it was worth while, for there were some remarkably . pretty little landscapes in water-colours and clever pen and ink sketches therein) he came upon one occupied by the Shake- spearian phrase, "A poor player," followed by the name of an eminent tragedian. Sir Ryland at once hit on this as a suitable model, and, selecting his own separate page, Ile wrote, "A House of Commons man," adding his signature with a proud flourish. Volunteers at Mess.</p>
        <p>The account of the Stockton Heath V.T.C.'s gathering at the Mulberry Tree Hotel flooded my mind with recollections of dinners which I attended in the days of the old Volunteers. There was the same good old misquotation, and the immortal and absolutely indispensable "jolly good fellow." As for the songs an the stories, I could reproduce several of them here and now, but exigencies of space oblige me to hold over my selection.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>Slit ROGER CASEMENT.</head>
        <p>Slit ROGER CASEMENT.</p>
        <p>Sir Roger Casement has acquired remarkable notoriety since the outbreak of war thrthigh his attempts to associate Irishmen with the cause of Germany. He was born in September, 1864, and between 1895 and 1913 he held positions as British Consul in Lorenzo Marques, the Congo Free State, and Para and as Consul-General at Rio de Janeiro. He was the exposer of the Putumayo atrocities. During the later months of last year British prisoners of war returning from Germany gave several descriptions of the attempts made by Sir Roger Casement to induce Irish poisoners to join an Irish Brigade. Private Moore, of the R.A.M.C., who landed at Tilbury on October 7th, said that Irishmen in the camp at Limburg had been starved with th eobject of getting them to join this brigade. They were visited by Sir Roger. who read a document stating what he in- tended to do for Ireland, for of the 2,400 men in the camp fewer than 50 were ready to listen to him, and these few were of no character and not fit to be soldiers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>"FIGHT FOR RIGHT"</head>
        <p>THE "FIGHT FOR RIGHT"</p>
        <p>MOVEMENT.</p>
        <p>To Hearten And Strengthen Each Other.</p>
        <p>(From a Correspondent.)</p>
        <p>The war drags on its weary course from recnth to month, and it is no confession of wca4l-ness to admit that everywhere men and women are feeling the tremendous strain. No one thought at the outset that it was humanly possible to continue this gigantic struggle so long, or that it would be -necessary; and no one realised what it would mean to the spirit of the nation, or how we were all to be tested and tried.</p>
        <p>From the beginning sacrifice's have had to be made by all, and these have grown gradually greater and greater, yet they have been made with patience and courage inspiring to behold. The story of heroic endurance manifested in every home, from the greatest to the humblest, can never be told. No pen could ever describe the courage, self-denial, and passionate devo- tion of the fathers and mothers, especially the mothers, of our nation; and it is impossible even to do more than catch a glimpse of all the suffering and anxiety they have so patiently borne. Whatever may be the gigantic evils of war, and they are frightful to contemplate, there is no denying the fact that we have realised how great can be the capacity of our people for self-sacrifice on behalf of a noble ideal. To Sustain Our Efforts.</p>
        <p>As the strain of the war becomes greater, as it is likely to do in the near future, it becomes more and more necessary that men and women should find some means of encouraging and cheering each other, of helping each other to bear their respective burdens, and Of hearten- ing and strengthening each other in the com- mon struggle. We all need the comfort and help which flow from the realisation that we are united in a great cause, and that we have undertaken a work which will be of great service to the whole of humanity. The "Fight for Right" Movement is the only one, amongst all the movements and societies to which the war has given rise, which definitely sets out with this object in view. It has been founded by Sir Francis Young. husband, and the declared object is "to heighten and sustain the spirit of the nation in the long, struggle against Germany—a struggle in which this country is fighting, not for her own safety and honour alone, but for the inter- ests of the whole world, for a right ideal of free civilisation against a wrong ideal of organ- ised power—the battle of humanity." The President is the Right Hon. Viscount Bryce, 0314 and the Chairman of the Execu- tive Committee is the Right Hon. Sir Frederick Pollock, Bart. Amongst those actively engaged in the movement are the following: Sir Henry Newbolt, Professor Gilbert Murray, Dr. L. P. Jacks, Dr. J. E. McTaggart, Prof. Caroline Spurgeon, Mrs., Stuart Moore (Evelyn Under- hill), Mr. Gervase Elwes, and many others.</p>
        <p>Decisive Victory To Lasting Peace.</p>
        <p>Sir Frederick Pollock, in a short article in the January number of thq "Hibbert Journal," explains more fully what the "Fight for Right" movement is, and what it hopes to do. He says: "The, purpose of the Fight for Right' movement is to encourage our fellow- countrymen, as well non-combatant as combat- ant, to use their utmost endeavour, in the sev- eral ways open to them, towards the end of attaining decisive victory as the only sure means of honourable and lasting peace; to maintain in them the spirit of bold confidence in a righteous cause, without which full Suc- cess is not possible; and to make plain to them the unexampled character of this war, being a war not merely for British interest, but for the freedom of the civilised world, as a reason for the most strenuous effort and for ungrudg- ing sacrifice." Further, he Says: "Being avowed propagandists in a cause which we are convinced is just, we make no pretence of being judicial or impartial. Our primary question is not: Is this war righteous? hut: This war being righteous, what' is the right spirit for winning in a greater contest than any liv- ing man remembers? Much has been done in the way of historical and political instruction; the conditions leading to the war have been explained and discussed in many forms of pub- lication and from many points of view. Little attention has yet been given to the men and women, probably a large number, who do not need further argument, being satisfied in their judgment, hut do need moral encouragement. Some are indolent in mind, seeing the facts, but not seeing the consequences. Others are indolent by disposition and take refuge in diffi- dence: what should their little effort matter among so many? Not a few are sound enough on the root of the matter, but too easily cast</p>
        <p>down by idle rumours or worse than idle depre- ciation of our leading or resources. We want to bring spiritual help and encouragement home to all these; to make them not only know but feel that, if this is a long war, the end we seek is worth all our patience; if it is a hard, war, victory is worth all our effort and endur- ance; if it is a war of cruel losses, peace and security for our children are worth all the sacrifice."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>Kill Croaking !</head>
        <p>Kill Croaking !</p>
        <p>One other quotation must be given, for it puts the whole matter in a nutshell. He says: "There is only one party to which we are opposed; and that is not a party, as that foolish people that dwelleth 'in Sichem' was not a nation. It is the congregation of the croakers and the scolders, the amateur generals who never handled a rifle, the correctors of naval strategy who have never been to sea, the dictators of foreign policy who know no lan- guage and no country but their own, all of them crying out how much better they could have cone the business themselves, and, what is more and worse, crying out for the enemy to hear. We have nothing to do with their motives. Those may be not only patriotic, but disinterested, for anything we positively know. Good intentions, however, will not ex- cuse those who execute them by doing their best to create an atmosphere of gloom, depres- sion, and distrust; certainly they will not excuse libel of any kind, much less libels which a century ago would have been held seditious, if not treasonable." Will Warrington Move?</p>
        <p>No definite steps have yet been taken in War- rington to establish a branch of this movement, but something ought to be done, for -there is as much need here, as in other places, to keep the great issue well to the fore, and to remind one another what is the purpose of the suffer- ing and the sacrifice of these days</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>SOCIALISTS SPLIT.</head>
        <p>SOCIALISTS SPLIT.</p>
        <p>Warrington Chairman Raised A Devil He Could Not Lay.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. E. SeaburY, of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council, was chairman of the fifth annual conference of the British Socialist party, which was opened on Monday in the Caxton Hall, Salford. The meeting was marked by turbulent Scenes and the defection of a body of 30 delegates. It was officially stated ,that the question of admitting the Press and public would be raised immediately after the election of a chairman. Within a few moments it became apparent to those waiting at the doors that there was la distinct cleavage on the issue, and when a vote was taken the figures were greeted with a great shout by the majority, who had pronounced for a secret session. They followed up their victory by singing "The Red Flag." __ _</p>
        <p>At this moment the docir 'of the conference room burst open, and Mr. H. M. Hyndman and 29 other delegates walked out.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hyndman's Explanation.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hyndman and his friends afterwards met at the Deansgate Hotel to consider the position. Mr. Dan Irving was voted to the chair. Mr. Hyndman's speech made it clear that the two sections of the conference, de- scribed later on as the "pro-Allies" and the "Pacifists," came to loggerheads on the ques- tion of admitting newspaper men, and that the tumult arose on the discussion of an aniend-' went to the original motion for exclusion. Hyndman said he had risen to protest against being closured, but was not allowed to reply to attacks upon himself. The men who had taken this attitude he denounced as desti- tute of a sense of decency and as ignorant of the rights of public meeting. One of his opponent; had made one of thejnost outrageous charges conceivable—that he had been spied upon, and that Hyndman and Irving had been in communication with Scotland Yard for the purpose of getting certain of their comrades incarcerated. The Chairman refused to listen to a protest against an accusation Of this pre- posterous character. AS a man who had been 35 years in the movement, Mr. Ilyndman had claimed "a fair show," but it had not been given him. The Chairman had raised the devil and had not been able to lay him after- wards. The resolution excluding the Press and public had been adopted by 76 to 28. The com- rades who were acting with him were repre- senting men of the party now at the Front, and hut for the fact that those men were on active service the decision would have been different. He now invited suggestions as to their future course of action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>"Saving Their Skins."</head>
        <p>"Saving Their Skins."</p>
        <p>Mr. Jack Jones (London) said it was not the old democratic party that was struck with fear as to what might happen to them if they con- ferred in public, but the men whom they had left had one idea, and that was the saving of their own skins. He moved "That the action of the delegates in protesting against a Pro-</p>
        <p>posal to hold the conference in camera be approved and that those present pledge them- selves to justify the attitude they have taken up."_</p>
        <p>-Other speakers were Mr. Hunter Watts, who condemned the behaviour of "anti-national pacifists"; Councillor Lothian (Carlisle), who said the better part of his branch were fighting in France, but not as conscripts; and Mr. Killip (Leeds), who reported that 120 members of his branch were in the trenches, and who ridiculed the conduct of "bellicose pacifists" at Caxton Hall.</p>
        <p>Mr. Headingley (London) accused the pacifist section of playing the part of pro-Germans, and of availing themselves of every opportunity of reproducing arguments from Berlin. The resolution was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>RIVAL POLICIES.</head>
        <p>THE RIVAL POLICIES.</p>
        <p>The resolution upon which the split actually occurred was as follows :</p>
        <p>"That the longer the war lasts the more com- plicated and insoluble become the issues in- volved and the more remote the possibilities of a satisfactory peace; and further, that the mili- tary destruction of either side or the exhaus- tion of both must inevitably lead to a peace that will be merely temporary and contain the menace of future wars."</p>
        <p>This was adopted by 61 votes to 17. It was explained by the seceders that 'their withdrawal was occasioned by an "accidental majority of delegates, such majority being dua to the fact that our own supporters are engaged at the front, are occupied in military training, or are scattered through the country working overtime in munition works." The resolution on the agenda which we should have supported and voted for had it been possible for use to remain in the conference and put our views pub- licly is the following:— "That the British Socialist Party is strongly convinced that the future of Socialism and the preservation of such freedom and democratic in- stitutions as were enjoyed in Western Europe before the war depend on the victory of the Allies and the liberation of Germany from rile thraldom of Prnssian militarist tyranny."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>Mr. Seabetry's Presidential Address.</head>
        <p>Mr. Seabetry's Presidential Address.</p>
        <p>Mr. Seabury, in his presidential address, we understand, stated that in consequence of having ' given expression to their conviction some of their comrades were now suffering in a prison .cell in segregation from their friends and families. They were witnessing at present a tendency to revert to the condition of child labour like that which obtained in the early part of the nineteenth century. It was a duty incumbent upon every individual to resist the reduction of the school years of the child and to strive for a further increase. This would allow a development of brain capacity which, if attracted into their movement, would assist in securing that consciousness 'among their class that was so essential to the achievement of their ultimate objective.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>CEMETERY.</head>
        <p>THE CEMETERY.</p>
        <p>Fewer Burials, But An Increase In Receipts.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Young, Cemetery Registrar, in the 59th annual report, which deals with the year ended March 31st, says the receipts amount to -£1,570 2s. 6d.; corresponding period last year, .11,479 17s. 10d. There have been interred during the past year 1,170 adults and minors, and 90 stillborn children and premature; corFespending number last year, 1,227 adults and minors, and 111 still- born children and premature.</p>
        <p>This shows a decrease a 57 buiials, a de- crease in stillborn children of 21, and an in- crease in receipts of .290 4s. Bd. Freehold graves sold during the past year, 290; corresponding number last year, 291.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>Sources of Income.</head>
        <p>Sources of Income.</p>
        <p>Amount received 'for purchased graves, £466 10s.; excavating, £368 15s. (d.; subsequent in- terments, 4289 10s.; unbought graves, £62 4s. 6d.; tolling hell, £44 us.; commission on new stones, '74 Os: ed..; placing new stones, £44 e " 145.; taking up and re-placing stones, £2B 125.; lettering stone, £B9 13s. Bd.; extra fees, morn- ing burial, .214 10s.; stillborn children and premature, £7 155.; sale of grass and plants, £26. 3s. 6d.; brickwork profit, 42 12s. 6d.; bier and ,burial certificates, 9s. 4d.; tiling and re- tiling, 450 ls.; total, .41,570 2s. 6d.</p>
        <p>Ages at Death.</p>
        <p>; Between one day and one year, 190 one year and three years, 111; three years and 5 years, 39; five years and 10 years, 51; 10 years and 15 years, 32; 15 years and 20 years, 42; 20 years and 25 years, 27;. 25 years and 30 years, 32; 30 years and 35 years, 34; 35 years and 40 years, 55; 40 years and 45 years, 51; 45 yeirs and 50 years, 53; 50 years and 55 years, 63; 55 years and 60 years, 69; 60 years and 65 years, 81; 85 65 years and 70 years, 66; 70 years and 75 years, 84:. 75 years and 80 years. 57; 80 years and years, 23; 85 years and 90 years, 9; 00 years and 95 years, 1; total, 1,170.</p>
        <p>STAGE AND KINEMA.</p>
        <p>Royal,Court.</p>
        <p>The Glenville family are well known in the theatrical world, and Mr. Mathew H. Glen. ville, whose production, "The Soldier Priest," has been a pleasing success at the Royal Court, is maintaining the best traditions of the family.</p>
        <p>For many tyt,ars now the Glenvilles have adorned the stage, and it may not be generally known that the famous comedian, Shaun Glen- ville, is brother to Mr. Mathew Glenville.</p>
        <p>With a merry twinkle in his eye, Mr. Glen- ville told me he was a true Irishman, born in Dublin—the town of rebellions Sinn Feiners. As a loyal and noble-hearted Irishman, he ex- pressed regret that the fair name of " Ould Ire- land" should be dragged through the mire by such men, "who were nothing but the scum of Ireland."</p>
        <p>"I should not like to class them as'lrishman. There is not a true Irishman who will consider them at all, but unfortunately they have been allowed to get into some power during the present war," he went on to declare.</p>
        <p>Mr. Glenville had had a fear that the present crisis in his native country would have affected the warmth of reception given to his Irish play in Warrington, but those fears had been allayed, for the production throughout the whole week had given pleasure The Glenville family, he told me, had the. Old Abbey Theatre, Dublin, for twelve years, and it was there that Mr. Mathew and Shaun Glenville received their training. Their father played with Barry Sullivan, and had also principal parts with the great Sir Henry Irving.</p>
        <p>Mr. Glenville's mother, although over three score and ten years old, still takes an interest in their business. In fact, she is anxious to go on the stage again!</p>
        <p>Next week Miss Henrietta Schrier and Mr. Lodge-Percy's Co. will present "Mary From Tipperary," twice nightly.</p>
        <p>Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>Charlie Bell, the little man, who is the big comedian in the sketch, "The Demon Photographer," has had a long stage career. He has always flavoured his humour with acro- batics, and when only three years old was per- forming " thuswise " at the Royal Aquarium, London. He was then known as" Prince Mite." Although 45 years old, he is under five feet high even now. He was never much at growing.</p>
        <p>his early introduction to the, stage was natural, his grandfather; father, mother, aunt, and almost everyone before him, having been in the business. Re has known what it is to run a circus with 22 shows a day, standing on the front beating the drum and shouting "Come and see the finest show on record V' be- tween times.</p>
        <p>• He. has been prOducing "The Demon Photographer" for 31 years, previous to which he was with Herbert Darnley and Fred Karno. The last-named gentleman had him for 101 years. Mr. Bell knew Charlie Chaplin well when that bright cinema Star—or constellation—played the Dude in the " Mumming Birds." "My im- pressions of Charlie in those days," he said to me, "was that he was 'nothing extra.' He happened to branch out into this new line and struck the public favour. And there you are !" So is Charlie! •</p>
        <p>Mr. Bell added that it was "a bit of a sur- prise" to all who knew Chaplin that he had got on so well. I don't think there is any pro- fessional jealousy in this remark, for Mr. Bell added sincerely, "Good luck to him," NOT for- getting us.</p>
        <p>The gentlemen playing with Mr. Bell have all been rejected by the Army authorities. Length is 'not always a criterion of military efficiency. One of them stands 6ft. 4in. In his red cashmere socks, another 6ft. 2in. in his Uncle Sam beard.</p>
        <p>The charming lady who adorns the production is Mr. Bell's nineteen-year-old daughter; the other lady—the wife of the old man from the country—is his son! And a good-looking lad he is, too, when he takes off his wig and skirt.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Watson, who are known as "The Waldrons," have been as entertaining as ever in. burlesque dances, songs,. and "cross fire talks." Mr. Watson revels in the Lancashire dialect—you see, he is a Lancashire man, hail- ing from a cotton district, where they forget Education Acts and such influences for the wel- fare of English pure and undefiled. Unlike not a few of our leading comedians, Mr. Watson originates his own songs and patter. He and his wife have just returned from Australia, where they have been for the fifth time. "Yes, a great success," was Mr. Watson's com- mentary upon his tour.</p>
        <p>When sailing in the Mediterranean, their ship was chased by a submarine and, unfortunately, Mrs. Watson has not yet got over the shock.</p>
        <p>The Kin emas. Star K inema. The programmes for the holiday week have been highly appreciated by large audiences.</p>
        <p>"The Explorer," shown en the latter three days, was a marvellous portrayal of South African jungle life, the many beautiful scenes being almost beyond description.</p>
        <p>Next week, on Thursday, Friday and Satur- day, a special "star" attraction will be screened entitled</p>
        <p>This great picture will be shown for three days only, and patrons are advised to book their seats.</p>
        <p>The Picture House. Two fine dramas have been screened at the Picture House this week. "The Mystery of the Green Room," shown during the first half of the week, was brimful with excitement; while the Trans-Atlantic film, "Juror Number Seven." featuring Ben Wilson and Dorothy Phillips, was equally good. A fine photo- drama, "Her Prey," was also shown during the latter half of the week.</p>
        <p>The " star " picture for the first half of next week will be "A Bride of Nancy Lee"; and for the Second half of the week "Pine's Revenge."</p>
        <p>Liverpool Olympia. 'The management of the Olympia, Liverpool, announce that Mr. D. W. Griffith's wonderful film, "The Birth of a Nation," will be screened for the second week. This remarkable film has attracted big audiences, and those who have not already seen it should take the oppor- tunity during the forthcoming week.</p>
        <p>FOYER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
