      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>ORFORD.</head>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>Death of a Ballast Foreman.-11r. Thomas Phillips, Orford Green, passed away on Tues- day morning from an attack of pneumonia. Deceased was 59 years of age, and had been a widower for eleven years. Be leaves three sons and three daughters. The two elder sons are at the Front„ serving in the Forces. Deceased had been a ballast foreman for a number of years, in the employ of the C,L.C.</p>
        <p>Social.—A very enjoyable social evening was held in Orford School on Thursday evening, the object being to provide material for a forthcom- • ing sale of work, which is to be held in aid of. the organ fund. A deputation from the Church visited an organ builder in Manchester for the. purpose of inspecting an organ. The deputa- tion expressed themselves satisfied that the in- strument would meet their requirements, sub- ject to slight alteration. This being done, and: details settled, the Organ should be duly in- stalled in the church wit bin the next month or so. It is hoped that the fund will receive every assistance, to enable the debt to be paid off. The social passed off in a very pleasant manner with games, competitions and dances. Songs were sung by Miss J. Wilson, "Sun- shine of your smile," "Because," and "There's. a land." Mr. J. Gibson contributed "Sea roamer" and "A thousand fathoms deep." Mrs. Cripps obliged with a recitation "Little Nan."' The accompaniments were played by Mrs. R. G. Brandreth and Mr. H. Brownbill. Easter Hymns on Church Tower.—Services. were held in Orford Chinch on Good Friday morning and evening, the one in the evening. taking the form of a musical service. Passion hymns and anthems were sung by the choir, and solos were sung by Mr. J. Gibson, "He was despised" ("Messiah ") and "0 rest in the Lord'? ("Elijah"); Master J. Ellis, "Angels ever bright and fair." Easter Day was com- menced in the usual way. hymns being sung by members of the choir and congregation on the roof of the tower at 7.13 a .111. ; Holy Communion at 8 o'clock, at which there was a very good attendance; matins and Holy Communion at 10.30; children's service at 3 o'clock; and even- song at 6.30. The preacher at all the services was the Vicar, the Rev. G. Vale Owen. The choir gave a very good rendering of the hymn. "Come, see the place where Jesus lay," ar- ranged as an anthem by Mr. 11. Brownbilt, deputy organist. The solo was effectively sung by Master J. Ellis. The collections through- out the day were given to the Vicarage build- ing fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>EASTER VESTRY.</head>
        <p>EASTER VESTRY.</p>
        <p>Vicarage Completed And Paid For; New Organ Coming.</p>
        <p>The annual vestry meeting was held in the church vestry on Tuesday evening, the Vicar,. the Rev. G. Vale Owen presiding. Mr. R. G. Brandreth presented the accounts, which were passed, Mr. L. Cross proposing and Mr. C. Woods seconding. The balance carried forward from Easter, 1915,. amounted to .211 us. 6d.; collections for sick and general fund amounted to £lO 14s. 10d.; for church expenses, £4B is. 4d.; Sunday school, .21 18s. 9d.; special collections, £29 9s. Bd.; bank interest, ss. 9d.; total, £95 is. 10d. The chief item on the other side was the sum of £5l 16s. 3d., church ex, penses. The balance remaining is 10s. 11d. The various funds stand as follows: Organ fund .2113 ss. 9d.; Magazine fund, 1.13 10s. 2d.; Sun— day school fund, credit balance of £6 15s. 6d. Mr. J. Swinton was again chosen by the Vicar- as his warden, and Mr. John Povey was re- elected people's warden. The following were appointed sidesmen for the ensuing year: Messrs. R. G. Brandreth, L. Cross, G: Simcock, E. Brownbill, 11. Armstrong, R. Monks; and F. E. White. Messrs. C. Woods. F. E. White and G. Sim- cock were re-elected auditors, and Messrs. F. E. White and R. G. Brandreth, ruri-decanal representatives.</p>
        <p>The Vicar thanked all the church officers for. their help. During the year the prayer list, containing the names of those serving with the Forces, had increased from 103 to 173. The past year had seen the vicarage completed and paid for, and the parsonage converted into cot- tages. They had also entered upon another venture, that of purchasing a new organ, which would be in the church in the course of a few weeks. They had only half the money as yet, but he was confident that; with God's help, the other half would be forthcoming to enable them* to nay for it.</p>
        <p>The Vicar proposed a vote of condolence with the relatives of those lads who had lost their lives in the war. This was carried in silence, all standing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>Liveroool Diocesan Conference.</head>
        <p>Liveroool Diocesan Conference.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the local electors for the Liverpool Diocesan Conference, in the St. Barnabas' Church Vestry on Thursday evening, Messrs. Dimelow and Siddall were appointed to represent the St. Barnabas' Church.</p>
