        <p>credit to the Corner, and the member might be promoted to Winner for it. Important Notice.—As soon as you have earned a promotion, write and remind Uncle Jack so. You. will then be promoted without any delay. Another Important Notice.—tlpon being pro- moted to President, a member may be awarded a special prize. These ideas, like most of Uncle Ned's ideas, are capital ones, and will be considered when the scheme is finally decided. Perhaps some of my nieces and nephews will have criticisms to offer. BEAUTIFUL AND UNEXPECTED. 170, Battersby-lane, April 26, 1916. Dear Uncle Jack,—l cannot thank you enough for sending me such a beautiful prize. I never expected to win it with my first letter, and I was delighted,when I received it. It is a most interesting book, and it gives me great pleasure to read it. I hope to win more prizes, but even if Ido not I will try my best. I will always be proud of the book.—l remain, your loving Niece, LILY SCARISBEICH. NEW MEMBERS. 783 Frank Roundel!, 32, GoNeplanc; age 10; Beamont council School. 784 Lucy Hankinson, 393, Wellfield-street; age 13; St. Barnabas' School. THE MEANING OF "EASTER EGGS." One of the most widespread and characteristic of many popular observances connected with 1 Easter is the use of "Pasch" (i.e., Easter eggs). , In country districts, where old customs are retained, eggs are stained different colours withl herbs or dye-woods, and friends mutually make presents of them. The townsman is more , familiar with the artificial productions of the ' confectioner and jeweller. In the remoter parts of Scotland it has becom a custom to search for the eggs Of ,wild fow early in the morning of Pasch Sunday, and goo( , luck is thought to attend the finders. On Easter Monday, in the North of England,.: the children still play a game with Pasch eggs ' that is similar to a game of marbles; and tilt, curious fact that in some Sussex villages it it' customary for the villagers—men and boys—tc play marbles on Good Friday would seem td; have some connection with the use of Pasch eggs. The use of eggs at this season was doubtless originally intended to be symbolical of the springing forth of life, and the old practice of making presents of eggs at this time has been very general. The Persians, at their festival of the new 3-..1 (in March) make presents of coloured eggs. 'The, Jews used eggs in the Feast of the Passover. whilst among Christian nations " e Feast of Eggs" has been considered as .mblematic of the Resurrection and a future life. Gardener. "Wanted, at once, clever, experienced man, with good knowledge of toms. cues., mums., &amp;c., to work up small nursery."--Provincial Paper. "One with a knowledge of nursery language preferred."—" Punch."</p>
